record
The catch
Told versus record
The block is of the rule, not the order.
The Associated Press, PBS NewsHour, and CNBC wrote that Talwani extended an order against the Postal Service "enforcing President Donald Trump's executive order." CBS wrote "enforcing President Trump's executive order." The September 4 injunction names sections of the Postal Service rule issued August 21 and published August 26. The Court's August 24 stay had already paused her earlier injunction of the executive order itself. Why it matters: a reader of those dispatches can think the justices are being asked to revive the March order, when the fight before the Court is the Postal Service's published rule.
15
18
10
Claim check
Told versus record
Citizenship lists are a different paper.
The Hill wrote that Sauer asked the justices to "permit the administration to bar the U.S. Postal Service from sending mail-in ballots to residents of states that have not submitted a list of eligible voters based on citizenship data." The Sunday application asks the Court to stay the September 4 injunction of the Postal Service rule. The rule's portal fields are name, address, two barcodes, and originating state. The citizenship list sits in section 2 of the executive order, a Homeland Security task, not in the Postal Service rule's enrollment form. Why it matters: the injunction the Court is being asked to lift reaches envelope standards, portal registration and enrollment, outbound verification, and the nonacceptance clauses, not a citizenship roll, and it is a Postal Service rule, not an order that the administration bar the Postal Service.
