August 24 was a win on procedure, not on the rule. The Court stayed an injunction of the executive order because, it said, the states had to speculate about a rule that had not issued. The Postal Service had already issued that rule and made it effective on August 21, 3 days before the opinion. The rule reached the Federal Register 2 days after the opinion, on August 26. Talwani then enjoined named sections of that rule. Sunday's application argues that the new injunction is "materially identical" to the August 27 temporary order and that the Postal Service is regulating mail, not elections.
