record
Election offices, outbound ballots, and the next few days
Who feels it
If you vote by mail this cycle, the injunction is what keeps the Postal Service from treating noncompliant outbound mailings presented by election offices as unaccepted mail for elections on or before November 3. Return ballots from voters are outside section 705.24.5. If the Court stays that injunction, offices sending non-UOCAVA outbound ballots would have to meet the envelope and portal rules while some of those mailings are already moving. Sauer's own line is that once envelopes "enter the mailstream, there is no retrieving them."
1
12
What the whistleblower alleged.
An anonymous USPS whistleblower, in a disclosure Senator Richard Blumenthal released on September 1, said the portal work was rushed and siloed, that the project parts "have not undergone meaningful testing," and that USPS adopted a "zero-percent failure threshold" under which one failed barcode could return an entire batch: "if a barcode scanner fails to correctly read one barcode in a batch, the entire batch is rejected and returned to the state." Those are allegations, not established facts.
32
What the Postal Service said.
On September 1 the Postal Service said it had "spent months developing a U.S. Federal Ballot Mail Portal," that the work "has at all times been conducted in a manner consistent with court orders," that it was "finalizing the portal" for officials who "voluntarily wish to familiarize themselves," and that "verification procedures prior to acceptance are not being performed."
33
What the court found.
Talwani wrote that the "Portal is not currently operational" and that the "absence of an appeals or escalation process" supported the states' irreparable-harm claim. The compressed approval and enrollment process, she found, supported blocking mandatory use through November 3.
