What Sunday's application actually asks
Claim checked against the record
The coverage's claim: the administration is asking the Court to let it enforce the March executive order for the midterms
Too broad. Application 26A305 asks a stay of the September 4 injunction of named sections of the Postal Service rule issued August 21 and published August 26. The March order's own injunction was stayed on August 24, and that opinion said the stay did not decide whether later implementing measures would be lawful. The injunction the Court is being asked to lift reaches mandatory outbound envelope standards and review; mandatory return-envelope standards and review; mandatory portal registration; mandatory voter enrollment; outbound verification; the first two sentences of the presentation rules; the nonacceptance, further-review, and no-responsibility clauses; and further preparatory steps to implement those sections.
1
10
The claim readers brought is the one in the candidate headlines: a new block, and another appeal. Both are true as dates. The new block is the September 4 injunction. The earlier appeal that "another" points to is 26A297 on Thursday, and behind that 26A124 in July.
4
how the day counts were made
November 3, 2026, minus September 6 is 58 days. Minus September 4 is 60. Minus September 3 is 61. Minus August 26 (publication) is 69. Minus August 21 (effective date, rule line 73) is 74. Minus March 31 is 217. September 19 minus September 6 is 13. November 3 minus September 19 is 45, the statutory 45-day overseas and military ballot date when a valid request arrived in time; the rule excludes UOCAVA ballots. August 26 minus August 24 is 2. August 24 minus August 21 is 3. Plaintiff states were counted from the parties list in 26A305: 23 named states, plus the District of Columbia and the Pennsylvania governor, and 12 named intervenors.
After Sunday
What happened next
On September 8 the 26A305 docket added amicus briefs from bipartisan current and former members of Congress and from the Society for the Rule of Law. The Congress brief argues that the order and final rule usurp Congress's constitutional role as the ultimate authority on federal-election administration. Jackson's response deadline remains 4 p.m. on September 9.
