0045	Three things to know
0046	On Sunday, September 6, Solicitor General D. John Sauer filed application 26A305. An application is a request to a justice to freeze a lower-court order while an appeal continues. This one asks the Supreme Court to stay, meaning pause, a September 4 injunction from U.S. District Judge Indira Talwani in Boston.
0047	2
0048	Talwani's injunction forbids the Postal Service from carrying out named mandatory sections of its Ballot Mail for Federal Elections rule, issued and effective August 21 and published August 26, for elections on or before November 3. It dissolves her August 27 temporary restraining order, a 14-day pause that the new order replaces.
0049	10
0050	12
0051	The same Sunday Sauer withdrew application 26A297, filed Thursday against that temporary order. The word "another" in the coverage is this third trip to the Court. Application 26A124, filed July 27, was granted August 24.
0052	5
0053	8
0054	Story updated 2026-09-08.
0055	This story rests on Executive Order 14399 as published in the Federal Register, the Postal Service's final rule (issued and effective August 21, published August 26), the Privacy Act notices for system USPS 820.225, Judge Talwani's September 4 injunction, the three Supreme Court applications and their dockets, Sauer's withdrawal letter, 2 U.S.C. 7, 52 U.S.C. 20302, Senator Blumenthal's September 1 whistleblower release, the Postal Service's September 1 portal statement, and coverage checked against those records. The days on this page are counted from those dates. The figures come from the official Federal Register PDFs.
0056	The order first, then the rule, then the Court
0057	What happened
0058	On March 31, 2026, President Donald J. Trump signed Executive Order 14399, "Ensuring Citizenship Verification and Integrity in Federal Elections." Section 3 of that order directed the Postmaster General to start a rulemaking on mail-in and absentee ballots, including a proposed line that the Postal Service "shall not transmit" those ballots from anyone not enrolled on a state-specific list.
0059	11
0060	On August 21 the Postal Service issued a final rule, Ballot Mail for Federal Elections, and made it effective that day. The rule was published in the Federal Register on August 26. It amends the Domestic Mail Manual. It requires official Election Mail logos, automation-compatible envelopes, unique Intelligent Mail barcodes, and an online Federal Ballot Mail Portal where election officials enroll the name, address, and barcodes of people they intend to send a ballot.
0061	12
0062	Outbound mailings presented by election offices that do not comply "will not be accepted" and "will be returned" to the "authorized ballot mailer." Section 705.24.5 "does not apply to Return Federal Ballot Mail." Return envelopes still have design rules; the batch nonacceptance screen is the outbound procedure.
0063	12
0064	The rule states an "express exclusion of UOCAVA ballots" from its scope. The Postal Service wrote that those voters "will experience no change" if the exemption is applied as written. UOCAVA covers uniformed and overseas voters under 52 U.S.C. 20302.
0065	12
0066	14
0067	The portal sits on a federal Privacy Act system, "USPS 820.225 Federal Ballot Mail." The July 17 notice places it at "USPS Headquarters, Supplier cloud" computing environment. The first category of records is "Mail in voter information: Name," address, unique Intelligent Mail barcode numbers, and originating election-office state. Records are retrievable by name, address, issuing state, and barcode, and "Records are retained for 5 years." A stated purpose is to help determine adherence to federal law and facilitate law-enforcement efforts. State and local officials load the rows. The system is not a citizenship file. The Postal Service's September 1 response to comments said the system is effective with that publication.
0068	30
0069	31
0070	A group of states had already sued over the executive order. On August 24 the Supreme Court granted application 26A124, staying the district court's injunction of that order. A stay is an order that pauses another court's order. The unsigned opinion said the states' challenge was not ripe because the Court did not yet know what a final rule would say. It added that the disposition "does not mean that any measure taken by the Government to implement the Order will necessarily be lawful."
0071	9
0072	On August 27 Talwani entered a temporary restraining order against mandatory parts of the new rule. On September 3 Sauer asked the Court, in application 26A297, to stay that order. The docket shows the application was submitted to Justice Jackson. She set a response for September 8. The emergency docket is the Court's fast track for those requests, usually without a full argument.
0073	4
0074	On September 4 Talwani issued a preliminary injunction. A preliminary injunction is a court order that forbids someone from doing something while a lawsuit continues. This one names specific Domestic Mail Manual sections and applies to elections on or before November 3. She dissolved the August 27 temporary order the same day. These holdings are preliminary findings for that order, not a final judgment on the merits.
0075	10
0076	Why the judge blocked it
0077	Talwani wrote under "Likelihood of Success on the Merits" that the plaintiffs had established a substantial likelihood of success on their Elections Clause claim. She found the rule is an election rule that regulates the time, place, and manner of elections, and that it is unconstitutional where Congress has not authorized such a rule under the Elections Clause.
0078	10
0079	She separately found the rule likely ultra vires, meaning beyond the agency's statutory authority: Congress has not authorized the Postal Service to make this election rule, and Title 39 does not grant that power.
0080	10
0081	She also found the rule likely creates a new category of nonmailable material, federal election mail, contrary to Title 39's scheme for what Congress has listed as nonmailable.
0082	10
0083	The government contests those findings. In application 26A305, Sauer writes that "The Rule is a lawful and modest exercise" of rulemaking authority Congress delegated to the Postal Service, that "it regulates the U.S. Mail, not federal elections," and that "it does not regulate state administration of elections."
0084	1
0085	On September 6 Sauer wrote the clerk that the United States was withdrawing 26A297, and he filed 26A305 against the new injunction. Jackson's order on that docket set any response for 4 p.m. Eastern on September 9. Sunday is 58 days before November 3, the federal election day named in the injunction and in 2 U.S.C. 7 as the Tuesday after the first Monday in November of an even-numbered year.
