   209	             APPLICATION FOR A STAY OF THE INJUNCTION
   210	           ISSUED BY THE UNITED STATES DISTRICT COURT
   211	                FOR THE DISTRICT OF MASSACHUSETTS
   212	        AND REQUEST FOR AN IMMEDIATE ADMINISTRATIVE STAY
   213	
   214	                                   ───────────
   215	      Pursuant to Rule 23 of the Rules of this Court and the All Writs Act, 28 U.S.C.
   216	
   217	1651, the Solicitor General—on behalf of applicants the United States Postal Service
   218	
   219	(“Postal Service” or “USPS”), et al.—respectfully requests that this Court stay the
   220	
   221	preliminary injunction issued by the United States District Court for the District of
   222	
   223	Massachusetts (App., infra, 1a-49a) pending appeal to the United States Court of
   224	
   225	Appeals for the First Circuit and, if the court of appeals affirms the order, pending
   226	
   227	the timely filing and disposition of a petition for a writ of certiorari and any further
   228	
   229	proceedings in this Court. The Solicitor General also respectfully requests an imme-
   230	
   231	diate administrative stay of the order to prevent ongoing irreparable injury while this
   232	
   233	application is pending.
   234	
   235	
   236	                                          (1)
   237	                                            2
   238	
   239	
   240	       Less than two weeks ago, this Court issued a stay of a prior order entered by
   241	
   242	the same district court. See Trump v. California, No. 26A124, 2026 WL 2473573
   243	
   244	(Aug. 24, 2026) (per curiam). That order had enjoined implementation of a potential
   245	
   246	final rule by the Postal Service before it had even issued. In granting a stay, this
   247	
   248	Court concluded that the order was necessarily unripe because the district court “had
   249	
   250	to engage in a string of speculations to find th[e] suit justiciable” given that it did not
   251	
   252	know, for example, “what [a] final rule would say” or whether it “would harm the
   253	
   254	States.” Id. at *4. Just three days later—the day after the Postal Service formally
   255	
   256	published a final rule—the district court issued a putative temporary restraining or-
   257	
   258	der that partially barred implementation of that rule, without meaningfully address-
   259	
   260	ing the rule’s contents or providing any meaningful analysis to support its conclusion
   261	
   262	that the rule was unlawful. See 26A297 Appl. App. 3a-13a. On Friday, September
   263	
   264	4—the day after applicants filed an application for a stay of that order in this Court,
   265	
   266	see 26A297 Appl.—the district court issued a preliminary injunction. App., infra, 1a-
   267	
   268	49a. The preliminary injunction is materially identical to the temporary restraining
   269	
   270	order, both in its substantive scope and its minimal, conclusory reasoning. Compare
   271	
   272	id. at 24a-31a, 47a-49a, with 26A297 Appl. App. 9a-10a, 12a-13a. The district court’s
   273	
   274	continuing prejudgment of the rule is baseless, and an immediate stay is warranted
   275	
   276	because “the District Court’s errors deal ‘a serious setback’ to the Executive’s ‘goals’
   277	
   278	while this litigation unfolds.” California, 2026 WL 2473573, at *4.
   279	
   280	       Contrary to the claims of the district court and respondents that the Postal
   281	
   282	Service is trying to federalize the rules for mail-in voting, App., infra, 25a-28a, the
   283	
   284	Postal Service’s final rule imposes only modest envelope-design and addressee-infor-
   285	
   286	mation requirements for federal-election ballots sent via U.S. Mail. See Ballot Mail
   287	                                           3
   288	
   289	
   290	for Federal Elections, 91 Fed. Reg. 54,966 (Aug. 26, 2026) (“Final Rule” or “Rule”).
   291	
   292	Specifically, the Rule creates standardized design and review requirements for ballot
   293	
   294	envelopes that build on practices the Postal Service has long recommended, some of
   295	
   296	which are already in use in many States (including many of the respondents here).
   297	
   298	And it requires States to upload the name, address, and barcode information of in-
   299	
   300	tended recipients of ballot mail in an online portal—information that election officials
   301	
   302	would necessarily already possess to send ballot mail under the Rule to those recipi-
   303	
   304	ents. The Rule ensures that States remain responsible for determining voter eligibil-
   305	
   306	ity and eligibility to vote by mail, and it does not dictate ballot content, mailing or
   307	
   308	receipt deadlines, or ballot-counting procedures. The Rule thus plainly does not seize
   309	
   310	control of States’ administration of elections—it simply imposes reasonable prepara-
   311	
   312	tion requirements for certain election-related mail.
   313	
   314	      The Postal Service has longstanding authority to “adopt * * * such rules and
   315	
   316	regulations * * * as may be necessary in the execution of its functions,” 39 U.S.C.
   317	
   318	401(2), which include “the collection, handling, transportation, delivery, forwarding,
   319	
   320	returning, and holding of mail,” 39 U.S.C. 404(a)(1). That authority plainly encom-
   321	
   322	passes the power to promulgate rules like this that govern envelope design and ad-
   323	
   324	dressee information. The Postal Service has previously wielded that authority to is-
   325	
   326	sue analogous preparation requirements for the mailing of cremated remains and
   327	
   328	replica explosives. Indeed, the Postal Service has also used that authority to adopt
   329	
   330	policies that specifically govern election-related mail, such as supporting the use of
   331	
   332	the Election Mail logo and prioritizing ballot mail for processing and delivery.
   333	
   334	      The Rule’s regulation of the U.S. Mail—not federal elections—is plainly con-
   335	
   336	stitutional. While the States have primary authority to regulate the manner of elec-
   337	                                           4
   338	
   339	
   340	tions, they cannot choose to use the federal mails to carry out their elections but then
   341	
   342	insist that their election-related mail is somehow exempt from the Postal Service’s
   343	
   344	rulemaking authority, conferred by Congress, to regulate envelope design and ad-
   345	
   346	dressee information. Neither the statutory nor constitutional provisions contain or
   347	
   348	compel any such carveout from this type of core regulation of use of the federal mails.
   349	
   350	      The district court’s preliminary-injunction opinion fails to seriously address,
   351	
   352	much less refute, any of these points. Instead, while devoting 18 pages to background,
   353	
   354	App., infra, 2a-19a, and another 15 pages to the equities, id. at 32a-46a, the court’s
   355	
   356	analysis of the merits is a scant seven pages, the substance of which can truly be
   357	
   358	reduced to three sentences: The Rule regulates elections rather than the mail be-
   359	
   360	cause it is election-specific; no statute explicitly authorizes USPS to adopt mandatory

