   228	       The Whistleblower cannot come forward publicly for fear of reprisal. They
   229	
   230	speak now through amicus, which compiled the Whistleblower’s Disclosure and filed
   231	
   232	it with U.S. Senator Richard Blumenthal, Ranking Member of the Permanent
   233	
   234	Subcommittee on Investigations, on August 30, 2026. 4 That Disclosure was then
   235	
   236	submitted to the Office of U.S. Representative Robert Garcia, Ranking Member of the
   237	
   238	House Committee on Oversight and Government Reform. The Disclosure was also
   239	
   240	provided to the Office of the Inspector General of the U.S. Postal Service.
   241	
   242	       The Whistleblower must remain anonymous, but they have the expertise and
   243	
   244	proximity to speak authoritatively on the facts adduced in the Disclosure. Because of
   245	
   246	its relationship with the Whistleblower, amicus is uniquely positioned to provide
   247	
   248	critical information on both the design and the functioning of the systems that were
   249	
   250	built in response to the Rule, whose legality is before the Court. Neither amicus nor
   251	
   252	the Whistleblower has any stake here beyond an interest in seeing that the Court
   253	
   254	decides this application on an accurate understanding of the systems it is being asked
   255	
   256	to switch on.
   257	
   258	                                         STATEMENT
   259	
   260	       The Whistleblower has come forward with direct and detailed knowledge of the
   261	
   262	systems the United States Postal Service developed to implement the Order and the
   263	
   264	Postal Service’s related final rule, Ballot Mail for Federal Elections, 91 Fed. Reg.
   265	
   266	54966 (Aug. 26, 2026) (to be codified at 39 C.F.R. pt. 111) (the “Rule”). The
   267	
   268	
   269	
   270	
   271	4 Letter from Sen. Richard Blumenthal to Postmaster General Steiner Appending Disclosure (Aug. 31,
   272	
   273	2026), https://perma.cc/377A-P85V (hereafter, the “Disclosure”).
   274	
   275	
   276	
   277	                                                  2
   278	Whistleblower knows nonpublic details about the systems’ intended functioning—
   279	
   280	and about the chaotic design process that almost guarantees malfunctioning.
   281	
   282	       On August 30, 2026, on the Whistleblower’s behalf, amicus transmitted a
   283	
   284	lawful Disclosure describing those systems to Senator Richard Blumenthal, the
   285	
   286	Ranking Member of the Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations. Senator
   287	
   288	Blumenthal forwarded the Disclosure to the Postmaster General the next day. The
   289	
   290	Disclosure reveals new facts, but it does not stand alone. Instead, it is amply
   291	
   292	corroborated by the public record, including recent reporting on the systems’ design
   293	
   294	and build process. 5
   295	
   296	       Three relevant themes emerge from the Disclosure. First, even if the new
   297	
   298	systems work exactly as designed, they will deny ballots to eligible voters. The
   299	
   300	systems were designed with a pair of chokepoints: one that can block ballots from
   301	
   302	even being brought to USPS facilities, and another that can reject an entire mailing—
   303	
   304	with tens of thousands of ballots or even more—when a barcode on a single sampled
   305	
   306	envelope fails to properly scan. Second, the systems will almost certainly fail, because
   307	
   308	they are the product of a rushed, secretive, and chaotic development process. The
   309	
   310	systems are largely untested and, as of the hearing below, still inoperable. Third, the
   311	
   312	systems were built in defiance of court orders, and the Postal Service appears to have
   313	
   314	misled the courts and Congress about its compliance with those orders.
   315	
   316	I. Overview of the systems designed to implement the Order and the Rule.
   317	
   318	
   319	
   320	
   321	5 See generally Jen Fifield, New Mail Voting Rules Moved Forward Despite USPS Officials’ Concerns
   322	
   323	About Mass Disenfranchisement, ProPublica (Sept. 2, 2026), https://perma.cc/PPV3-NN4W.
   324	
   325	
   326	
   327	                                               3
   328	      The Order’s Section 3 directed the Postmaster General to propose a rule under
   329	
   330	which States would give the Postal Service lists of voters approved to vote by mail;
   331	
   332	the Postal Service, in turn, would furnish States with lists of the voters “enrolled”
   333	
   334	with the Postal Service; and the Postal Service would “not transmit the ballot of any
   335	
   336	voter” not so enrolled. App. 2a–3a.
   337	
   338	      The Rule purports to carry out that directive. Under the Rule, every federal-
   339	
   340	election ballot sent by mail must be enclosed in an envelope bearing a unique
   341	
   342	Intelligent Mail barcode (“IMb”) and the official Election Mail logo; state election
   343	
   344	officials must submit their envelope designs to the Postal Service for pre-approval;
   345	
   346	election officials must upload the name, address, and barcode of every voter receiving
   347	
   348	a mail ballot to a new Postal Service database, the Federal Ballot Mail Portal; and
   349	
   350	the Postal Service must verify, before accepting any outbound ballot mailing, that the
   351	
   352	barcodes on the envelopes match the data in the Portal. 91 Fed. Reg. 54990–91; App.
   353	
   354	7a–8a. If the barcodes on a mailing do not match, the Postal Service will not mail it.
   355	
   356	91 Fed. Reg. 54991; App. 8a.
   357	
   358	      To implement that last requirement, the Postal Service undertook to roll out
   359	
   360	an entirely new set of information technology systems—the kind of massive project
   361	
   362	that ordinarily takes nine months to a year, or even longer, to design, build, test, and
   363	
   364	bring online. Disclosure ¶ 14. The Postal Service needed to build the Portal; it needed
   365	
   366	to establish connections between the Portal and existing bulk-mail systems, so that
   367	
   368	the manifest a local election official uploads for each batch of ballots can be verified
   369	
   370	against the Portal’s data before the official brings the batch to a post office; and it
   371	
   372	
   373	
   374	
   375	                                           4
   376	needed a procedure, and scanning equipment and protocols, for Postal Service clerks
   377	
   378	to perform a second round of verification, checking physical envelopes against the
   379	
   380	data in the Portal. Id. ¶¶ 3, 11–13. 6
   381	
   382	II. The verification procedures are designed to disenfranchise lawful
   383	    voters.
   384	
   385	       Under the Rule, state election officials must upload into the Portal the name,
   386	
   387	address, and unique IMb associated with every voter who will receive a mail ballot.
   388	
   389	91 Fed. Reg. 54991. Per the Postal Service’s plans, as revealed by the Disclosure,
   390	
   391	there is then an initial verification step that appears nowhere in the text of the Rule:
   392	
   393	Before election officials can even bring outgoing bulk mailings of ballots to a postal
   394	
   395	facility for mailing, they must upload a manifest with the names of the intended
   396	
   397	recipients, and match that manifest with the data in the Portal. Disclosure ¶¶ 11–12.
   398	
   399	A mismatch means the ballots do not pass Go. Id.
   400	
   401	       The second verification point occurs when election officials bring a batch of
   402	
   403	outgoing mail ballots to a postal facility. There, Postal Service employees are to scan
   404	
   405	barcodes and confirm that they match the Portal’s data. 91 Fed. Reg. 54981. The Rule
   406	
   407	describes this step as “straightforward.” Id. It says that “[s]canning a barcode
   408	
   409	generally takes less than a minute per mailpiece,” that “the verification process
   410	
   411	should take no more than a few hours for larger mailings,” and that a mailing which
   412	
   413	fails may be corrected and “resubmit[ted].” Id. 54981, 54991. Asked in the rulemaking
   414	
   415	
   416	
   417	
   418	6 The Whistleblower speaks to the verification process and the related systems, but does not and cannot
   419	
   420	speak to envelope design review, to the States’ ability to comply, or to anything outside the Postal
   421	Service.
   422	
   423	
   424	
   425	                                                  5
   426	what share of ballots it expected to reject, the Postal Service “decline[d] to conduct,
   427	
   428	publish, or otherwise disclose” any “anticipated ballot-rejection rate.” Id. 54988.
   429	
   430	        Three planned features of this second verification step do not appear in the
   431	
   432	Rule. The Whistleblower knows them from direct knowledge of the work of building
   433	
   434	the implementation systems. Disclosure ¶ 26. Together, those three features mean
   435	
   436	that systems operating exactly as intended, with no software failure of any kind, will
   437	
   438	reject entire batches of ballots if they find even a single mismatch. And batches can
   439	
   440	be enormous. The Disclosure describes batches of tens of thousands of ballots,
   441	
   442	Disclosure ¶ 3(c), and mailings can be far larger than that: Maricopa County, Arizona,
   443	
   444	“often sends out more than 2 million ballots at a time in its initial mailing.” 7
   445	
   446	        First, the verification standard is zero percent failure: a batch is deemed non-
   447	
   448	compliant if a single envelope fails the barcode verification. Disclosure ¶¶ 3, 26–27.
   449	
   450	Second, the unit of rejection is the batch, not the ballot. When one sampled barcode
   451	
   452	fails to scan, or scans but is not found in the uploaded Portal data, the Postal Service
   453	
   454	will reject the entire mailing. Id. ¶ 27. The State is expected to take the whole batch
   455	
   456	back, locate and cure the single ballot, upload a new batch manifest, and restart the
   457	
   458	process. Id. ¶¶ 28, 35. The Postal Service’s systems and process, as designed and built,
   459	
   460	do not allow curing a batch at the postal facility. And as Senator Blumenthal observed
   461	
   462	in forwarding the Disclosure, “there is no clear process by which state election
   463	
   464	officials or voters themselves can challenge the rejection,” so voters “may not even be
   465	
   466	aware that their ballots have been rejected, or were part of a rejected batch, until it
   467	
   468	
   469	
   470	7 Fifield, supra, note 5.
   471	
   472	
   473	
   474	
   475	                                            6
   476	is too late to secure an alternative ballot or vote in person.” 8 Third, the verification
   477	
   478	requires sampling. Postal Service clerks are to scan a fixed number of pieces from
   479	
   480	each batch, on a schedule that as of the final week before the planned September 1
   481	
   482	launch called for 15 sampled pieces from a batch under 1,000; 350 from a batch of
   483	
   484	1,000 to 10,000; and 400 from a batch above 10,000. Disclosure ¶ 32. Those numbers
   485	
   486	were still provisional in the week before that planned launch. Id. ¶ 33. 9
   487	
   488	        Barcode scanning is not error-free, and the Postal Service knows it. Its
   489	
   490	Inspector General audited package scanning in 2017 and found that one scanner
   491	
   492	issue contributed to errors in five percent of audited scans, with additional failures
   493	
   494	caused by signal obstruction. Disclosure ¶ 31 & n.25. 10 Information available to the
   495	
   496	Whistleblower also appears to reflect concern that some of the scanners to be used to
   497	
   498	validate ballot samples have connectivity and docking limitations, so that not all
   499	
   500	sampling can occur in real time. Disclosure ¶ 31. A Postal Regulatory Commission
   501	
   502	
   503	
   504	
   505	8 Disclosure at 3. The Rule provides only that mailers “will be informed of the escalation procedures
   506	
   507	should they decide to challenge a rejection.” 91 Fed. Reg. 54981. The District Court found that the
   508	Rule “excludes any detail as to how the USPS intends to handle States’ disagreement with the USPS’s
   509	feedback” and that there is an “absence of an appeals or escalation process.” App. 38a. The Postal
   510	Service has also “declined to publish a contingency plan for if the Ballot Portal is not operational or
   511	user-error impedes the process.” App. 41a.
   512	9 The Rule does not mention sampling, though it assumes it. 91 Fed. Reg. 54981. Two people who
   513	
   514	attended internal Postal Service committee meetings on the Rule have separately described the same
   515	design to the press: “USPS officials confirmed it was possible that one ballot with a bad barcode or
   516	wrong voter information within the more than 2 million ballots could delay the entire mailing,” and
   517	“[s]ome in the meetings recommended trying to limit harm to voters by not rejecting the entire batch.”
   518	Fifield, supra, note 5.
   519	10 USPS Office of Inspector Gen., Audit Rep. No. DR-AR-18-001, Package Delivery Scanning—
