   224	                                   INTEREST OF AMICUS CURIAE
   225	
   226	        Amicus The Society for the Rule of Law and Democracy, Inc., d/b/a The Society for the
   227	
   228	Rule of Law (“SRL”), is a national non-profit, non-partisan membership organization of
   229	
   230	conservative, libertarian, right-of-center-lawyers, and others, who are dedicated to defending the
   231	
   232	rule of law, separation of powers, and federalism.1 SRL has an interest in seeing that the USPS
   233	
   234	does not violate these principles by violating the Constitution, federal election statutes, and Title
   235	
   236	39.
   237	
   238	                      INTRODUCTION AND SUMMARY OF ARGUMENT
   239	
   240	        Among the myriad bases for denying a stay, this brief focuses on three independently
   241	
   242	sufficient reasons why the unprecedented USPS ballot rule plainly lacks authority. First, rather
   243	
   244	than delegate authority to USPS, in the federal election statutes of the Help America Vote Act of
   245	
   246	2002 (“HAVA”), the National Voter Registration Act of 1993 (“NVRA”), and the Voting Rights
   247	
   248	Act (“VRA”), Congress specifically delineated where federal law altered and where federal law
   249	
   250	preserved state authority concerning the election practices addressed by the USPS ballot rule—
   251	
   252	voter lists, delivery of “any . . . paper ballots” to voters, and absentee balloting. 52 U.S.C. §§
   253	
   254	21081, 21083, 20506-07, 10502. Except where expressly altered by these statutes, Congress
   255	
   256	specifically preserved authority concerning these election practices and “election technology and
   257	
   258	administration requirements” for the States only, thereby withholding any such authority from
   259	
   260	the USPS. 52 U.S.C. §21084; see id. §§21085, 20507(a), 10502(g). And 52 USC §21145(a)
   261	
   262	specifically superseded resort to all the Title 39 non-election provisions on which Applicants
   263	
   264	rely.
   265	
   266	
   267	
   268	1
   269	 Amicus states that no counsel for any party authored this brief in whole or in part, and that no entity, aside from
   270	amicus and its counsel, made any monetary contribution toward its preparation or submission. The views of SRL do
   271	not necessarily represent the views of all individual members.
   272	
   273	                                                         1
   274	       Second, election mail was specifically withheld from the nonmailability provisions in 39
   275	
   276	U.S.C. §§3001-3018. Those provisions are the only place where—nine times—Congress
   277	
   278	expressly gives the USPS authority to condition or restrict the mailing of a category of items.
   279	
   280	Election mail is not one of these categories. Instead, the only Title 39 election provisions are 39
   281	
   282	U.S.C. §3629, which NVRA enacted and requires USPS to give favorable postage rates to
   283	
   284	registration mail, and 39 U.S.C. §3406, which UOCAVA enacted and requires expeditious and
   285	
   286	free carriage of UOCAVA voting materials. The non-election, boilerplate Title 39 provisions on
   287	
   288	which Applicants rely are not a secret, alternative way for the USPS to bar ballot mail,
   289	
   290	conditionally or otherwise, on the eve of election day.
   291	
   292	       Third, under the federalism presumption and the major question doctrine, the USPS
   293	
   294	needed a clear statement of statutory authority. The boilerplate provisions in Title 39 do not
   295	
   296	come close.
   297	
   298	                                            ARGUMENT
   299	
   300	I.     The Constitution And Federal Election Statutes Preclude The USPS Ballot Rule.
   301	
   302	       The Elections Clause empowers only Congress to “alter” state election practices. The
   303	
   304	USPS ballot rule entitled “Ballot Mail for Federal Elections” is an election-only rule that
   305	
   306	illegally alters state election practices, not a general postal rule like extra postage for a heavy
   307	
   308	package or requiring a proper address. The Applicants stated that they needed the final rule for
   309	
   310	the 2026 election. Trump v. California, 2026 WL 2473573, at *4 (U.S. Aug. 24, 2026)(per
   311	
   312	curiam). The USPS ballot rule is replete with purported election integrity justifications. 91 Fed.
   313	
   314	Reg. 54966-70 (2026). The rule derives from EO 14399, which is entitled: “Ensuring
   315	
   316	Citizenship Verification And Integrity In Federal Elections.” The rule applies to ballot mail
   317	
   318	only. The District Court therefore properly considered statutes in Title 52, the Title for “Voting
   319	
   320	
   321	
   322	                                                   2
   323	and Elections,” in addressing whether Congress allocated authority here to the States rather than
   324	
   325	to USPS.
   326	
   327	        Because the Constitution gives the States full power over federal elections except where
   328	
   329	Congress alters their regulations of elections, “Congress’s power to regulate elections
   330	
   331	‘supersede[s] state law ‘so far as it is exercised, and no farther.’” Watson v. Republican Nat'l
   332	
   333	Comm., 146 S. Ct. 2165, 2181 (2026)(citation omitted). This means that where a federal election
   334	
   335	statute alters a state election practice to a stated extent, the Constitution and Congress are
   336	
   337	preserving state authority concerning that practice except as so altered. HAVA, NVRA, and the
   338	
   339	VRA expressly addressed both where federal election law altered and where federal election law
   340	
   341	preserved state authority concerning any voter list, delivering any paper ballots, absentee
   342	
   343	balloting, and election technology and administration. By specifically preserving state authority
   344	
   345	over these election practices where these federal election statutes had not altered them, these
   346	
   347	federal election statutes specifically and necessarily withheld from USPS any additional federal
   348	
   349	regulatory power to alter such state election practices, by a lot or a little.
   350	
   351	        A. HAVA.
   352	
   353	        HAVA sets the limits of federal aleration of state election practices concerning voter lists,
   354	
   355	the delivery of any paper ballots to voters, and election technology and administration.
   356	
   357	Tellingly, 52 U.S.C. §21085 states: “The specific choices on the methods of complying with the
   358	
   359	requirements of this subchapter shall be left to the discretion of the State.” (Emphasis added).
   360	
   361	        HAVA requires only “a single, . . . official . . . statewide voter registration list” for each
   362	
   363	state with such list “defined, maintained, and administered at the State level.” 52 U.S.C.
   364	
   365	§21083(a)(1)(A)(emphasis added). This “computerized list shall serve as the single system for
   366	
   367	storing and managing the official list of registered voters throughout the State” and “shall serve
   368	
   369	
   370	
   371	                                                   3
   372	as the official voter registration list for the conduct of all elections for Federal office in the
   373	
   374	State.” Id. §21083(a)(1)(A)(i), (viii)(emphases added). Contradicting “the” and “single,” the
   375	
   376	USPS ballot rule illegally establishes a second federally-mandated statewide voter list for each
   377	
   378	State, i.e., a list of those enrolled to vote by mail. A State cannot have any mail-in voting unless
   379	
   380	the state enrolls voters through a portal on a “List” of mail-in voters, and then only for voters
   381	
   382	verified by the USPS as “enrolled” on the new “List.” DMM 705.24.5.
   383	
   384	        Moreover, Applicants ignore that the District Court found that the ballot rule transgressed
   385	
   386	52 U.S.C. §21084. App. 26a. Section 21084 provides that only “a State”—not a federal
   387	
   388	agency—may “establish[] election technology and administration requirements that are more
   389	
   390	strict than the requirements under this subchapter so long as such State requirements are not
   391	
   392	inconsistent with the Federal requirements under this subchapter or [four other federal election
   393	
   394	statutes and two federal disability statutes] described in section 21145 of this title.” (Emphases
   395	
   396	added). The USPS ballot rule exceeded this §21084 limit by establishing stricter federal
   397	
   398	requirements for portals, Lists, and verification by the USPS.
   399	
   400	        There is more. 52 U.S.C. §21081(a) establishes the federal “requirements” for “[e]ach
   401	
   402	voting system used in an election for Federal office.” (Emphasis added.) The term “voting
   403	
   404	system” includes “the practices and associated documentation used . . . to make available any
   405	
   406	materials to the voter (such as notices, instructions, forms, or paper ballots).” 52 U.S.C.
   407	
   408	§21081(b)(2)(E)(emphases added). “[T]he word ‘any’ has an expansive meaning, that is, ‘one or
   409	
   410	some indiscriminately of whatever kind.’” Ali v. Federal Bureau of Prisons, 552 U.S. 214, 219
   411	
   412	(2008)(brackets added; citation omitted). Every method for delivering any “paper balloting” is a
   413	
   414	“voting system” under HAVA. United States v. Town of Thornapple, 143 F.4th 793, 798-99 (7th
   415	
   416	Cir. 2025).
   417	
   418	
   419	
   420	                                                   4
   421	       The documentation and practices used for delivering any paper ballots to mail-in voters,
   422	
   423	including mailing and envelopes, constitute a “voting system.” Here, under the USPS ballot rule,
   424	
   425	the barcoded Outbound Federal Ballot Mail Envelopes, portal information, and Mail-in and
   426	
   427	Absentee Participation List, singly and combined, illegally establish a new, more restrictive
   428	
   429	federally-imposed “voting system” because they are documentation and practices used to make
   430	
   431	notices, instructions, forms, and mail-in paper ballots available to fewer voters. But again 52
   432	
   433	U.S.C. §21084 provides that only “a State”—not a federal agency—may “establish[] election
   434	
   435	technology and administration requirements that are more strict than the requirements under this
   436	
   437	subchapter.” (Emphases added).
   438	
   439	       To remove any doubt, 52 U.S.C. §21145(a) states that “nothing in this chapter may be
   440	
   441	construed to authorize or require conduct prohibited under any of the following laws, or to
   442	
   443	supersede, restrict, or limit the application of such laws.” (Emphases added). The six, specified,
   444	
   445	protected federal statutes that comprise “the following laws” and “such laws” are only
   446	
   447	UOCAVA, VRA, NVRA, the Voting Accessibility for the Elderly and Handicapped Act, and
   448	
   449	two primarily non-election disability statutes. Id. Both “such laws” and “the following laws”
   450	
   451	are narrowing phrases. See Corner Post, Inc. v. Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve
   452	
   453	System, 603 U.S. 799, 817 (2024) (“the” “focuses on a specific” thing); Slack Technologies, LLC
   454	
   455	v. Pirani, 598 U.S. 759, 767 (2023) (“‘such’ … narrows the law’s focus”). The “well
   456	
   457	established canon of statutory interpretation: expressio unius est exclusio alterius . . . has
   458	
   459	particular force here because the” six federal statutes protected by §21145(a) “constitute an
   460	
   461	‘established series,’ such that any ‘omission’ from that series necessarily ‘bespeaks a negative
   462	
   463	implication.’” Esteras v. United States, 606 U.S. 185, 195 (2025)(citation omitted). Congress
   464	
   465	here “exclude[d] another [federal provision] left unmentioned.” Id. (quotations omitted). Thus,
   466	
   467	
   468	
   469	                                                  5
   470	concerning HAVA subjects, the application of a federal statute not listed in “the following”
   471	
   472	series of six specified statutes is superseded, restricted, and limited. Section 21145(a) omits from
   473	
   474	the series all the Title 39 provisions on which the Applicants rely. Enforcing this deliberate
   475	
   476	exclusion fulfills a central HAVA purpose that state and local “administration” of federal
   477	
   478	elections “must be preserved” so that it remains “impossible for a single centrally controlled
   479	
   480	authority to dictate how elections will be run, and thereby be able to control the outcome.” H.R.
   481	
   482	Rep. No. 107-329, pt. 1, at 31-32 (2001)(“HAVA Report”)(emphasis added).
   483	
   484	       The Application at 28 nonetheless argues that the party-presentation principle blinds this
   485	
   486	Court to HAVA, the federal election statute that specifically preserves for States only, and thus
   487	
   488	withholds from USPS, authority on the very subjects in this case: voter lists, delivery of any
   489	
   490	paper ballots to voters, and election technology and administration.     Applicants are incorrect for
   491	
   492	three independently sufficient reasons. First, the plaintiffs relied in the District Court on HAVA
   493	
   494	(and NVRA and the VRA) in support of their preliminary injunction motions. For example, the
   495	
   496	State Plaintiffs-Respondents wrote: “Congress has set requirements related to voter lists, the
   497	
   498	sending of paper ballots to voters, and absentee ballots. See, e.g., 52 U.S.C. §§ 21083(a)(1)(A);
   499	
   500	21081, 10502(c)-(f). But Congress has otherwise left decisions about the administration of
   501	
   502	federal elections, including mail ballot programs, in the hands of the States. See, e.g., 52 U.S.C.
   503	
   504	§§ 21084, 10502(g).” California v. USPS, ECF 103 at 7. See also id., ECF 5 at 2; id., ECF 1 at
   505	
   506	35, 45; League of Women Voters v. Trump (“LVW”), ECF 207 at 9; id., ECF 205, at 22; see also
   507	
   508	Sept. 3, 2026 Hrg. at 41-42. This was more than sufficient for the District Court to rely on the
   509	
   510	federal election statutes that preserved state authority concerning the election practices at issue.
   511	
   512	       Second, under the related-statute canon and clear statement rules, the express provisions
   513	
   514	of the Title 52 election statutes must be considered. That is because they are pertinent to refuting
   515	
   516	
   517	
   518	                                                  6
   519	the Applicants’ argument that non-election Title 39 provisions surreptitiously delegated authority
   520	

