  1022	“tailor their procedures to meet the demands of disparate and unique communities,”
  1023	
  1024	and to ensure that citizens “know whom to hold accountable.” H.R. Rep. No. 107-329,
  1025	
  1026	at 32 (2001); App. 26a. The Disclosure describes the opposite of Congress’ vision: one
  1027	
  1028	national database through which every mail ballot must pass, one rejection standard
  1029	
  1030	applied to every jurisdiction, a cure process that assigns the work back to the States,
  1031	
  1032	and design choices that were never disclosed in the rulemaking. 91 Fed. Reg. 54988;
  1033	
  1034	Disclosure ¶ 26. An arrangement that defies express congressional intent should not
  1035	
  1036	be inferred from statutory silence.
  1037	
  1038	   C. The Disclosure disproves the premise behind the Government’s
  1039	      nonmailability argument.
  1040	
  1041	      The District Court held that the Rule creates a category of nonmailable matter
  1042	
  1043	that Congress did not authorize. App. 30a–31a. The Government answers that the
  1044	
  1045	Rule renders nothing nonmailable, because “[a]ll outbound federal-election ballot
  1046	
  1047	mail that meets the Rule’s requirements will be accepted, processed, and delivered as
  1048	
  1049	before.” Appl. 22.
  1050	
  1051	      The Disclosure shows why that premise is wrong. Under the process the
  1052	
  1053	Disclosure describes, a ballot envelope that meets every requirement is rejected if
  1054	
  1055	
  1056	
  1057	                                          18
  1058	another envelope in the same batch fails a scan. Disclosure ¶¶ 26–27, 32. Contrary to
  1059	
  1060	the Government’s insistence, compliance does not ensure transmission. The
  1061	
  1062	Government’s postage analogy, Appl. 22, shows the difference: the customer who pays
  1063	
  1064	the postage mails the package, but the State that meets every condition of the Rule
  1065	
  1066	may still find its ballots returned. A rule under which conforming mail is rejected
  1067	
  1068	because of other, unrelated mail is not a condition on acceptance of the conforming
  1069	
  1070	mail. It is a categorical bar, which only Congress can impose. App. 30a–31a.
  1071	
  1072	II. The Disclosure shows that the Government faces no irreparable harm
  1073	    from the injunction.
  1074	
  1075	      According to the Government, it cannot implement the Rule for the November
  1076	
  1077	election without a stay: ballots are entering the mail and cannot be recalled, and this
  1078	
  1079	Court has already said that reversal “would come too late for the 2026 midterms.”
  1080	
  1081	Appl. 5, 29 (quoting Trump v. California, No. 26A124, 2026 WL 2473573, at *4 (Aug.
  1082	
  1083	24, 2026)). The Disclosure undercuts that claim about irreparable harm, in two ways.
  1084	
  1085	First, the Postal Service’s own chaotic processes, not the injunction, are what
  1086	
  1087	hindered the systems’ implementation. Second, the Government itself set the
  1088	
  1089	calendar that now purportedly forecloses implementation.
  1090	
  1091	   A. A stay would not cure the Government’s claimed harm, because the
  1092	      systems do not yet work.
  1093	
  1094	      The Government’s irreparable harm argument assumes that, but for the
  1095	
  1096	injunction, the Postal Service would be verifying ballot mailings by now. The record
  1097	
  1098	says otherwise: the systems are not ready, and would not be ready even if the
  1099	
  1100	injunction were lifted today. See supra, at 10–12. What remains enjoined is the
  1101	
  1102	
  1103	
  1104	
  1105	                                          19
  1106	mandatory operation of a system that, on the Government’s own showing, could not
  1107	
  1108	operate on September 3, the day of the hearing below. A stay, then, would not restore
  1109	
  1110	the Rule to operation. It would make mandatory a verification process that has not
  1111	
  1112	been tested and that its own developers expect will fail. Disclosure App. A ¶ s. That
  1113	
  1114	is not relief from harm. It is the harm the District Court found, transferred to voters.
  1115	
  1116	App. 45a.
  1117	
  1118	   B. The Government’s own choices created any time pressure here.
  1119	
  1120	       Harm of a party’s own making does not support a stay. In granting the
  1121	
  1122	Government’s application in Dep’t of Educ. v. California, this Court held that any
  1123	
  1124	irreparable harm the respondents would suffer from declining to keep their programs
  1125	
  1126	running “would be of their own making,” and that “[s]uch self-imposed costs are not
  1127	
  1128	properly the subject of inquiry on a motion for stay.” 604 U.S. 650, 652 (2025) (per
  1129	
  1130	curiam) (quoting Cuomo v. NRC, 772 F.2d 972, 977 (D.C. Cir. 1985) (per curiam)). A
  1131	
  1132	stay “is not a matter of right, even if irreparable injury might otherwise result.” Nken
  1133	
  1134	v. Holder, 556 U.S. 418, 433 (2009). And a party seeking interim relief “must
  1135	
  1136	generally show reasonable diligence,” a requirement “as true in election law cases as
  1137	
  1138	elsewhere.” Benisek v. Lamone, 585 U.S. 155, 159 (2018) (per curiam); see 11A
  1139	
  1140	WRIGHT & MILLER’S FEDERAL PRACTICE & PROCEDURE § 2948.1 (3d ed. Apr. 2026) (“a
  1141	
  1142	party may not satisfy the irreparable harm requirement if the harm complained of is
  1143	
  1144	self-inflicted”).
  1145	
  1146	       The Disclosure shows that the Government’s claimed harm is a product of its
  1147	
  1148	own self-imposed calendar. The Executive Order issued on March 31, only seven
  1149	
  1150	
  1151	
  1152	
  1153	                                          20
  1154	months before the midterm elections. App. 2a. The Postal Service did not propose a
  1155	
  1156	rule until June 2, App. 4a, and the teams responsible for building the system did not
  1157	
  1158	begin meeting until June 15. Disclosure App. A ¶ a. The Rule was published on
  1159	
  1160	August 26, less than seventy days before the election, App. 7a & n.10, and leadership
  1161	
  1162	set the September 1 launch date only on August 6. Disclosure App. A ¶ l. The
  1163	
  1164	Government cannot blame anyone else for its belated decision to embark on an
  1165	
  1166	ambitious project and for its months-long failure to launch. The costs of that poor
  1167	
  1168	project-planning are self-imposed and “not properly the subject of inquiry on a motion
  1169	
  1170	for stay.” Dep’t of Educ., 604 U.S. at 652.
  1171	
  1172	      The Disclosure also refutes the Government’s suggestion that the District
  1173	
  1174	Court’s orders are “partly to blame” for the Postal Service’s unreadiness. Appl. 32.
  1175	
  1176	Work resumed on July 29 while the June 25 injunction was in force, continued
  1177	
  1178	through the injunction of August 11, and continued after the August 27 temporary
  1179	
  1180	restraining order. Disclosure ¶¶ 5, 37–39. The rulemaking itself was completed while
  1181	
  1182	the Postal Service was enjoined. Dkt. 201 at 3. Whatever else may be said of the
  1183	
  1184	Government’s conduct, see infra Part III, it forecloses the claim that court orders are
  1185	
  1186	responsible for the project’s delays.
  1187	
  1188	III. The Disclosure shows that the public interest and the balance of
  1189	    equities weigh against a stay.
  1190	
  1191	      The Government contends that the Court balances the equities only in close
  1192	
  1193	cases, Appl. 6, 31, and that this is not one. Parts I and II of this Argument, supra,
  1194	
  1195	refute the second point. As to the first, election cases are the setting in which this
  1196	
  1197	Court has weighed the equities most heavily. Purcell rests on consequences alone:
  1198	
  1199	
  1200	
  1201	                                              21
  1202	late changes to the rules of an election “can themselves result in voter confusion and
  1203	
  1204	consequent incentive to remain away from the polls.” Purcell v. Gonzalez, 549 U.S. 1,
  1205	
  1206	4–5 (2006) (per curiam). Purcell need not be a binding rule to matter here: it reflects
  1207	
  1208	the Court’s judgment that, in the weeks before an election, the equities can make all
  1209	
  1210	the difference. And here, as the Disclosure highlights, the equities and the public
  1211	
  1212	interest militate in favor of keeping the injunction in place.
  1213	
  1214	   A. The Disclosure substantiates the District Court’s harm findings.
  1215	
  1216	      The Disclosure shows that allowing the Postal Service to resume
  1217	
  1218	implementation of the Rule would disrupt the November 2026 election and threaten
  1219	
  1220	the “disenfranchisement of millions of United States citizens who seek to vote by
  1221	
  1222	mail.” App. 45a. The District Court found that compliance with the Rule for this
  1223	
  1224	election would be “virtually impossible,” and that disenfranchisement of eligible
  1225	
  1226	voters was “practically inevitable,” because “any minor malfunction will result in the
  1227	
  1228	denial of mail ballots.” App. 44a–45a. The Government claims that the District
  1229	
  1230	Court’s findings on harm rest on “speculative fears, mischaracterizations of the
  1231	
  1232	record, and delays occasioned by its own improper orders,” and on the views of state
  1233	
  1234	officials “who lack direct knowledge of the Postal Service’s implementation plans.”
  1235	
  1236	Appl. 6, 32. But the Disclosure reflects direct knowledge, and it confirms the findings
  1237	
  1238	the Government calls speculation.
  1239	
  1240	      The Disclosure shows that the predicted malfunction is almost inevitable—
  1241	
  1242	baked into the design and rollout of the new systems. As the Whistleblower explains,
  1243	
  1244	the problems with the Postal Service’s new system began with its “slapdash software
  1245	
  1246	
  1247	
  1248	
  1249	                                          22
  1250	development process as the USPS attempted to create [a] complex IT system, with
  1251	
  1252	multiple points of ballot review, in a matter of weeks.” Disclosure ¶ 14; see id. ¶¶ 16–
  1253	
  1254	18.
  1255	
  1256	      The risk that this “slapdash” process will disable mail-in voting is compounded
  1257	
  1258	by the likelihood of failure in the two new ballot verification processes. Id. ¶ 22. As
  1259	
  1260	shown above, the manifest verification process is a prerequisite to ballots even
  1261	
  1262	reaching the post office. Id. ¶ 23. The second process is characterized by batch-
  1263	
  1264	rejection, a zero-percent failure tolerance, and sampling rather than individual
  1265	
  1266	checking. See supra, at 6–7. After a single failed barcode verification, the Postal
  1267	
  1268	Service will stop the sample scanning and reject the entire batch of ballots. Disclosure
  1269	
  1270	¶¶ 26–27. This is notwithstanding the fact that zero-percent failure is far stricter
  1271	
  1272	than documented error rates for USPS scanning, see Disclosure ¶ 31 & n.25; supra,
  1273	
  1274	at 8, and appears without precedent for IMb scanning. Supra, at 8. Because of the
  1275	
  1276	“apparently sloppy and rushed manner in which the Federal Ballot Mail Portal and
  1277	
  1278	supporting IT systems are being built,” “[p]otentially millions of American voters may
  1279	
  1280	not receive their mail-in ballot this election cycle in a timely manner, or at all.”

