    55	systems (the “Portal”), which will be used to screen ballots submitted by state election officials
    56	prior to USPS agreeing to mail them to voters. The Disclosure identifies problems at every stage
    57	of USPS’s development of the Portal, demonstrating deeply flawed plans for implementation.
    58	
    59	        According to the whistleblower, USPS’s effort to develop and deploy the Portal has been
    60	“rushed,” “risky and haphazard” because leadership has demanded an impossible timeframe. 4 In
    61	an effort to meet impossible deadlines, USPS has eliminated standard and needed testing, thereby
    62	creating substantial risk of a “catastrophic failure” of the system that could “derail the midterm
    63	elections.” 5
    64	
    65	         USPS began work building the Portal on or around June 15, 2026 just three months
    66	before the date USPS planned to launch the system and just five months before the November
    67	2026 midterm elections. 6 On or about June 25, 2026, USPS ordered work on the Portal to cease
    68	due to a court order enjoining implementation of the EO. 7 That work stoppage persisted for
    69	approximately a month, further reducing the time that USPS had to build the new system. 8
    70	According to the whistleblower, building the information technology infrastructure necessary to
    71	complete the Portal could take a year or more. 9 Yet, USPS leadership demanded that the Portal
    72	be completed for a launch date of September 1, 2026, less than six months after the EO was
    73	issued. 10 As a result of this rushed process, USPS has been unable to conduct tests of the Portal
    74	to ensure its proper functioning, troubleshoot problems, or distribute instructions on use to state
    75	election officials. According to the whistleblower, the Portal “violates standard principles of
    76	testing and debugging new software before launch.” 11 Normal procedures at USPS for such
    77	systems include internal testing, customer acceptance testing, and a final development stage
    78	before release to public facing users. 12 The Portal has gone through none of these basic checks.
    79	
    80	       Not only is this system astonishingly untested, USPS has simultaneously implemented an
    81	impracticable zero percent failure rate. 13 When ballots are submitted to USPS in large-volume
    82	batches, if any one ballot in the batch cannot be verified against the Portal, all ballots in that
    83	batch will be rejected. For example, if a state election official brings a batch of 10,000 ballots to
    84	USPS and USPS is unable to match just one of those ballots against the Portal – because, for
    85	example, someone has recently changed their name after marriage or they’ve moved – then
    86	USPS would refuse to mail the remaining 9,999 ballots as well. As the whistleblower notes,
