   113	“USPS expects the state to take back the entire batch to cure the issue with the single ballot…” 14
   114	Should the slapdash Portal mistakenly mark a ballot as unverified, there is no clear process by
   115	which state election officials or voters themselves can challenge the rejection. The Rule simply
   116	vaguely states that they “will be informed of the escalation procedures should they decide to
   117	challenge a rejection.” 15 Voters intending to cast ballots by mail may not even be aware that their
   118	ballots have been rejected, or were part of a rejected batch, until it is too late to secure an
   119	alternative ballot or vote in person. Expecting a well-built, thoughtful Portal to return an accurate
   120	result 100 percent of the time is already a stretch—expecting a “rushed,” “risky and haphazard”
   121	Portal to do the same is a recipe for disaster.
   122	
   123	        These risks are compounded by other revelations in the Disclosure. USPS leadership has
   124	shown an incredible lack of effective management while building the Portal including siloed
   125	work streams, changing requirements, competing understandings of work output, and multiple
   126	actors having ownership of the same unique tasks. For example, there was little or no
   127	communication among teams working on the project to such an extent that “when elements were
   128	brought together, the teams were unaware of various developments, creating more work.” 16
   129	Shockingly, there are also “no clear written requirements” for the information technology and
   130	infrastructure of the Portal, leaving workers to “glean requirements from opaque comments at
   131	meetings.” 17 As late as August 21, 2026, workers were still receiving new requirements for the
   132	project. 18 Without clear guidance, “[d]ifferent team members continued to have different
   133	understandings of how the system is supposed to function which caused ongoing and greater
   134	confusion among the group,” substantially increasing the risk that the system will fail and,
   135	ultimately, disenfranchise voters. 19
   136	         Each of these problems: rushed development; a zero percent failure rate; and poor
   137	management are cause for concern on their own. Taken together they present a clear picture of a
   138	fatally flawed process that cannot and will not protect American voters. Put plainly: this process
   139	will likely lead to chaos, confusion, and the inability of many Americans to have their vote
   140	counted.
