   355	3.​ There are three core changes to ballot mail processing that this disclosure will
   356	    speak about. In the past, election officials would design compliant ballot mailers
   357	    and bring ballot mail to the USPS. That past process simply involved uploading the
   358	    mail ballot details like barcode number and mailing address information into legacy
   359	    bulk mail digital systems, where there would be some verification focused on
   360	    confirming cost and mailing type details.1 Under this new system, Election officials
   361	    will be responsible for reconciling issues with three new components USPS is
   362	    adding to the election ballot mail system
   363	
   364	                  a.​ The new Portal for entering voter names and ballot barcodes, a system
   365	                      where elections officials will need to sort out errors with uploading new
   366	                      information into a new system.
   367	
   368	                  b.​ A new manifest verification process, where mail ballot batch manifests
   369	                      are compared to the information in the Portal. Here election officials will
   370	                      need to resolve errors between their batch manifest information and the
   371	                      Portal information.
   372	
   373	                  c.​ A new physical barcode sampling verification standard, with a zero
   374	                      percent failure rate, where Postal Service clerks will scan a sample of the
   375	                      ballot mail batch to confirm the physical ballot mail information matches
   376	                      the information in the Portal. Election officials will be responsible for
   377	                      resolving any errors purportedly identified in the scan, and for
   378	                      resubmitting the batch manifest - USPS will refuse to accept the ballot
   379	                      batch until the errors are resolved. (Batches can be tens of thousands of
   380	                      ballots or more.)
