  3225	Ballot mailing. Business Mail Entry Unit
  3226	(‘‘BMEU’’) technicians and Retail clerks
  3227	will not perform a separate review of
  3228	this information.
  3229	After completing the certification,
  3230	Federal Ballot Mail Portal Users must
  3231	provide: the name and address of the
  3232	voter as found on Outbound Federal
  3233	Ballot Mail envelope; the issuing state;
  3234	and the IMbs on the Outbound and
  3235	Return Federal Ballot Mail envelopes.
  3236	This information must be uploaded to
  3237	the Portal prior to presenting Outbound
  3238	Federal Ballot Mail for acceptance.
  3239	These data help to ensure that the rule’s
  3240	preparation standards have been
  3241	satisfied, can provide valuable insight to
  3242	election officials and law enforcement,
  3243	and facilitate the verification process.
  3244	This type of information is regularly
  3245	collected by the Postal Service,
  3246	including on mailing manifests. No
  3247	other voter information, such as
  3248	birthdate, social security number, or
  3249	other voter registration details, will be
  3250	uploaded to the Portal. As described
  3251	further below, the Postal Service is
  3252	developing this Portal with appropriate
  3253	cybersecurity and privacy safeguards to
  3254	protect Portal data, consistent with
  3255	applicable law.
  3256	The required information can be
  3257	uploaded into the Portal using an Excel
  3258	template specifically formatted for
  3259	Portal submission. Uploading and
  3260	generating the necessary information is
  3261	similar to submitting a manifest for bulk
  3262	mailings, which larger election offices
  3263	and their MSPs routinely complete. For
  3264	smaller jurisdictions, the (free and
  3265	optional) IMsb Tool can be used to
  3266	generate a file that can be uploaded
  3267	directly into the Portal. This will help
  3268	minimize any data entry burden. The
  3269	Postal Service does not anticipate that
  3270	providing Portal data should require any
  3271	change to statewide systems or
  3272	databases. States must necessarily
  3273	already maintain lists or databases with
  3274	the relevant name and address
  3275	information, otherwise it would never
  3276	have been possible to mail those ballots
  3277	in the first place. These systems are
  3278	intended to remain separate, and do not
  3279	need to be interconnected with the
  3280	Portal. One commenter mentioned that
  3281	states may wish to update their
  3282	databases to include IMb data. States are
  3283	free to undertake such actions, but it is
  3284	not required by the rule.
  3285	Under the proposed rule, there is a
  3286	flexible timeline for uploading data into
  3287	the Portal. Initial mailing lists should be
  3288	entered into the Portal at least 30 days
  3289	before the election, if possible, or by the
  3290	date on which ballots must begin being
  3291	mailed under state law. This 30-day
  3292	timeline, retained without modification
  3293	in the final rule, is not a rigid deadline
  3294	
