  3301	for completing Portal data entry.
  3302	Although Executive Order 14399
  3303	contemplated a proposal in which states
  3304	would submit such lists ‘‘no fewer than
  3305	60 days before the election,’’ the flexible
  3306	30-day timeline accommodates
  3307	variations in state law.
  3308	The Postal Service does not expect
  3309	these initial lists to be complete or final.
  3310	States may continue to add, amend, or
  3311	remove data from the Portal to account
  3312	for new voters, new mail-in or absentee
  3313	ballot requests, replacement ballots,
  3314	addressing administrative errors, and
  3315	other changes consistent with their
  3316	state’s law. These changes can be made
  3317	as a new separate entry or by updating
  3318	prior entries. This flexibility is intended
  3319	to facilitate—not constrain—voters’
  3320	ability to access mail-in or absentee
  3321	voting as permitted by their own state’s
  3322	voting law, none of which are altered by
  3323	this rule.
  3324	Federal Ballot Mail Portal Users can
  3325	update the information submitted
  3326	through the Portal as frequently as
  3327	needed, consistent with state law. This
  3328	process is designed to be flexible to
  3329	account for variation in state law. For
  3330	example, as one commenter explained,
  3331	California Elections Code Section
  3332	3000.5(a) dictates when county election
  3333	officials are to mail ballots to registered
  3334	voters. Section 3005.5(a) provides, in
  3335	part: ‘‘for each election, the elections
  3336	official shall, no later than 29 days
  3337	before the day of the election, begin
  3338	mailing the materials specified in
  3339	Section 3010 to every registered voter.
  3340	The elections official shall have five
  3341	days to mail a ballot to each person who
  3342	is registered to vote on the 29th day
  3343	before the day of the election and five
  3344	days to mail a ballot to each person who
  3345	is subsequently registered to vote.’’
  3346	Under California law, a voter may also
  3347	register as late as 15 days before an
  3348	election and will subsequently be sent
  3349	a ballot via mail. Even after the 15th day
  3350	before an election, voters may request a
  3351	replacement mail ballot upon request to
  3352	their county election official. This state
  3353	statutory scheme is consistent with the
  3354	rule. California election officials could
  3355	submit initial mailing lists into the
  3356	Portal 30 days (or sooner) before the
  3357	election and present Outbound Federal
  3358	Mailings for acceptance ‘‘no later than
  3359	29 days before the day of the election,’’
  3360	consistent with state law. California
  3361	election officials may continue to add
  3362	Portal entries for Outbound Federal
  3363	Ballot Mail destined for voters who
  3364	register after that time and until the 15th
  3365	day before the election, and even after
  3366	the 15th day before the election may
  3367	continue to make supplemental entries
  3368	for any replacement requests.
