  3612	and Retail clerks will receive training on
  3613	this process.
  3614	The verification process is
  3615	straightforward. The Outbound Federal
  3616	Ballot mailings will be verified using
  3617	the IMb on the outside of the Outbound
  3618	Federal Ballot Mail envelope. Relying
  3619	on existing scanning technology and
  3620	devices, the Postal Service will confirm
  3621	the presence of scanned barcodes in the
  3622	Federal Ballot Mail Portal. Portal data
  3623	will be available for verification
  3624	purposes on a nationwide basis.
  3625	Therefore, if a state uses an MSP located
  3626	in a state different than the issuing state
  3627	of the ballot, the Postal Service will still
  3628	be able to conduct verification.
  3629	This process can be completed
  3630	promptly. Scanning a barcode generally
  3631	takes less than a minute per mailpiece.
  3632	Depending on how many barcodes are
  3633	scanned, the verification process should
  3634	take no more than a few hours for larger
  3635	mailings, and potentially only a few
  3636	minutes for smaller mailings.
  3637	Authorized Ballot Mailers should
  3638	remain at the BMEU or postal retail
  3639	location during the verification process.
  3640	If the Outbound Federal Ballot
  3641	mailing meets the verification
  3642	standards, it will be accepted by the
  3643	Postal Service. No further action is
  3644	needed by election officials or voters
  3645	under the rule.
  3646	Authorized Ballot Mailers will be
  3647	informed of the escalation procedures
  3648	should they decide to challenge a
  3649	rejection; relevant Postal Service
  3650	employees will also receive training
  3651	about these procedures. The ability to
  3652	cure rests not with the voter, as the
  3653	franchise itself is not impacted, but with
  3654	the officials responsible for preparing
  3655	the envelope and entering Portal data.
  3656	This approach is consistent with how
  3657	election mail works now, where election
  3658	officials and mailers are responsible for
  3659	preparing the ballot mail, addressing the
  3660	mailpieces properly, ensuring that they
  3661	are going to the proper individual, and
  3662	correcting any error that may occur or
  3663	prevent delivery to the intended
  3664	recipient. Thus, the Postal Service will
  3665	continue to resolve issues consistent
  3666	with our current approach, rather than
  3667	creating a new ‘‘appeal’’ process for
  3668	voters, as many commenters urged, that
  3669	would impose a direct burden on voters
  3670	and increase the risk of confusion and
  3671	concern.
  3672	Once Outbound Federal Ballot Mail is
  3673	accepted by the Postal Service, it will
  3674	enter and move through the mailstream
  3675	as it has in prior elections. It will not
  3676	be intercepted, returned, or delayed due
  3677	to the rule.
