  1292	        B. Likelihood of Success on the Merits
  1293	
  1294	        1. The Final Rule is Unconstitutional 30
  1295	
  1296	        First, Plaintiffs challenge the constitutionality of the Final Rule, asserting that the USPS’s
  1297	
  1298	regulation impermissibly encroaches upon the States’ and Congress’s Elections Clause powers.
  1299	
  1300	See Pl. Orgs.’ Supp. Compl. ¶¶ 69–81 [Doc. No. 205]; Pl. States’ Compl. ¶¶ 138–50, California
  1301	
  1302	II, No. 1:26-cv-13917 [Doc. No. 1]. The court finds that Plaintiffs have established a substantial
  1303	
  1304	likelihood of success on this claim.
  1305	
  1306	        As discussed above, the Constitution’s Elections Clause tasks the States with the role of
  1307	
  1308	election administration, subject only to Congress’s superseding legislation, and the executive has
  1309	
  1310	no inherent authority in this joint endeavor. U.S. CONST. art. I, § 4, cl. 1.
  1311	
  1312	        Pursuant to its Elections Clause power, Congress has passed significant legislation
  1313	
  1314	“alter[ing]” the States’ election practices. Id.; see also United States v. Gradwell, 243 U.S. 476,
  1315	
  1316	485 (1917) (“whenever [Congress] has assumed to regulate [] elections it has done so by positive
  1317	
  1318	and clear statutes”). In enacting voting legislation like HAVA, NVRA, and the VRA, Congress
  1319	
  1320	has expressly delegated its Elections Clause power only in limited instances. 31 Otherwise, while
  1321	
  1322	Congress has set certain time, place, and manner restrictions pursuant to the Elections Clause, it
  1323	
  1324	has left further regulation to States, not federal agencies.
  1325	
  1326	
  1327	30
  1328	  Defendants and Intervenor-Defendants do not dispute that this court has jurisdiction to review
  1329	Plaintiffs’ constitutional claims.
  1330	31
  1331	   For example, through HAVA, Congress “established” the Election Assistance Commission, see
  1332	52 U.S.C. § 20921, and tasked the agency with specific enumerated duties, see id. § 20922, such
  1333	as “develop[ing]” the standard federal mail voter registration form, “in consultation with the
  1334	chief election officers of the States,” see id. § 20508(a)(2). Congress also delegated to the
  1335	Election Assistance Commission narrowly confined rulemaking authority necessary to carry out
  1336	the agency’s duties to maintain the federal registration form and make reports to Congress. See
  1337	id. §§ 20929; 20508(a); see also H.R. Rep. No. 107-730, at 69 (2002) (explaining that HAVA
  1338	“[p]rohibits” the EAC “from imposing any rule, regulation, or taking any action that imposes
  1339	requirements on State or local governments except as permitted under the [NVRA]”).
