  1346	
  1347	        In HAVA, for example, Congress has set minimum election standards for “voting
  1348	
  1349	systems” that States must follow, see 52 U.S.C. § 21081, and has allowed States, not federal
  1350	
  1351	agencies, the ability to set more restrictive standards or rules around election administration and
  1352	
  1353	technology, see id. § 21084. Congress also required that a State’s voter registration system be
  1354	
  1355	“the single system for storing and managing the official list of registered voters throughout the
  1356	
  1357	State” and “the official voter registration list for the conduct of all elections for Federal office in
  1358	
  1359	the State.” 52 U.S.C. §§ 21083(a)(1)(A)(i), (viii). Without authorization under the Elections
  1360	
  1361	Clause, the USPS’s requirement that the States furnish a Mail-In and Absentee Participation List
  1362	
  1363	conflicts with Congress’s requirement in HAVA that the States alone be in possession of a “single
  1364	
  1365	system[.]” Id.
  1366	
  1367	        This careful reservation is consistent with the purpose of HAVA, which sought to
  1368	
  1369	preserve the benefits of elections administered at the state and local level:
  1370	
  1371	        The dispersal of responsibility for election administration has made it impossible
  1372	        for a single centrally controlled authority to dictate how elections will be run, and
  1373	        thereby be able to control the outcome. This leaves the power and responsibility for
  1374	        running elections where it should be, in the hands of the citizens of this country.
  1375	        Local control has the further added benefit of allowing for flexibility, so that local
  1376	        authorities can tailor their procedures to meet the demands of disparate and unique
  1377	        communities. Further by leaving the responsibility for election administration in
  1378	        the hands of local authorities, if a problem arises, the citizens who live within their
  1379	        jurisdictions know whom to hold accountable. The local authorities who bear the
  1380	        responsibility cannot now, and should not in the future be able to, point the finger
  1381	        of blame at some distant, unaccountable, centralized bureaucracy.
  1382	
  1383	H.R. Rep. 107-329, at 32 (2001).
  1384	
  1385	        Similarly, in the VRA, to maximize enfranchisement, Congress set certain minimum
  1386	
  1387	voter eligibility rules around Presidential elections, including requiring States to make absentee
  1388	
  1389	voting available for those elections. 52 U.S.C. § 10502(b)–(f). But Congress made clear that
  1390	
  1391	States were free to adopt for all elections “less restrictive voting practices” than the baseline
  1392	
  1393	requirements set by Congress. Id. § 10502(g).
  1394	                                                      26
