  1396	
  1397	
  1398	
  1399	
  1400	       As these statutes illustrate, Congress has always respected the Elections Clause’s
  1401	
  1402	reservation of power to the States, and has explicitly permitted the States, but not federal
  1403	
  1404	agencies, to legislate except where state laws would contradict or undermine congressionally set
  1405	
  1406	standards. Congress has never explicitly delegated its Elections Clause power to the USPS, and
  1407	
  1408	Defendants and Intervenor-Defendants do not claim otherwise. While all delegation issues
  1409	
  1410	ultimately condense to a separation-of-powers problem, see Loving v. United States, 517 U.S.
  1411	
  1412	748, 758–59 (1996), because of the Elections Clause’s structure, the USPS’s arrogation here
  1413	
  1414	implicates not only Congress, but also the States. See Arizona, 570 U.S. at 9 (Congress’s
  1415	
  1416	Elections Clause power “is paramount” but “no farther” than “it is exercised[.]”) (quoting Ex
  1417	
  1418	parte Siebold, 100 U.S. 371, 392 (1880)).
  1419	
  1420	       Defendants and Intervenor-Defendants seek to avoid the Elections Clause’s structure by
  1421	
  1422	denying that the Final Rule is an election rule. See Defs.’ Opp’n 14 [Doc. No. 235]; see
  1423	
  1424	Intervenors’ Opp’n 12 [Doc. No. 234]. This denial rings hollow. Titled “Ballot Mail for Federal
  1425	
  1426	Elections,” the Final Rule explicitly and exclusively regulates election mail. 91 Fed. Reg. at
  1427	
  1428	54990–92 (DMM. 705.24). The Final Rule does not purport to regulate any other type of mail
  1429	
  1430	and requires action only of the States’ “chief election official[s.]” Id. Further, the USPS’s cited
  1431	
  1432	policy justification is “the integrity of federal elections.” 91 Fed. Reg. at 54966, see id. at 54976
  1433	
  1434	(citing Exec. Order 14399, entitled “Ensuring Citizenship Verification and Integrity in Federal
  1435	
  1436	Elections,” as generating the policy goals justifying the Final Rule).
  1437	
  1438	       Throughout its body of election legislation, Congress has never delegated its Elections
  1439	
  1440	Clause power to the USPS. Unauthorized by the Elections Clause, the Final Rule clashes with
  1441	
  1442	Congress’s statutory scheme, and is unconstitutional where it intrudes not only on Congress’s
  1443	
  1444	Elections Clause powers but also that power left to the States. Watson, 146 S. Ct. at 2181
  1445	
  1446	
  1447	
