  1486	32
  1487	   Defendants argue that ultra vires review is unavailable where Plaintiffs have failed to plead a
  1488	specific violation of the USPS’s statutory mandates. Defs.’ Opp’n 8–9 [Doc. No. 235] (citing
  1489	Nuclear Regul. Comm’n v. Texas, 605 U.S. 665, 681–2 (2025)). However, the heightened
  1490	standard set out by the Supreme Court in Nuclear Regul. Comm’n only applies where Congress
  1491	has provided a reticulated judicial review scheme that allows injured parties “a meaningful and
  1492	adequate opportunity for judicial review.” Bd. of Governors of the Fed. Rsrv. Sys. v. MCorp Fin.,
  1493	Inc., 502 U.S. 32, 43 (1991). Where Congress has generally excepted the USPS from APA
  1494	review, the court, sitting in equity, may consider whether the USPS’s final actions are “outside
  1495	the bounds of its statutory authority” because Plaintiffs otherwise have no access to judicial
  1496	relief. City of Providence v. Barr, 954 F.3d 23, 31 (1st Cir. 2020). Plaintiffs’ ultra vires claim sits
  1497	apart from their APA claim, described further below.
