  2469	
  2470	
  2471	
  2472	
  2473	          The Supreme Court has explained that movants must (1) make a strong showing that they
  2474	
  2475	are likely to succeed on the merits on appeal; (2) show that they will be irreparably injured
  2476	
  2477	absent a stay; (3) show that a stay will not substantially injure the other parties interested in the
  2478	
  2479	proceeding; and (4) show that the stay would be in the public interest. Nken v. Holder, 556 U.S.
  2480	
  2481	418, 434 (2009). Likelihood of success and irreparable injury are “the most critical” factors. Id.
  2482	
  2483	“It is not enough that the [movant’s] chance of success on the merits be better than negligible.”
  2484	
  2485	Id. (citation modified). Similarly, “simply showing some possibility of irreparable injury fails to
  2486	
  2487	satisfy the second factor.” Id. at 434–35 ( citation modified). Courts assess the harm to other
  2488	
  2489	interested parties and weigh the public interest “[o]nce an applicant satisfies the first two
  2490	
  2491	factors[.]” Id. at 435.
  2492	
  2493	          For the reasons discussed above, the court finds that Defendants have not made a strong
  2494	
  2495	showing that they are likely to succeed on the merits, have shown no irreparable injury absent a
  2496	
  2497	stay, have not shown that a stay will not substantially injure the other parties interested in the
  2498	
  2499	proceeding, and have not shown that a stay would be in the public interest. Accordingly, the
  2500	
  2501	request for a stay pending appeal is DENIED.
