65:         USPS began work building the Portal on or around June 15, 2026 just three months
66-before the date USPS planned to launch the system and just five months before the November
67:2026 midterm elections. 6 On or about June 25, 2026, USPS ordered work on the Portal to cease
68:due to a court order enjoining implementation of the EO. 7 That work stoppage persisted for
69-approximately a month, further reducing the time that USPS had to build the new system. 8
70-According to the whistleblower, building the information technology infrastructure necessary to
71:complete the Portal could take a year or more. 9 Yet, USPS leadership demanded that the Portal
72-be completed for a launch date of September 1, 2026, less than six months after the EO was
73:issued. 10 As a result of this rushed process, USPS has been unable to conduct tests of the Portal
74-to ensure its proper functioning, troubleshoot problems, or distribute instructions on use to state
75:election officials. According to the whistleblower, the Portal “violates standard principles of
76:testing and debugging new software before launch.” 11 Normal procedures at USPS for such
77:systems include internal testing, customer acceptance testing, and a final development stage
78:before release to public facing users. 12 The Portal has gone through none of these basic checks.
