     1	                 Cite as: 609 U. S. ____ (2026)            1
     2	
     3	                          Per Curiam
     4	
     5	SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES
     6	                         _________________
     7	
     8	                          No. 26A124
     9	                         _________________
    10	
    11	
    12	  DONALD J. TRUMP, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED
    13	      STATES, ET AL. v. CALIFORNIA, ET AL.
    14	                 ON APPLICATION FOR STAY
    15	                         _________________
    16	
    17	                          No. 26A139
    18	                         _________________
    19	
    20	
    21	        ALABAMA, ET AL. v. CALIFORNIA, ET AL.
    22	                 ON APPLICATION FOR STAY
    23	                       [August 24, 2026]
    24	
    25	
    26	   PER CURIAM.
    27	   The President recently issued an Executive Order regard-
    28	ing election integrity. A group of States sued the Govern-
    29	ment in the District of Massachusetts, contending that the
    30	Order unconstitutionally infringes on their authority to ad-
    31	minister elections. The District Court agreed and enjoined
    32	the Government from implementing the Order. In doing so,
    33	the District Court rejected the Government’s argument that
    34	it lacked jurisdiction over the States’ claims because the
    35	States lack standing to bring them. The Government has
    36	asked us to stay the injunction, arguing that it is likely to
    37	succeed on the merits of its jurisdictional argument, that
    38	the injunction is causing it irreparable harm, and that the
    39	equities do not favor leaving the injunction in place while
    40	the Government’s appeal is pending. We agree and grant
    41	the stay.
    42	2                   TRUMP v. CALIFORNIA
    43	
    44	                          Per Curiam
    45	
    46	                                I
    47	  About five months ago, President Trump issued an Exec-
    48	utive Order designed to maintain public confidence in fed-
    49	eral elections. See Exec. Order No. 14399, 91 Fed. Reg.
    50	17125 (2026). The Order is an internal directive from the
    51	President to his subordinates mandating that certain agen-
    52	cies pursue certain policies. It neither requires nor forbids
    53	anything of anyone outside the Executive Branch. Three
    54	sections of the Order are relevant here.
    55	  First, §2(a) orders the Secretary of Homeland Security to
    56	“take appropriate action to” create and send “State Citizen-
    57	ship List[s]” to each State. Ibid. These Lists will catalog
    58	the residents of each State who are U. S. citizens and “who
    59	will be above the age of 18 at the time of an upcoming Fed-
    60	eral election.” Ibid. States are not required to use the Lists,
    61	and the Secretary may compile and transmit them only “[t]o
    62	the extent feasible and consistent with applicable law.” Id.,
    63	at 17125–17126.
    64	  Second, §2(b) requires the Attorney General to “prioritize
    65	the investigation and, as appropriate, the prosecution of
    66	State and local officials or any others” who issue federal bal-
    67	lots to ineligible voters. Id., at 17126.
    68	  Third, §3(b) directs the Postal Service to “initiate a pro-
    69	posed rulemaking” that includes several “[p]roposed provi-
    70	sions.” Ibid. One such provision is that outbound ballot
    71	envelopes must contain a unique barcode to facilitate track-
    72	ing. Ibid. The Order does not demand that any proposed
    73	provision appear in the final rule. Id., at 17126–17127.
    74	  All provisions of the Order must “be implemented con-
    75	sistent with applicable law.” Id., at 17127.
    76	  A few days after the Order was issued, a group of States
    77	and the District of Columbia (hereinafter “States”) filed suit
    78	in the District of Massachusetts. As relevant here, the
    79	States argued that §§2 and 3 are unconstitutional and
    80	sought to enjoin their implementation; the Government
    81	countered that the States’ suit is not justiciable because the
    82	                     Cite as: 609 U. S. ____ (2026)                     3
    83	
    84	                              Per Curiam
    85	
    86	Order does not injure them. The District Court largely
    87	agreed with the States and entered a final judgment forbid-
    88	ding the Government to implement §§2 and 3 as to them for
    89	the upcoming midterms. After the First Circuit denied the
    90	Government’s request for a stay, the Government sought a
    91	stay from this Court.
    92	   To secure a stay pending appeal, the Government must
    93	show that it is likely to succeed on the merits and that it is
    94	likely to suffer irreparable harm without a stay. See Trump
    95	v. Cook, 609 U. S. ___, ___ (2026) (slip op., at 8). It must
    96	also demonstrate that the balance of equities does not coun-
    97	sel against relief. See ibid. Of these factors, the likelihood
    98	of success on the merits and the likelihood of irreparable
    99	harm “are the most critical.” Nken v. Holder, 556 U. S. 418,
   100	434 (2009).1
   101	                              II
   102	  As to the first factor: The Government is likely to prevail
   103	on the merits of its argument that the District Court lacked
   104	jurisdiction to enter the judgment.
   105	  Two related doctrines of justiciability block the States’
   106	suit. One is standing, which requires “an injury that is con-
   107	crete, particularized, and imminent rather than ‘conjec-
   108	tural or hypothetical.’ ” Carney v. Adams, 592 U. S. 53, 60
   109	(2020). The other is ripeness, which forbids federal courts
   110	to adjudicate disputes that are “dependent on ‘contingent
   111	future events that may not occur as anticipated, or indeed
   112	may not occur at all.’ ” Trump v. New York, 592 U. S. 125,
   113	131 (2020) (per curiam). Because “standing is not dis-
   114	pensed in gross,” we address each challenged provision of
   115	the Order separately. TransUnion LLC v. Ramirez, 594
   116	U. S. 413, 431 (2021).
   117	
   118	
   119	——————
   120	  1 The States do not dispute that this application presents a significant
   121	
   122	legal question. Trump v. Cook, 609 U. S., at ___ (slip op., at 8).
   123	4                   TRUMP v. CALIFORNIA
   124	
   125	                          Per Curiam
   126	
   127	                               A
   128	   Section 2(a) directs the Secretary of Homeland Security
   129	to take appropriate action to create and distribute State
   130	Citizenship Lists. This provision is an internal directive
   131	from the President to a subordinate. It imposes no obliga-
   132	tions on the States, so they “suffer no concrete harm from”
   133	it. Trump v. New York, 592 U. S., at 134. And without con-
   134	crete harm, there is no standing. TransUnion, 594 U. S., at
   135	442.
   136	   The true “source of any injury” to the States would be the
   137	downstream action that the Secretary “might take in the
   138	future” to implement §2(a). Trump v. New York, 592 U. S.,
   139	at 133–134. Emphasis on might. Section 2(a) can be imple-
   140	mented only “[t]o the extent feasible and consistent with
   141	applicable law.” 91 Fed. Reg. 17125. And as the Court ex-
   142	plained in another case involving a Presidential directive
   143	with a similar qualifier: “Any prediction how the Executive
   144	Branch might eventually implement” §2(a) while balancing
   145	feasibility and lawfulness “is ‘no more than conjecture’ at
   146	this time.” Trump v. New York, 592 U. S., at 131. The Sec-
   147	retary’s “eventual action will reflect both legal and practical
   148	constraints, making any prediction about future injury just
   149	that—a prediction.” Id., at 133; see 91 Fed. Reg. 17125.
   150	   To see why, consider the unduly long “chain of inferences”
   151	necessary to support the States’ supposedly concrete injury.
   152	Clapper v. Amnesty Int’l USA, 568 U. S. 398, 414–415, n. 5
   153	(2013). The District Court first had to speculate about
   154	whether it would be “feasible” for the Secretary to create
   155	and transmit the Lists before the midterms. 91 Fed. Reg.
   156	17125. But §2(a) “may not prove feasible to implement in
   157	any manner whatsoever.” Trump v. New York, 592 U. S.,
   158	at 132. The court then had to guess how the Secretary
   159	might tailor his implementation to be “consistent with ap-
   160	plicable law.” 91 Fed. Reg. 17125. Next, the court had to
   161	predict what “action” the Secretary would deem “appropri-
   162	ate” for implementing §2(a). Ibid. After all that, the court
   163	                  Cite as: 609 U. S. ____ (2026)            5
   164	
   165	                           Per Curiam
   166	
   167	still had to estimate whether those actions would be taken
   168	“in a manner substantially likely to harm” the States.
   169	Trump v. New York, 592 U. S., at 132. But Article III pro-
   170	hibits federal courts from stacking hypothetical on hypo-
   171	thetical to speculate that harm will eventually materialize.
   172	Clapper, 568 U. S., at 414–415, n. 5. Adjudicating this
   173	claim requires a prediction not only about whether the Sec-
   174	retary will act before the midterms, but also about the
   175	shape that any such action might ultimately take. Article
   176	III demands more certainty.
   177	   The lower courts saw it differently, concluding that the
   178	States suffered “a present injury” from §2(a) because it re-
   179	quired them “to begin planning now on how to comply with”
   180	it. App. to Application in No. 26A124, pp. 7a–8a. But the
   181	States “cannot manufacture standing merely by inflicting
   182	harm on themselves based on their fears of hypothetical fu-
   183	ture harm that is not certainly impending.” Clapper, 568
   184	U. S., at 416. And the hypothetical future harm that the
   185	States fear—the Secretary’s implementation of §2(a)—is far
   186	from “certainly impending.” Ibid. By allowing the States
   187	“to bring this action based on costs they incurred in re-
   188	sponse to a speculative threat,” the District Court “improp-
   189	erly water[ed] down the fundamental requirements of Arti-
   190	cle III.” Ibid.
   191	   Perhaps recognizing the weakness of that standing ra-
   192	tionale, the Court of Appeals added another: §2(a) infringes
   193	the States’ sovereign authority to create election rules. But
   194	§2(a) does no such thing. Instead, it simply directs the Sec-
   195	retary to take certain actions if appropriate, feasible, and
   196	lawful. 91 Fed. Reg. 17125–17126. This internal directive
   197	does not regulate the States’ voter registration processes.
   198	Nor does it impinge on the States’ authority “ ‘to create and
   199	enforce a legal code’ for elections.” Contra, App. to Applica-
   200	tion in No. 26A124, p. 13a.
   201	6                   TRUMP v. CALIFORNIA
   202	
   203	                          Per Curiam
   204	
   205	                               B
   206	   The States lack standing to challenge §2(b) for much the
   207	same reason: It does nothing to them. This provision di-
   208	rects the Attorney General to “prioritize” the investigation
   209	and prosecution of those who violate existing federal laws
   210	by issuing ballots to ineligible voters. 91 Fed. Reg. 17126
   211	(collecting statutes). Setting prosecutorial priorities lies
   212	within the President’s Article II power. And his decision to
   213	prioritize the prosecution of certain offenses does not inflict
   214	a traditionally recognized harm on the States.              See
   215	TransUnion, 594 U. S., at 424–425.
   216	   The States try to avoid this straightforward analysis by
   217	misreading the Order. They insist that §2(b) credibly
   218	threatens them with prosecution if they do not use the Cit-
   219	izenship Lists from §2(a). See Opposition to Applications
   220	21–22, 29–30; see also post, at 2–3 (SOTOMAYOR, J., dissent-
   221	ing); post, at 14–15 (JACKSON, J., dissenting). But that view
   222	is inconsistent with the text of §2. The States concede that
   223	§2(a) does not require them to use the Lists at all (assuming
   224	they receive them in the first place). Opposition to Applica-
   225	tions 21. So it is logically impossible for a State to “violate”
   226	§2(a)—and §2(b) does not suggest otherwise. Section 2(b)
   227	directs the Attorney General to “prioritize” prosecutions of
   228	those who violate federal election laws. It then provides an
   229	array of existing statutes for the Attorney General to en-
   230	force. 91 Fed. Reg. 17126. Absent is any reference to Citi-
   231	zenship Lists. Without a textual basis for their concerns,
   232	the States lack a “well-founded fear” that they will be pros-
   233	ecuted for not using the Lists. Virginia v. American
   234	Booksellers Assn., Inc., 484 U. S. 383, 393 (1988).
   235	   The States’ reference to certain state laws allowing 17-
   236	year-olds to vote in primary elections is a red herring. See
   237	Opposition to Applications 29–30. Because §2(b) defines el-
   238	igible voters as otherwise qualified citizens who are “18
   239	years of age or older,” 91 Fed. Reg. 17126, the States insist
   240	that they face a real threat of prosecution if they deliver a
   241	                  Cite as: 609 U. S. ____ (2026)              7
   242	
   243	                           Per Curiam
   244	
   245	ballot to a 17-year-old who is eligible to vote in a primary
   246	under state law. Not so. Section 7(b) of the Order requires
   247	§2(b) to “be implemented consistent with applicable law.”
   248	Id., at 17127. And on top of that, §2(b) limits itself to only
   249	“appropriate” prosecutions. Id., at 17126. Given these pro-
   250	visions, the Government has unsurprisingly disavowed any
   251	intention to prosecute state officials for lawfully providing
   252	ballots to 17-year-olds.
   253	                                 C
   254	   Last is the States’ challenge to §3, which directs the
   255	Postal Service to issue a notice of proposed rulemaking. Ac-
   256	cording to the States, this internal directive demands that
   257	they overhaul their existing election procedures. See Oppo-
   258	sition to Applications 24–26; see also post, at 1, 3, 7 (opinion
   259	of JACKSON, J.). It does not. Section 3 orders the Postal
   260	Service to initiate the process of proposing a rule that might
   261	bind the States in the future. It is not a proposed rule, let
   262	alone a final rule. And it imposes no legal requirements on
   263	the States (or, for that matter, on anyone outside the Exec-
   264	utive Branch).
   265	   The District Court’s conclusion that this internal di-
   266	rective inflicts an Article III injury on the States necessarily
   267	rests on an “attenuated chain of inferences.” Clapper, 568
   268	U. S., at 414–415, n. 5. At the time this suit was filed—the
   269	time when standing is measured—the District Court had to
   270	engage in a string of speculations to find this suit justicia-
   271	ble. It first had to speculate about whether the Postal Ser-
   272	vice would propose a rule. Then it had to speculate about
   273	how the agency would weigh the comments it received. Cf.
   274	Perez v. Mortgage Bankers Assn., 575 U. S. 92, 96 (2015)
   275	(emphasizing that agencies typically “must consider and re-
   276	spond to significant comments”). Then it had to speculate
   277	about whether the agency would issue a final rule. See
   278	Long Island Care at Home, Ltd. v. Coke, 551 U. S. 158, 175
   279	(2007) (explaining that a proposed rule is “simply a
   280	8                   TRUMP v. CALIFORNIA
   281	
   282	                          Per Curiam
   283	
   284	proposal,” and an agency “might choose to adopt the pro-
   285	posal or to withdraw it”). Then it had to speculate about
   286	what the final rule would say. Cf. Encino Motorcars, LLC
   287	v. Navarro, 579 U. S. 211, 218 (2016) (noting that the final
   288	rule “took the opposite position from the proposed rule”).
   289	Finally, after all that, the District Court still had to specu-
   290	late about whether the text of a hypothetical final rule
   291	would harm the States. It should go without saying that
   292	the District Court made a decision “dependent on ‘contin-
   293	gent future events that may not occur as anticipated, or in-
   294	deed may not occur at all.’ ” Trump v. New York, 592 U. S.,
   295	at 131.
   296	   The States cannot preemptively concretize their injury by
   297	expending funds before the Postal Service finalizes a rule.
   298	Spending money to hedge against a “speculative threat”
   299	does not constitute an Article III injury. Clapper, 568 U. S.,
   300	at 416. Though it may sometimes be “a reasonable reac-
   301	tion” to “incu[r] certain costs” in advance of a final rule,
   302	those costs cannot establish standing when the final rule
   303	remains a “hypothetical future harm that is not certainly
   304	impending.” Ibid. Federal courts review final rules, not
   305	proposed rules—and certainly not antecedent internal di-
   306	rectives to propose a rule. If the Postal Service’s final rule
   307	harms the States, they may challenge that rule.
   308	                             III
   309	  The remaining stay factors also favor relief. The Govern-
   310	ment is likely to suffer irreparable harm without a stay.
   311	The District Court’s breach of Article III interferes with the
   312	internal operations of the Executive Branch. See INS v.
   313	Legalization Assistance Project of Los Angeles County Fed-
   314	eration of Labor, 510 U. S. 1301, 1306 (1993) (O’Connor, J.,
   315	in chambers). The injunction bars Homeland Security from
   316	trying to compile Citizenship Lists for about half the States
   317	in the Union. It purports to forbid the Attorney General to
   318	implement the President’s enforcement priorities as to
   319	                      Cite as: 609 U. S. ____ (2026)                       9
   320	
   321	                                Per Curiam
   322	
   323	those same States.2 And it prevents the Postal Service from
   324	so much as initiating a rulemaking that could yield a rule
   325	applicable to these States. Because reversal of the District
   326	Court’s judgment would come too late for the 2026 mid-
   327	terms, the District Court’s errors deal “a serious setback” to
   328	the Executive’s “goals” while this litigation unfolds. Cole-
   329	man v. Paccar Inc., 424 U. S. 1301, 1307 (1976) (Rehnquist,
   330	J., in chambers). When a federal court “improperly in-
   331	trudes on a coordinate branch” in this way, interim relief is
   332	justified. Trump v. CASA, Inc., 606 U. S. 831, 859 (2025)
   333	(alterations and internal quotation marks omitted).
   334	   Fighting this Court’s precedent, JUSTICE JACKSON main-
   335	tains that “[t]he Government is not injured in the slightest”
   336	because the Order contemplates that the Government will
   337	engage in “patently unlawful conduct.” Post, at 7. That,
   338	however, remains to be seen. And though JUSTICE JACKSON
   339	wishes it were otherwise, an applicant can show “irrepara-
   340	ble harm from a threshold error without also showing that,
   341	at the end of the day, it will prevail on the underlying mer-
   342	its.” CASA, 606 U. S., at 860. Such harm exists here be-
   343	cause the injunction meddles in “the workings of ” the Ex-
   344	ecutive Branch, preventing it from even trying to lawfully
   345	
   346	——————
   347	   2 The principal dissent suggests that, if the Order does not legally bind
   348	
   349	the States, then the Government cannot suffer irreparable harm from
   350	the injunction. See post, at 3 (opinion of SOTOMAYOR, J.). The dissent
   351	reasons that this is so because the injunction merely prohibits prosecu-
   352	tions “ ‘stemming from violations or purported violations of the unconsti-
   353	tutional provisions in’ ” the Order. Ibid. And if it is impossible to violate
   354	the Order, then the injunction targets a null set of conduct, so the Gov-
   355	ernment suffers no harm at all. Ibid. But the injunction is far broader
   356	than the dissent lets on. It declares all of §2 ultra vires as to the States,
   357	blocks the Government from “giving effect to” §2 as to the States, and
   358	requires the Government “to prevent explicit or implicit implementation
   359	of ” §2 as to the States. Final Judgment in No. 1:26–cv–11581 (D Mass.),
   360	ECF Doc. 207, pp. 2–4. It is the breadth of these provisions—and the
   361	resulting intrusion into the Executive Branch’s internal operations—
   362	that irreparably harms the Government.
   363	10                  TRUMP v. CALIFORNIA
   364	
   365	                          Per Curiam
   366	
   367	implement the Order. Legalization Assistance Project, 510
   368	U. S., at 1306.
   369	  Finally, “the balance of equities does not counsel against
   370	awarding the Government interim relief.” CASA, 606 U. S.,
   371	at 861. The Executive Order makes no demand of the
   372	States, so staying the District Court’s injunction will not
   373	concretely harm them. That is why this suit was not justi-
   374	ciable in the first place.
   375	                         *     *     *
   376	  The Court’s disposition of this application does not mean
   377	that any measure taken by the Government to implement
   378	the Order will necessarily be lawful. On that score, time
   379	will tell. But the Order itself does not harm the States, so
   380	the District Court lacked jurisdiction to bar the Govern-
   381	ment from trying to implement it. And for the reasons dis-
   382	cussed, that injunction is likely to irreparably harm the
   383	Government if it remains in place while the appellate pro-
   384	cess runs its course.
   385	  Accordingly, the application for stay presented to
   386	JUSTICE JACKSON in No. 26A124 and by her referred to the
   387	Court is granted. The injunction entered by the United
   388	States District Court for the District of Massachusetts in
   389	case No. 1:26–cv–11581 is stayed pending the disposition of
   390	the appeal in the United States Court of Appeals for the
   391	First Circuit and disposition of a petition for a writ of certi-
   392	orari in this Court, if such a writ is timely sought. Should
   393	certiorari be denied, this stay shall terminate automati-
   394	cally. In the event certiorari is granted, the stay shall ter-
   395	minate upon the sending down of the judgment of this
   396	Court. The application for stay presented to JUSTICE
   397	JACKSON in No. 26A139 and by her referred to the Court is
   398	denied as moot.
   399	                 Cite as: 609 U. S. ____ (2026)           1
   400	
   401	                   SOTOMAYOR, J., dissenting
   402	
   403	SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES
   404	                         _________________
   405	
   406	                          No. 26A124
   407	                         _________________
   408	
   409	
   410	  DONALD J. TRUMP, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED
   411	      STATES, ET AL. v. CALIFORNIA, ET AL.
   412	                ON APPLICATION FOR STAY
   413	                         _________________
   414	
   415	                          No. 26A139
   416	                         _________________
   417	
   418	
   419	       ALABAMA, ET AL. v. CALIFORNIA, ET AL.
   420	                ON APPLICATION FOR STAY
   421	                       [August 24, 2026]
   422	
   423	  JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR, with whom JUSTICE KAGAN joins,
   424	dissenting.
   425	  Today’s decision does not address whether the
   426	President’s    attempts     to    interfere  with    States’
   427	administration of the November 2026 elections are lawful.
   428	Nor does it suggest that the Executive Branch has any
   429	constitutional or statutory authority to implement the
   430	President’s directives. Instead, today’s decision merely
   431	postpones adjudication of those challenges. Nonetheless,
   432	because I believe the respondent States’ challenges are
   433	justiciable now, I respectfully dissent.
   434	  A commonsense reading of the Executive Order,
   435	corroborated by the Government’s own representations,
   436	makes clear that the respondent States face a sufficiently
   437	concrete and imminent injury to satisfy Article III. Section
   438	2 of the Executive Order has two subsections. Subsection
   439	2(a) orders the Secretary of Homeland Security to create
   440	state Citizenship Lists of “confirmed” citizens who will be
   441	over the age of 18 as of an upcoming federal election.
   442	Subsection 2(b) then directs the Attorney General to
   443	2                   TRUMP v. CALIFORNIA
   444	
   445	                    SOTOMAYOR, J., dissenting
   446	
   447	prioritize the investigation and prosecution of state and
   448	local officials who provide ballots to individuals who are not
   449	eligible to vote, citing statutes prohibiting non-citizens from
   450	voting. The majority accepts the Government’s invitation
   451	to divorce 2(a) from 2(b), parsing the text of each subsection
   452	individually but refusing to read §2 as a unified whole.
   453	According to the majority, the investigations and
   454	prosecutions that subsection 2(b) prioritizes might have
   455	nothing to do with the citizenship lists that the immediately
   456	preceding subsection 2(a) orders to be created. The clear
   457	import of §2, however, is that the Federal Government will
   458	use its lists of “confirmed” citizens, which it has conceded
   459	will be incomplete, App. to Application for Stay 5a, to
   460	initiate those investigations and prosecutions.
   461	   Far from disclaiming such intention, the Government has
   462	admitted that the lists “might be used solely to facilitate
   463	this sort of post-election law-enforcement activity,” Govt.
   464	Brief in Opposition in No. 1:26–cv–11581 (D Mass.), ECF
   465	Doc. 157, p. 47, and that a State’s “refusal to consider” the
   466	Federal Government’s list “could conceivably, depending on
   467	the circumstances, be relevant evidence of an intent to
   468	violate, or to facilitate the violation of, the federal laws
   469	prohibiting non-citizen voting that the Attorney General is
   470	directed to prioritize for enforcement under Section 2(b),”
   471	Reply Brief in No. 26A124, p. 13. To pretend that the lists
   472	assembled pursuant to 2(a) bear no relation to the
   473	prosecutions directed by 2(b) is to ignore the structure of
   474	the Executive Order and the Government’s words alike. As
   475	this Court has long recognized, “[p]eople do not lightly
   476	disregard public officers’ thinly veiled threats to institute
   477	criminal proceedings against them if they do not come
   478	around,” Bantam Books, Inc. v. Sullivan, 372 U. S. 58, 68
   479	(1963), and the respondent States have standing based on
   480	the credible threat that the Government will initiate
   481	investigations and prosecutions because they refuse to use
   482	the lists. See First Choice Women’s Resource Centers, Inc.
   483	                     Cite as: 609 U. S. ____ (2026)                    3
   484	
   485	                       SOTOMAYOR, J., dissenting
   486	
   487	v. Davenport, 608 U. S. 174, 183 (2026) (“Because this
   488	standard tolerates suits involving ‘actual or imminent’
   489	injuries, a party need not always wait for the government
   490	to take coercive action against it before filing suit to
   491	challenge the government’s conduct. Instead, a litigant
   492	may bring a pre-enforcement suit seeking prospective relief
   493	against government officials so long as it faces ‘a credible
   494	threat of enforcement.’ ” (quoting Susan B. Anthony List v.
   495	Driehaus, 573 U. S. 149, 161 (2014)).
   496	   Nor can I agree that the Government suffers irreparable
   497	harm from the portion of the District Court’s injunction
   498	tailored to these threatened prosecutions. The majority
   499	suggests that the injunction “purports to forbid the
   500	Attorney General to implement the President’s
   501	enforcement priorities as to those same States.” Ante, at 8–
   502	9.    But this portion of the injunction prohibits the
   503	Department of Justice and the Attorney General only from
   504	“initiating any investigation or prosecution of Plaintiff
   505	States . . . stemming from violations or purported violations
   506	of the unconstitutional provisions in Sections 2 and 3 of the
   507	EO.” Final Judgment in No. 1:26–cv–11581 (D Mass.), ECF
   508	Doc. 207, p. 4, ¶2.C. If, as the majority explains, it is
   509	“logically impossible for a State to ‘violate’ §2(a),” and §3
   510	“imposes no legal requirements on the States,” ante, at 6–
   511	7, this portion of the injunction would be toothless. If that
   512	is true, it is hard to see how the Government suffers any
   513	harm at all, much less irreparable harm, from this portion
   514	of the injunction.*
   515	   The same is true for the §2(a) injunction. If, as the
   516	Government insists, §2(a) “simply give[s] States a valuable
   517	tool that can help in vetting their own lists of registered
   518	voters if they wish to use it,” Reply Brief in No. 26A124, p.
   519	——————
   520	  *The majority responds by pointing to other portions of the injunction.

