eme Court &middot; September 6, 2026</p><h1 data-layer="fact">The administration asks the Supreme Court to lift a new block on mail-ballot rules</h1><p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">On Sunday the solicitor general filed application 26A305, asking the justices to pause a September 4 injunction of Postal Service ballot-mail rules. That injunction covers elections on or before November 3. Sunday&#39;s filing is 58 days before that election.</p><div class="mode-switcher" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Reading mode"><a class="mode-btn active" href="?mode=story" data-mode="story" role="radio" aria-checked="true">The story</a><a class="mode-btn" href="?mode=facts" data-mode="facts" role="radio" aria-checked="false">Just the facts</a><a class="mode-btn" href="?mode=work" data-mode="work" role="radio" aria-checked="false">Show the work</a></div><script type="module">document.addEventListener(`DOMContentLoaded`,()=>{let e=document.getElementById(`story-root`),t=document.querySelectorAll(`.mode-btn`);if(!e||!t.length)return;let n=new URLSearchParams(window.location.search),r=[`story`,`facts`,`work`].includes(n.get(`mode`))?n.get(`mode`):`story`;e.classList.add(`mode-${r}`);let i=e.querySelector(`[data-record-ledger]`);i&&(i.open=r===`work`),t.forEach(e=>{let t=e.getAttribute(`data-mode`)===r;e.classList.toggle(`active`,t),e.setAttribute(`aria-checked`,t?`true`:`false`),e.setAttribute(`tabindex`,t?`0`:`-1`)}),t.forEach((n,r)=>{n.addEventListener(`keydown`,e=>{let n=e.key===`ArrowRight`||e.key===`ArrowDown`?1:e.key===`ArrowLeft`||e.key===`ArrowUp`?-1:0;if(!n&&e.key!==`Home`&&e.key!==`End`&&e.key!==` `)return;e.preventDefault();let i=e.key===`Home`?t[0]:e.key===`End`?t[t.length-1]:t[(r+n+t.length)%t.length];i.click(),i.focus()}),n.addEventListener(`click`,r=>{r.preventDefault();let a=[...e.querySelectorAll(`section[id], aside[id]`)],o=a.find(e=>`#`+e.id===location.hash&&e.getBoundingClientRect().top>=0&&e.getBoundingClientRect().top<innerHeight)??a.filter(e=>e.getBoundingClientRect().height&&e.getBoundingClientRect().top<=160).at(-1),s=n.getAttribute(`data-mode`);i&&(i.open=s===`work`),e.className=e.className.replace(/mode-\w+/g,``).trim()+` mode-${s}`,t.forEach(e=>{let t=e===n;e.classList.toggle(`active`,t),e.setAttribute(`aria-checked`,t?`true`:`false`),e.setAttribute(`tabindex`,t?`0`:`-1`)});let c=new URL(window.location.href);if(c.searchParams.set(`mode`,s),o){let e=a.indexOf(o),t=o.getBoundingClientRect().height?o:[...a.slice(0,e).reverse(),...a.slice(e+1)].find(e=>e.getBoundingClientRect().height);t&&(requestAnimationFrame(()=>t.scrollIntoView({block:`start`})),c.hash=t.id)}history.replaceState(null,``,c.toString()),document.dispatchEvent(new Event(`reading-mode-change`))})})});</script><nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact"><details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><ol><li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li><li><a href="#why-blocked">Why the judge blocked it</a></li><li><a href="#the-catch">The catch</a></li><li><a href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a></li><li><a href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a></li><li><a href="#outlets">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</a></li><li><a href="#claim-check">Claim checked against the record</a></li><li><a href="#what-happened-next">What happened next</a></li><li><a href="#unknowns">What we do not know yet</a></li><li><a href="#records">The records</a></li></ol></details></nav><div class="kpi-strip" data-layer="fact"><div class="kpi"><span class="kpi-value">26A305<small></small></span><span class="kpi-label">Sunday stay application, filed September 6</span></div><div class="kpi"><span class="kpi-value">58<small> days</small></span><span class="kpi-label">from that filing to November 3</span></div><div class="kpi"><span class="kpi-value">23<small> states</small></span><span class="kpi-label">plus D.C. and Pennsylvania&#39;s governor, as plaintiffs</span></div><div class="kpi"><span class="kpi-value">Nov. 3<small></small></span><span class="kpi-label">federal election the injunction names</span></div></div><aside class="three-things" data-layer="fact"><p class="tt-label">Three things to know</p><ol><li>On Sunday, September 6, Solicitor General D. John Sauer filed application 26A305. An application is a request to a justice to freeze a lower-court order while an appeal continues. This one asks the Supreme Court to stay, meaning pause, a September 4 injunction from U.S. District Judge Indira Talwani in Boston.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-2" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-docket/" data-passage="No. 26A305" aria-label="Source 2: read the evidence">2</a></sup></li><li>Talwani's injunction forbids the Postal Service from carrying out named mandatory sections of its Ballot Mail for Federal Elections rule, issued and effective August 21 and published August 26, for elections on or before November 3. It dissolves her August 27 temporary restraining order, a 14-day pause that the new order replaces.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-10" data-record-href="/records/talwani-pi-2026-09-04/" data-passage="elections occurring before or on November 3" aria-label="Source 10: read the evidence">10</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-12" data-record-href="/records/usps-final-rule/" data-passage="Effective August 21, 2026" aria-label="Source 12: read the evidence">12</a></sup></li><li>The same Sunday Sauer withdrew application 26A297, filed Thursday against that temporary order. The word "another" in the coverage is this third trip to the Court. Application 26A124, filed July 27, was granted August 24.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-5" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a297-withdrawal/" data-passage="the United States withdraws its September 3 stay application" aria-label="Source 5: read the evidence">5</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-8" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a124-docket/" data-passage="Application (26A124) granted by the Court." aria-label="Source 8: read the evidence">8</a></sup></li></ol></aside><p class="railed rail-note" data-layer="fact">Story updated 2026-09-08.</p><div data-layer="proof" class="event-record-context"><p class="sources-line">This story rests on Executive Order 14399 as published in the Federal Register, the Postal Service's final rule (issued and effective August 21, published August 26), the Privacy Act notices for system USPS 820.225, Judge Talwani's September 4 injunction, the three Supreme Court applications and their dockets, Sauer's withdrawal letter, 2 U.S.C. 7, 52 U.S.C. 20302, Senator Blumenthal's September 1 whistleblower release, the Postal Service's September 1 portal statement, and coverage checked against those records. The days on this page are counted from those dates. The figures come from the official Federal Register PDFs.</p></div><section id="what-happened"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The order first, then the rule, then the Court</p><h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2><p data-layer="narrative">On March 31, 2026, President Donald J. Trump signed Executive Order 14399, "Ensuring Citizenship Verification and Integrity in Federal Elections." Section 3 of that order directed the Postmaster General to start a rulemaking on mail-in and absentee ballots, including a proposed line that the Postal Service "shall not transmit" those ballots from anyone not enrolled on a state-specific list.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-11" data-record-href="/records/eo-14399/" data-passage="the USPS shall not transmit mail-" aria-label="Source 11: read the evidence">11</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">On August 21 the Postal Service issued a final rule, Ballot Mail for Federal Elections, and made it effective that day. The rule was published in the Federal Register on August 26. It amends the Domestic Mail Manual. It requires official Election Mail logos, automation-compatible envelopes, unique Intelligent Mail barcodes, and an online Federal Ballot Mail Portal where election officials enroll the name, address, and barcodes of people they intend to send a ballot.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-12" data-record-href="/records/usps-final-rule/" data-passage="Effective August 21, 2026" aria-label="Source 12: read the evidence">12</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">Outbound mailings presented by election offices that do not comply "will not be accepted" and "will be returned" to the "authorized ballot mailer." Section 705.24.5 "does not apply to Return Federal Ballot Mail." Return envelopes still have design rules; the batch nonacceptance screen is the outbound procedure.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-12" data-record-href="/records/usps-final-rule/" data-passage="does not apply to Return Federal Ballot Mail" aria-label="Source 12: read the evidence">12</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">The rule states an "express exclusion of UOCAVA ballots" from its scope. The Postal Service wrote that those voters "will experience no change" if the exemption is applied as written. UOCAVA covers uniformed and overseas voters under 52 U.S.C. 20302.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-12" data-record-href="/records/usps-final-rule/" data-passage="express exclusion of UOCAVA ballots" aria-label="Source 12: read the evidence">12</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-14" data-record-href="/records/uocava-52usc20302/" data-passage="not later than 45 days before the election" aria-label="Source 14: read the evidence">14</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">The portal sits on a federal Privacy Act system, "USPS 820.225 Federal Ballot Mail." The July 17 notice places it at "USPS Headquarters, Supplier cloud" computing environment. The first category of records is "Mail in voter information: Name," address, unique Intelligent Mail barcode numbers, and originating election-office state. Records are retrievable by name, address, issuing state, and barcode, and "Records are retained for 5 years." A stated purpose is to help determine adherence to federal law and facilitate law-enforcement efforts. State and local officials load the rows. The system is not a citizenship file. The Postal Service's September 1 response to comments said the system is effective with that publication.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-30" data-record-href="/records/usps-sorn-820-225/" data-passage="Records are retained for 5 years." aria-label="Source 30: read the evidence">30</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-31" data-record-href="/records/usps-sorn-response-2026-09-01/" data-passage="effective with this publication of the" aria-label="Source 31: read the evidence">31</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">A group of states had already sued over the executive order. On August 24 the Supreme Court granted application 26A124, staying the district court's injunction of that order. A stay is an order that pauses another court's order. The unsigned opinion said the states' challenge was not ripe because the Court did not yet know what a final rule would say. It added that the disposition "does not mean that any measure taken by the Government to implement the Order will necessarily be lawful."<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-9" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a124-opinion/" data-passage="any measure taken by the Government to implement" aria-label="Source 9: read the evidence">9</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">On August 27 Talwani entered a temporary restraining order against mandatory parts of the new rule. On September 3 Sauer asked the Court, in application 26A297, to stay that order. The docket shows the application was submitted to Justice Jackson. She set a response for September 8. The emergency docket is the Court's fast track for those requests, usually without a full argument.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-4" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a297-docket/" data-passage="submitted to Justice Jackson" aria-label="Source 4: read the evidence">4</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">On September 4 Talwani issued a preliminary injunction. A preliminary injunction is a court order that forbids someone from doing something while a lawsuit continues. This one names specific Domestic Mail Manual sections and applies to elections on or before November 3. She dissolved the August 27 temporary order the same day. These holdings are preliminary findings for that order, not a final judgment on the merits.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-10" data-record-href="/records/talwani-pi-2026-09-04/" data-passage="The Temporary Restraining Order" aria-label="Source 10: read the evidence">10</a></sup></p><h3 id="why-blocked" data-layer="fact">Why the judge blocked it</h3><p data-layer="narrative">Talwani wrote under "Likelihood of Success on the Merits" that the plaintiffs had established a substantial likelihood of success on their Elections Clause claim. She found the rule is an election rule that regulates the time, place, and manner of elections, and that it is unconstitutional where Congress has not authorized such a rule under the Elections Clause.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-10" data-record-href="/records/talwani-pi-2026-09-04/" data-passage="Likelihood of Success on the Merits" aria-label="Source 10: read the evidence">10</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">She separately found the rule likely ultra vires, meaning beyond the agency's statutory authority: Congress has not authorized the Postal Service to make this election rule, and Title 39 does not grant that power.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-10" data-record-href="/records/talwani-pi-2026-09-04/" data-passage="The Final Rule is Also Ultra Vires" aria-label="Source 10: read the evidence">10</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">She also found the rule likely creates a new category of nonmailable material, federal election mail, contrary to Title 39's scheme for what Congress has listed as nonmailable.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-10" data-record-href="/records/talwani-pi-2026-09-04/" data-passage="new category of nonmailable materials" aria-label="Source 10: read the evidence">10</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">The government contests those findings. In application 26A305, Sauer writes that "The Rule is a lawful and modest exercise" of rulemaking authority Congress delegated to the Postal Service, that "it regulates the U.S. Mail, not federal elections," and that "it does not regulate state administration of elections."<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-application/" data-passage="The Rule is a lawful and modest exercise" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">On September 6 Sauer wrote the clerk that the United States was withdrawing 26A297, and he filed 26A305 against the new injunction. Jackson's order on that docket set any response for 4 p.m. Eastern on September 9. Sunday is 58 days before November 3, the federal election day named in the injunction and in 2 U.S.C. 7 as the Tuesday after the first Monday in November of an even-numbered year.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-2" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-docket/" data-passage="4 p.m. (EDT) on September 9, 2026." aria-label="Source 2: read the evidence">2</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-13" data-record-href="/records/usc-2-7/" data-passage="Tuesday next after the 1st Monday in November" aria-label="Source 13: read the evidence">13</a></sup></p><figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact"><figcaption><strong>From the order to the election</strong><span>Dated steps in the filings and dockets.</span></figcaption><div class="decision-timeline"><div class="dt-head"><span class="dt-date">Date</span><span class="dt-body">Decision</span></div><div class="dt-row"><span class="dt-date">Mar 31</span><span class="dt-body">Executive Order 14399 <span class="dt-sub">Directs a Postal Service rulemaking on mail-in and absentee ballots</span></span></div><div class="dt-row"><span class="dt-date">Jul 27</span><span class="dt-body">Application 26A124 filed <span class="dt-sub">Stay of the district court&#39;s injunction of the order</span></span></div><div class="dt-row"><span class="dt-date">Aug 21</span><span class="dt-body">USPS issues the final rule <span class="dt-sub">Effective the same day: Ballot Mail for Federal Elections</span></span></div><div class="dt-row"><span class="dt-date">Aug 24</span><span class="dt-body">The Court grants 26A124 <span class="dt-sub">Unsigned opinion: the States&#39; challenge to the order was not ripe</span></span></div><div class="dt-row"><span class="dt-date">Aug 26</span><span class="dt-body">USPS final rule published <span class="dt-sub">Federal Register, 91 Fed. Reg. 54,966</span></span></div><div class="dt-row"><span class="dt-date">Aug 27</span><span class="dt-body">Temporary restraining order <span class="dt-sub">Judge Talwani pauses mandatory parts of the rule for 14 days</span></span></div><div class="dt-row"><span class="dt-date">Sep 3</span><span class="dt-body">Application 26A297 filed <span class="dt-sub">Stay of that temporary order, submitted to Justice Jackson</span></span></div><div class="dt-row"><span class="dt-date">Sep 4</span><span class="dt-body">Preliminary injunction <span class="dt-sub">Talwani enjoins named sections of the rule through November 3</span></span></div><div class="dt-row dt-current"><span class="dt-date">Sep 6</span><span class="dt-body">26A297 withdrawn; 26A305 filed <span class="dt-sub">Stay of the September 4 injunction; response due September 9</span></span></div><div class="dt-row"><span class="dt-date">Nov 3</span><span class="dt-body">Federal election day <span class="dt-sub">The Tuesday after the first Monday in November</span></span></div></div><p class="chart-source">Sunday's application is the third stay request in this fight.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-2" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-docket/" data-passage="No. 26A305" aria-label="Source 2: read the evidence">2</a></sup></p></figure><figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact"><figcaption><strong>The four court steps Sunday's application sits on</strong><span>Temporary order, Thursday's stay request, the injunction, Sunday's stay request.</span></figcaption><div class="decision-timeline"><div class="dt-head"><span class="dt-date">Date</span><span class="dt-body">Decision</span></div><div class="dt-row"><span class="dt-date">Aug 27</span><span class="dt-body">Temporary restraining order <span class="dt-sub">Talwani pauses mandatory parts of the August 21 rule for 14 days</span></span></div><div class="dt-row"><span class="dt-date">Sep 3</span><span class="dt-body">Application 26A297 filed <span class="dt-sub">Stay of that temporary order, submitted to Justice Jackson</span></span></div><div class="dt-row"><span class="dt-date">Sep 4</span><span class="dt-body">Preliminary injunction <span class="dt-sub">Talwani enjoins named sections of the rule through November 3</span></span></div><div class="dt-row dt-current"><span class="dt-date">Sep 6</span><span class="dt-body">Application 26A305 filed <span class="dt-sub">26A297 withdrawn; stay of the September 4 injunction; response due September 9</span></span></div></div><p class="chart-source">26A297 was withdrawn the day 26A305 was docketed.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-4" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a297-docket/" data-passage="Application (26A297) withdrawn." aria-label="Source 4: read the evidence">4</a></sup></p></figure><figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact"><figcaption><strong>The enjoined Domestic Mail Manual sections</strong><span>What each named section of the August 26 rule requires, in the rule's own terms.</span></figcaption><figure class="data-table-wrap"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th class="hl">Section</th><th class>What it requires</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="hl">705.24.3.1</td><td class>Outbound ballot envelopes must carry the official Election Mail logo, be automation-compatible, and bear the voter&#39;s unique Intelligent Mail barcode, and must be submitted for design review.</td></tr><tr><td class="hl">705.24.3.2</td><td class>The same envelope standards for return ballot envelopes.</td></tr><tr><td class="hl">705.24.4.1</td><td class>Each portal user must register. The state&#39;s chief election official authorizes users.</td></tr><tr><td class="hl">705.24.4.2</td><td class>States that intend to receive mail ballots through the Postal Service must enroll each voter: name, address, outbound barcode, return barcode, and originating election-office state.</td></tr><tr><td class="hl">705.24.5.1</td><td class>Before accepting an outbound mailing, the Postal Service reviews it against those enrollment records.</td></tr><tr><td class="hl">705.24.5.2, first two sentences</td><td class>Outbound federal ballot mail is verified when presented for acceptance, and must be entered at a business-mail facility or a retail counter.</td></tr><tr><td class="hl">705.24.5.3(a), (b), and (c)</td><td class>Outbound mailings that do not comply will not be accepted and will be returned to the authorized ballot mailer. The mailer may request further review. The Postal Service assumes no responsibility until a mailing is accepted.</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><p class="chart-source">The September 4 injunction names these sections and the first two sentences of 705.24.5.2. It does not enjoin 705.24.5.3(d). Separate from the injunction, section 705.24.5 "does not apply to Return Federal Ballot Mail."<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-10" data-record-href="/records/talwani-pi-2026-09-04/" data-passage="DMM Section 705.24.5.3(a), (b), (c)" aria-label="Source 10: read the evidence">10</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-12" data-record-href="/records/usps-final-rule/" data-passage="does not apply to Return Federal Ballot Mail" aria-label="Source 12: read the evidence">12</a></sup></p></figure><figure class="quote-card" data-layer="fact"><figcaption class="qc-head"><span class="qc-when">September 6, 2026</span><span class="qc-sep">&middot;</span><span class="qc-where">Application 26A305</span></figcaption><blockquote class="qc-words"><p>Ballots have already begun to be mailed in North Carolina, and more States will begin the mailing process as the injunction remains in place.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-application/" data-passage="Ballots have already begun to be mailed in North Carolina" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup></p></blockquote><p class="qc-label">Solicitor General D. John Sauer</p></figure><figure class="quote-card" data-layer="fact"><figcaption class="qc-head"><span class="qc-when">September 4, 2026</span><span class="qc-sep">&middot;</span><span class="qc-where">Memorandum and order, District of Massachusetts</span></figcaption><blockquote class="qc-words"><p>... the court finds, on one side, that immediate implementation of the Final Rule issued less than seventy days before the November 2026 election threatens disenfranchisement of millions of United States citizens who seek to vote by mail.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-10" data-record-href="/records/talwani-pi-2026-09-04/" data-passage="the court finds, on one side," aria-label="Source 10: read the evidence">10</a></sup></p></blockquote><p class="qc-label">Judge Indira Talwani</p></figure><figure class="quote-card" data-layer="fact"><figcaption class="qc-head"><span class="qc-when">September 4, 2026</span><span class="qc-sep">&middot;</span><span class="qc-where">Memorandum and order, District of Massachusetts</span></figcaption><blockquote class="qc-words"><p>... fraudulent mail voting to support the rushed implementation of the Final Rule.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-10" data-record-href="/records/talwani-pi-2026-09-04/" data-passage="fraudulent mail voting to support the rushed implementation" aria-label="Source 10: read the evidence">10</a></sup></p></blockquote><p class="qc-label">Judge Indira Talwani</p></figure><figure class="quote-card" data-layer="fact"><figcaption class="qc-head"><span class="qc-when">August 24, 2026</span><span class="qc-sep">&middot;</span><span class="qc-where">Dissent in Trump v. California, 26A124</span></figcaption><blockquote class="qc-words"><p>It also lets another shoe drop in the Kafkaesque nightmare that our precedents have been steadily creating for certain plaintiffs who seek to bring election-related challenges.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-9" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a124-opinion/" data-passage="Kafkaesque" aria-label="Source 9: read the evidence">9</a></sup></p></blockquote><p class="qc-label">Justice Ketanji Brown Jackson</p></figure><figure class="quote-card" data-layer="fact"><figcaption class="qc-head"><span class="qc-when">September 8, 2026</span><span class="qc-sep">&middot;</span><span class="qc-where">Amicus brief of bipartisan current and former members of Congress, 26A305</span></figcaption><blockquote class="qc-words"><p>THE ORDER AND FINAL RULE USURP CONGRESS’S CONSTITUTIONAL ROLE AS THE ULTIMATE AUTHORITY ON FEDERAL-ELECTION ADMINISTRATION.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-29" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-congress-amicus/" data-passage="THE ORDER AND FINAL RULE USURP CONGRESS" aria-label="Source 29: read the evidence">29</a></sup></p></blockquote><p class="qc-label">Heading I</p></figure><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="scotus-26a305-application"><p>The Sunday application asks the Court to stay the September 4 injunction pending appeal to the First Circuit and any later petition, and it asks for an immediate administrative stay, a short pause while the justices decide the full request. Sauer writes that the rule imposes "modest envelope-design and addressee-information requirements", and that states remain responsible for who may vote by mail.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-application/" data-passage="addressee-information requirements" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="talwani-pi-2026-09-04"><p>The injunction names Domestic Mail Manual sections 705.24.3.1, 705.24.3.2, 705.24.4.1, 705.24.4.2, 705.24.5.1, the first two sentences of 705.24.5.2, and 705.24.5.3(a), (b), and (c). Those are the envelope standards, portal registration and enrollment, and the noncompliance rules. It does not bar states from complying voluntarily. It does not enjoin the rule for elections after November 3.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-10" data-record-href="/records/talwani-pi-2026-09-04/" data-passage="DMM Section 705.24.5.3(a), (b), (c)" aria-label="Source 10: read the evidence">10</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div></section><section id="the-catch"><aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact"><p class="tt-label">The catch</p><ol><li><span class="catch-tag">Told versus record</span><span class="catch-lead">The block is of the rule, not the order.</span>The Associated Press, PBS NewsHour, and CNBC wrote that Talwani extended an order against the Postal Service "enforcing President Donald Trump's executive order." CBS wrote "enforcing President Trump's executive order." The September 4 injunction names sections of the Postal Service rule issued August 21 and published August 26. The Court's August 24 stay had already paused her earlier injunction of the executive order itself. Why it matters: a reader of those dispatches can think the justices are being asked to revive the March order, when the fight before the Court is the Postal Service's published rule.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-15" data-record-href="/records/ap-2026-09-06/" data-passage="enforcing President Donald Trump" aria-label="Source 15: read the evidence">15</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-18" data-record-href="/records/cbs-2026-09-06/" data-passage="enforcing President Trump's executive order" aria-label="Source 18: read the evidence">18</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-10" data-record-href="/records/talwani-pi-2026-09-04/" data-passage="Ballot Mail for Federal Elections" aria-label="Source 10: read the evidence">10</a></sup><a class="catch-jump" href="#claim-check">Claim check</a></li><li><span class="catch-tag">Told versus record</span><span class="catch-lead">Citizenship lists are a different paper.</span>The Hill wrote that Sauer asked the justices to "permit the administration to bar the U.S. Postal Service from sending mail-in ballots to residents of states that have not submitted a list of eligible voters based on citizenship data." The Sunday application asks the Court to stay the September 4 injunction of the Postal Service rule. The rule's portal fields are name, address, two barcodes, and originating state. The citizenship list sits in section 2 of the executive order, a Homeland Security task, not in the Postal Service rule's enrollment form. Why it matters: the injunction the Court is being asked to lift reaches envelope standards, portal registration and enrollment, outbound verification, and the nonacceptance clauses, not a citizenship roll, and it is a Postal Service rule, not an order that the administration bar the Postal Service.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-20" data-record-href="/records/hill-2026-09-06/" data-passage="permit the administration to bar the U.S. Postal Service" aria-label="Source 20: read the evidence">20</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-11" data-record-href="/records/eo-14399/" data-passage="State Citizenship List" aria-label="Source 11: read the evidence">11</a></sup><a class="catch-jump" href="#claim-check">Claim check</a></li><li><span class="catch-tag">Left out</span><span class="catch-lead">Thursday's application is closed.</span>Coverage of Sunday's filing called it "another" appeal. The docket for 26A297, the Thursday filing, shows it withdrawn the same day 26A305 was docketed. Jackson's Sunday order is a response deadline of 4 p.m. on September 9. Why it matters: Sunday's application is 26A305; the Thursday application is not pending.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-4" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a297-docket/" data-passage="Application (26A297) withdrawn." aria-label="Source 4: read the evidence">4</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-2" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-docket/" data-passage="4 p.m. (EDT) on September 9, 2026." aria-label="Source 2: read the evidence">2</a></sup><a class="catch-jump" href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a></li><li><span class="catch-tag">Who pays</span><span class="catch-lead">Mail voters in the plaintiff states.</span>The September 4 order binds the Postal Service defendants for elections on or before November 3. It does not limit that command to the plaintiff states. Sauer told the Court North Carolina had already begun mailing, Alabama would on September 9, and at least five states the week of September 13. If the stay is granted, election offices sending non-UOCAVA outbound ballots would face the portal and envelope rules while some of those mailings are already moving. Why it matters: the next order lands on those offices and the voters waiting on outbound ballots, not only on voters in the states that sued, and not on UOCAVA ballots the rule excludes.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-application/" data-passage="Ballots have already begun to be mailed in North Carolina" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-10" data-record-href="/records/talwani-pi-2026-09-04/" data-passage="elections occurring before or on November 3" aria-label="Source 10: read the evidence">10</a></sup><a class="catch-jump" href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a></li></ol><p class="catch-minor">Smaller slips: The Associated Press Sunday story spells Biden as "Joe Bden." NBC's "federal database" line is treated under the outlet cards, where it checks out against the Privacy Act notice.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-15" data-record-href="/records/ap-2026-09-06/" data-passage="Joe Bden" aria-label="Source 15: read the evidence">15</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-19" data-record-href="/records/nbc-2026-09-06/" data-passage="not included in a federal database" aria-label="Source 19: read the evidence">19</a></sup></p></aside></section><section id="where-this-sits"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Three applications, one election date</p><h2 data-layer="fact">Where this sits</h2><figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact"><figcaption><strong>Days remaining to November 3, from each dated step</strong><span>Counted from the dates in the filings to the election named in 2 U.S.C. 7 and in the injunction.</span></figcaption><figure class="rate-chart"><svg viewBox="0 0 720 228" role="img" aria-label="Days from each dated step to November 3, 2026"><line x1="8" y1="192" x2="712" y2="192" stroke="#d8d4cc" stroke-width="1"></line><g><rect x="74.28571428571429" y="18" width="40" height="174" fill="#7a9cc4" rx="1"></rect><text x="94.28571428571429" y="14" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-note" font-size="9">+217</text><text x="94.28571428571429" y="216" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-axis" font-size="9">EO, Mar 31</text></g><g><rect x="180.57142857142858" y="132.66359447004606" width="40" height="59.33640552995392" fill="#7a9cc4" rx="1"></rect><text x="200.57142857142858" y="128.66359447004606" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-note" font-size="9">+74</text><text x="200.57142857142858" y="216" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-axis" font-size="9">Rule effective, Aug 21</text></g><g><rect x="286.8571428571429" y="136.67281105990781" width="40" height="55.32718894009217" fill="#7a9cc4" rx="1"></rect><text x="306.8571428571429" y="132.67281105990781" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-note" font-size="9">+69</text><text x="306.8571428571429" y="216" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-axis" font-size="9">Rule published, Aug 26</text></g><g><rect x="393.14285714285717" y="143.08755760368663" width="40" height="48.91244239631337" fill="#7a9cc4" rx="1"></rect><text x="413.14285714285717" y="139.08755760368663" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-note" font-size="9">+61</text><text x="413.14285714285717" y="216" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-axis" font-size="9">26A297, Sep 3</text></g><g><rect x="499.42857142857144" y="143.889400921659" width="40" height="48.11059907834102" fill="#7a9cc4" rx="1"></rect><text x="519.4285714285714" y="139.889400921659" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-note" font-size="9">+60</text><text x="519.4285714285714" y="216" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-axis" font-size="9">Injunction, Sep 4</text></g><g><rect x="605.7142857142858" y="145.4930875576037" width="40" height="46.50691244239631" fill="#c98a1b" rx="1"></rect><text x="625.7142857142858" y="141.4930875576037" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-note" font-size="9">+58</text><text x="625.7142857142858" y="216" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-axis" font-size="9">26A305, Sep 6</text></g></svg></figure><p class="chart-source">74 days run from the rule's August 21 effective date to November 3. 69 days run from August 26 publication to that election. Sunday's filing is 58 days before the election. September 19 is the UOCAVA statutory mailing date, 13 days after the filing; the rule excludes UOCAVA ballots, so that date is context, not a deadline under this rule.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-12" data-record-href="/records/usps-final-rule/" data-passage="Effective August 21, 2026" aria-label="Source 12: read the evidence">12</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-14" data-record-href="/records/uocava-52usc20302/" data-passage="the request is received at least 45 days before an election" aria-label="Source 14: read the evidence">14</a></sup></p><p class="chart-vintage" data-layer="proof">Day counts are calendar days between those dates at UTC midnight. 74 is November 3 minus August 21. 69 is November 3 minus August 26. 58 is November 3 minus September 6. 45 is November 3 minus September 19.</p></figure><figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact"><figcaption><strong>The three stay applications</strong><span>From the Supreme Court dockets and Sauer's withdrawal letter.</span></figcaption><figure class="data-table-wrap"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th class="hl">Docket</th><th class>Filed</th><th class>Relief asked</th><th class>What the Court did</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="hl">26A124</td><td class>July 27</td><td class>Stay the injunction of Executive Order 14399</td><td class>Granted August 24</td></tr><tr><td class="hl">26A297</td><td class>September 3</td><td class>Stay the August 27 temporary restraining order of the USPS rule</td><td class>Withdrawn September 6</td></tr><tr><td class="hl">26A305</td><td class>September 6</td><td class>Stay the September 4 preliminary injunction of the USPS rule</td><td class>Response due September 9, 4 p.m.</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><p class="chart-source">26A139, a companion application by Alabama and other states in the first round, was denied as moot when 26A124 was granted.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-8" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a124-docket/" data-passage="Application (26A124) granted by the Court." aria-label="Source 8: read the evidence">8</a></sup></p></figure><p data-layer="narrative">The Sunday application's caption lists 23 states, the District of Columbia, and Josh Shapiro, Governor of Pennsylvania, as plaintiffs. 12 states intervened on the government's side. Washington Attorney General Nick Brown's September 4 release said "25 states have secured a" preliminary injunction. That is the same coalition: Brown names 23 states, including Washington, plus the District of Columbia and Pennsylvania's governor. "25 states" is a loose label because the District of Columbia is not a state.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-application/" data-passage="Josh Shapiro, Governor of Pennsylvania" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-28" data-record-href="/records/brown-2026-09-04/" data-passage="25 states have secured a" aria-label="Source 28: read the evidence">28</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">August 24 was a win on procedure, not on the rule. The Court stayed an injunction of the executive order because, it said, the states had to speculate about a rule that had not issued. The Postal Service had already issued that rule and made it effective on August 21, 3 days before the opinion. The rule reached the Federal Register 2 days after the opinion, on August 26. Talwani then enjoined named sections of that rule. Sunday's application argues that the new injunction is "materially identical" to the August 27 temporary order and that the Postal Service is regulating mail, not elections.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-9" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a124-opinion/" data-passage="string of speculations" aria-label="Source 9: read the evidence">9</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-application/" data-passage="materially identical" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">The dates leave little room. The public record of this fight starts with the March 31 order. Sunday's stay request was filed 58 days before the election the injunction names, and 3 days before Jackson's response deadline. September 19 is the UOCAVA statutory mailing date, 13 days after the filing; the rule's "express exclusion of UOCAVA ballots" means that date is not a deadline this rule imposes.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-2" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-docket/" data-passage="4 p.m. (EDT) on September 9, 2026." aria-label="Source 2: read the evidence">2</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-12" data-record-href="/records/usps-final-rule/" data-passage="express exclusion of UOCAVA ballots" aria-label="Source 12: read the evidence">12</a></sup></p><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="scotus-26a305-application"><p>Plaintiff states named in the Sunday application: California, Massachusetts, Nevada, Washington, Arizona, Colorado, Connecticut, Delaware, Hawaii, Illinois, Maine, Maryland, Michigan, Minnesota, New Jersey, New Mexico, New York, North Carolina, Oregon, Rhode Island, Vermont, Virginia, Wisconsin. Also the District of Columbia; Josh Shapiro, Governor of Pennsylvania. Intervenors: Alabama, Florida, Indiana, Kansas, Louisiana, Missouri, Montana, Nebraska, Oklahoma, South Carolina, South Dakota, Texas. Organization plaintiffs: League of Women Voters of Massachusetts; League of Women Voters Lotte E. Scharfman Memorial Education Fund; League of Women Voters of the United States; League of Women Voters Education Fund; Association of Americans Resident Overseas; U.S. Vote Foundation; OCA-Asian Pacific American Advocates; Delta Sigma Theta Sorority, Inc..<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-application/" data-passage="League of Women Voters of Massachusetts" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div></section><section id="who-feels-it"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Election offices, outbound ballots, and the next few days</p><h2 data-layer="fact">Who feels it</h2><p data-layer="narrative">If you vote by mail this cycle, the injunction is what keeps the Postal Service from treating noncompliant outbound mailings presented by election offices as unaccepted mail for elections on or before November 3. Return ballots from voters are outside section 705.24.5. If the Court stays that injunction, offices sending non-UOCAVA outbound ballots would have to meet the envelope and portal rules while some of those mailings are already moving. Sauer's own line is that once envelopes "enter the mailstream, there is no retrieving them."<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-application/" data-passage="there is no retrieving them" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-12" data-record-href="/records/usps-final-rule/" data-passage="does not apply to Return Federal Ballot Mail" aria-label="Source 12: read the evidence">12</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative"><strong>What the whistleblower alleged.</strong> An anonymous USPS whistleblower, in a disclosure Senator Richard Blumenthal released on September 1, said the portal work was rushed and siloed, that the project parts "have not undergone meaningful testing," and that USPS adopted a "zero-percent failure threshold" under which one failed barcode could return an entire batch: "if a barcode scanner fails to correctly read one barcode in a batch, the entire batch is rejected and returned to the state." Those are allegations, not established facts.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-32" data-record-href="/records/blumenthal-whistleblower-2026-09-01/" data-passage="zero-percent failure threshold" aria-label="Source 32: read the evidence">32</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative"><strong>What the Postal Service said.</strong> On September 1 the Postal Service said it had "spent months developing a U.S. Federal Ballot Mail Portal," that the work "has at all times been conducted in a manner consistent with court orders," that it was "finalizing the portal" for officials who "voluntarily wish to familiarize themselves," and that "verification procedures prior to acceptance are not being performed."<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-33" data-record-href="/records/usps-portal-statement-2026-09-01/" data-passage="spent months developing a U.S. Federal Ballot Mail Portal" aria-label="Source 33: read the evidence">33</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative"><strong>What the court found.</strong> Talwani wrote that the "Portal is not currently operational" and that the "absence of an appeals or escalation process" supported the states' irreparable-harm claim. The compressed approval and enrollment process, she found, supported blocking mandatory use through November 3.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-10" data-record-href="/records/talwani-pi-2026-09-04/" data-passage="Portal is not currently operational" aria-label="Source 10: read the evidence">10</a></sup></p><ol class="dated-list" data-layer="fact"><li><strong>September 4:</strong> North Carolina, Sauer told the Court, had already begun mailing ballots. NPR, writing the day before, said the state had to start mailing absentee ballots on Friday.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-application/" data-passage="Ballots have already begun to be mailed in North Carolina" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-23" data-record-href="/records/npr-2026-09-03/" data-passage="North Carolina has to start mailing out its absentee ballots on Friday" aria-label="Source 23: read the evidence">23</a></sup></li><li><strong>September 9, 4 p.m. Eastern:</strong> Jackson's deadline for a response to 26A305. The same calendar day is the Alabama mailing date Sauer named.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-2" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-docket/" data-passage="4 p.m. (EDT) on September 9, 2026." aria-label="Source 2: read the evidence">2</a></sup></li><li><strong>Week of September 13:</strong> "at least five States," Sauer wrote, begin mailing.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-application/" data-passage="at least five States the week of September 13" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup></li><li><strong>November 3:</strong> federal election day, 58 days after the filing, 60 days after the injunction.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-13" data-record-href="/records/usc-2-7/" data-passage="Tuesday next after the 1st Monday in November" aria-label="Source 13: read the evidence">13</a></sup></li></ol><figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact"><figcaption><strong>Mailing dates Sauer named</strong><span>From the September 6 application, and the overseas-ballot date in the injunction record.</span></figcaption><figure class="data-table-wrap"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th class="hl">Who</th><th class>When</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="hl">North Carolina</td><td class>Already begun, as of the September 6 filing</td></tr><tr><td class="hl">Alabama</td><td class>September 9</td></tr><tr><td class="hl">At least five states</td><td class>Week of September 13</td></tr><tr><td class="hl">Uniformed and overseas voters (UOCAVA)</td><td class>September 19 is the statutory mailing date for a validly requested absentee ballot. The Postal Service rule excludes UOCAVA ballots; that date is not a deadline under this rule.</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><p class="chart-source">Sauer wrote that ballots had already begun to be mailed in North Carolina, with Alabama on September 9 and at least five states the week of September 13. The UOCAVA row is the statutory date; the rule's "express exclusion of UOCAVA ballots" keeps that date off the rule's calendar.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-application/" data-passage="at least five States the week of September 13" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-12" data-record-href="/records/usps-final-rule/" data-passage="express exclusion of UOCAVA ballots" aria-label="Source 12: read the evidence">12</a></sup></p></figure><p data-layer="narrative">Election offices that use the mail would have to reprint envelopes, load the portal, and certify designs if the stay is granted. Talwani wrote that the record includes "no evidence relating to" fraudulent mail voting to support rushing the rule. The injunction leaves voluntary compliance open.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-10" data-record-href="/records/talwani-pi-2026-09-04/" data-passage="no evidence relating to" aria-label="Source 10: read the evidence">10</a></sup></p></section><section id="outlets"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Checked against the filings</p><h2 data-layer="fact">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</h2><p data-layer="narrative">Sunday's story ran on the Associated Press wire and on PBS and CNBC in that form, on CBS with a shorter "President Trump" line, and in separate writes at NBC and The Hill. Thursday's application had its own Hill, CBS, and NPR stories. The errors below are the ones the filings contradict.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-15" data-record-href="/records/ap-2026-09-06/" data-passage="The appeal marks the third time" aria-label="Source 15: read the evidence">15</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-18" data-record-href="/records/cbs-2026-09-06/" data-passage="enforcing President Trump's executive order" aria-label="Source 18: read the evidence">18</a></sup></p><p data-layer="fact">Six outlet cards: three mislabeled, two that check out, one consistent.</p><div data-layer="fact"><div class="outlet-check warn"><p class="oc-head"><strong>The Hill</strong>, <span>wrote on Sunday that Sauer asked the justices to &ldquo;permit the administration to bar the U.S. Postal Service from sending mail-in ballots to residents of states that have not submitted a list of eligible voters based on citizenship data.&rdquo;</span></p><p class="oc-verdict"><span class="chip chip-warn">mislabeled</span> <span>The Sunday application asks the Court to stay the September 4 injunction of the Postal Service rule. The portal fields in the rule are name, address, barcodes, and originating state. The citizenship list is section 2 of the executive order. The 40-page count and the withdrawal of Thursday's application check out. Talwani's order covers elections on or before November 3; The Hill also wrote that she halted the plan 'while lower courts decided on the policy,' which is how long the injunction lasts during the case, not a claim about later elections.</span></p><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-20" data-record-href="/records/hill-2026-09-06/" data-passage="permit the administration to bar the U.S. Postal Service" aria-label="Source 20: read the evidence">20</a></sup></div><div class="outlet-check warn"><p class="oc-head"><strong>Associated Press</strong>, <span>wrote that Talwani extended an order against the Postal Service &ldquo;enforcing President Donald Trump's executive order.&rdquo; PBS NewsHour and CNBC carried the same dispatch.</span></p><p class="oc-verdict"><span class="chip chip-warn">mislabeled</span> <span>The Sunday filing asks a stay of the September 4 injunction of the Postal Service rule. The August 24 Supreme Court stay had already paused the injunction of the executive order. The third-time count, the Sauer quotes, and the North Carolina mailing line check out against 26A305.</span></p><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-15" data-record-href="/records/ap-2026-09-06/" data-passage="enforcing President Donald Trump" aria-label="Source 15: read the evidence">15</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-16" data-record-href="/records/pbs-2026-09-06/" data-passage="enforcing President Donald Trump" aria-label="Source 16: read the evidence">16</a></sup></div><div class="outlet-check warn"><p class="oc-head"><strong>CBS News</strong>, <span>wrote that Talwani extended an order against the Postal Service &ldquo;enforcing President Trump's executive order.&rdquo;</span></p><p class="oc-verdict"><span class="chip chip-warn">mislabeled</span> <span>Same mismatch as the Associated Press dispatch: the September 4 injunction names sections of the Postal Service rule, not the March executive order. CBS did not write 'Donald' in that line.</span></p><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-18" data-record-href="/records/cbs-2026-09-06/" data-passage="enforcing President Trump's executive order" aria-label="Source 18: read the evidence">18</a></sup></div><div class="outlet-check ok"><p class="oc-head"><strong>NBC News</strong>, <span>wrote that the rule would let the Postal Service &ldquo;potentially reject ballots that are sent to and from voters not included in a federal database.&rdquo;</span></p><p class="oc-verdict"><span class="chip chip-ok">checks out</span> <span>That wording checks out. USPS 820.225 is a federal Privacy Act system at Postal Service headquarters and a supplier cloud environment. It records names, addresses, issuing states, and barcodes, keeps them for five years, and states a law-enforcement purpose. State and local officials load the rows. It is not a citizenship file.</span></p><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-19" data-record-href="/records/nbc-2026-09-06/" data-passage="not included in a federal database" aria-label="Source 19: read the evidence">19</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-30" data-record-href="/records/usps-sorn-820-225/" data-passage="Records are retained for 5 years." aria-label="Source 30: read the evidence">30</a></sup></div><div class="outlet-check ok"><p class="oc-head"><strong>NPR</strong>, <span>wrote on September 3 that key parts of the USPS plan were blocked by a temporary restraining order, that voting by mail was still available to eligible voters, and that the August ruling was on a procedural question.</span></p><p class="oc-verdict"><span class="chip chip-ok">checks out</span> <span>Checks out against the August 27 order then in force, 26A124's ripeness holding, and the September 3 docket.</span></p><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-23" data-record-href="/records/npr-2026-09-03/" data-passage="currently blocked by a court order" aria-label="Source 23: read the evidence">23</a></sup></div><div class="outlet-check ok"><p class="oc-head"><strong>The Hill</strong>, <span>wrote on September 3 that the Court had lifted the judge's block on the executive order itself, and that she had now blocked the Postal Service's final plan.</span></p><p class="oc-verdict"><span class="chip chip-ok">consistent</span> <span>Consistent with 26A124 and the August 27 temporary order then before the Court.</span></p><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-21" data-record-href="/records/hill-2026-09-03/" data-passage="The high court previously lifted the judge" aria-label="Source 21: read the evidence">21</a></sup></div></div></section><section id="claim-check"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">What Sunday's application actually asks</p><h2 data-layer="fact">Claim checked against the record</h2><div data-layer="fact"><div class="contested-block"><p class="cb-label">The coverage&#39;s claim: the administration is asking the Court to let it enforce the March executive order for the midterms</p><p>Too broad. Application 26A305 asks a stay of the September 4 injunction of named sections of the Postal Service rule issued August 21 and published August 26. The March order's own injunction was stayed on August 24, and that opinion said the stay did not decide whether later implementing measures would be lawful. The injunction the Court is being asked to lift reaches mandatory outbound envelope standards and review; mandatory return-envelope standards and review; mandatory portal registration; mandatory voter enrollment; outbound verification; the first two sentences of the presentation rules; the nonacceptance, further-review, and no-responsibility clauses; and further preparatory steps to implement those sections.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-application/" data-passage="APPLICATION FOR A STAY OF THE INJUNCTION" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-10" data-record-href="/records/talwani-pi-2026-09-04/" data-passage="may not take any further preparatory steps to implement" aria-label="Source 10: read the evidence">10</a></sup></p></div></div><p data-layer="narrative">The claim readers brought is the one in the candidate headlines: a new block, and another appeal. Both are true as dates. The new block is the September 4 injunction. The earlier appeal that "another" points to is 26A297 on Thursday, and behind that 26A124 in July.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-4" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a297-docket/" data-passage="Application (26A297) withdrawn." aria-label="Source 4: read the evidence">4</a></sup></p><details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>how the day counts were made</summary><p>November 3, 2026, minus September 6 is 58 days. Minus September 4 is 60. Minus September 3 is 61. Minus August 26 (publication) is 69. Minus August 21 (effective date, rule line 73) is 74. Minus March 31 is 217. September 19 minus September 6 is 13. November 3 minus September 19 is 45, the statutory 45-day overseas and military ballot date when a valid request arrived in time; the rule excludes UOCAVA ballots. August 26 minus August 24 is 2. August 24 minus August 21 is 3. Plaintiff states were counted from the parties list in 26A305: 23 named states, plus the District of Columbia and the Pennsylvania governor, and 12 named intervenors.</p></details></section><section id="what-happened-next"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">After Sunday</p><h2 data-layer="fact">What happened next</h2><p data-layer="narrative">On September 8 the 26A305 docket added amicus briefs from bipartisan current and former members of Congress and from the Society for the Rule of Law. The Congress brief argues that the order and final rule usurp Congress's constitutional role as the ultimate authority on federal-election administration. Jackson's response deadline remains 4 p.m. on September 9.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-29" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-congress-amicus/" data-passage="THE ORDER AND FINAL RULE USURP CONGRESS" aria-label="Source 29: read the evidence">29</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-2" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-docket/" data-passage="Amicus brief of The Society for The Rule of Law submitted." aria-label="Source 2: read the evidence">2</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">Watching: the September 9 responses, any administrative stay, and whether Alabama mails on the date Sauer named.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-application/" data-passage="Alabama" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup></p></section><section id="unknowns"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions</p><h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2><ul data-layer="fact"><li>Whether respondents filed on September 9, and whether any administrative stay or full stay has issued. The docket we hold shows Jackson's 4 p.m. Eastern deadline, not a response or a stay order.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-2" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-docket/" data-passage="4 p.m. (EDT) on September 9, 2026." aria-label="Source 2: read the evidence">2</a></sup></li><li>The official First Circuit dockets in Nos. 26-2029 and 26-2031, including any September 7 filings or order. The Sunday application lists stay motions filed September 5 in those numbers. The Circuit's order is not in our records.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-application/" data-passage="No. 26-2031" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup></li><li>Whether the Postal Service produced the records Senator Blumenthal asked for by September 8. His letter set that date; we do not hold a production.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-32" data-record-href="/records/blumenthal-whistleblower-2026-09-01/" data-passage="by September 8, 2026" aria-label="Source 32: read the evidence">32</a></sup></li><li>Which five states Sauer meant by "the week of September 13" in the Sunday application. The Thursday application named Indiana, Florida, Michigan, Nevada, and Wisconsin among "at least 16 more States" if the temporary order were extended.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-application/" data-passage="the week of September 13" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-3" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a297-application/" data-passage="including Indiana, Florida," aria-label="Source 3: read the evidence">3</a></sup></li><li>How many mail ballots each plaintiff state has already sent. Sauer names North Carolina as having begun; the application does not give a count.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/scotus-26a305-application/" data-passage="Ballots have already begun to be mailed in North Carolina" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup></li></ul></section><section id="records" data-layer="fact"><p class="sec-kicker">Sources</p><h2>The records</h2><p class="section-lede">Everything this page rests on, numbered where the story cites it. We keep a dated copy of each one.</p><h3 class="src-group">Primary documents</h3><ol class="src-list" start="1"><li id="src-1" value="1"><a href="/records/scotus-26a305-application/">Application 26A305 for a stay of the September 4 injunction</a> · filed September 6, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; Sunday stay application, Sauer quotes, parties list, North Carolina mailing line</span></li><li id="src-2" value="2"><a href="/records/scotus-26a305-docket/">Supreme Court docket 26A305</a> · docketed September 6, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; docket number, Jackson&#39;s September 9 response deadline, September 8 amicus entries</span></li><li id="src-3" value="3"><a href="/records/scotus-26a297-application/">Application 26A297 for a stay of the August 27 temporary restraining order</a> · filed September 3, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; Thursday stay application against the temporary restraining order</span></li><li id="src-4" value="4"><a href="/records/scotus-26a297-docket/">Supreme Court docket 26A297</a> · docketed September 3, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; Thursday docket and the withdrawal entry</span></li><li id="src-5" value="5"><a href="/records/scotus-26a297-withdrawal/">Letter withdrawing application 26A297</a> · September 6, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; Sauer&#39;s letter withdrawing 26A297</span></li><li id="src-6" value="6"><a href="/records/scotus-26a297-admin-stay-opp/">Opposition to the 26A297 administrative stay</a> · September 3, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; plaintiff opposition to an immediate administrative stay of the temporary order</span></li><li id="src-7" value="7"><a href="/records/scotus-26a124-application/">Application 26A124 for a stay of the injunction of Executive Order 14399</a> · filed July 27, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; July stay application against the injunction of the executive order</span></li><li id="src-8" value="8"><a href="/records/scotus-26a124-docket/">Supreme Court docket 26A124</a> · docketed July 27, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the August 24 grant</span></li><li id="src-9" value="9"><a href="/records/scotus-26a124-opinion/">Per curiam opinion in Trump v. California, 26A124</a> · August 24, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the ripeness holding and the line that implementation is not thereby lawful</span></li><li id="src-10" value="10"><a href="/records/talwani-pi-2026-09-04/">Memorandum and order granting a preliminary injunction</a> · September 4, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the September 4 injunction, named DMM sections, November 3 limit</span></li><li id="src-11" value="11"><a href="/records/eo-14399/">Executive Order 14399</a> · signed March 31, published April 3, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the March 31 order, section 3 shall-not-transmit line, citizenship list</span></li><li id="src-12" value="12"><a href="/records/usps-final-rule/">Postal Service final rule, Ballot Mail for Federal Elections</a> · 91 Fed. Reg. 54,966, August 26, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the rule issued and effective August 21 and published August 26, including the outbound nonacceptance line, the UOCAVA exclusion, and the return-mail carve-out</span></li><li id="src-29" value="29"><a href="/records/scotus-26a305-congress-amicus/">Amicus brief of bipartisan current and former members of Congress</a> · filed September 8, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the Elections Clause argument in the Congress amicus brief</span></li><li id="src-30" value="30"><a href="/records/usps-sorn-820-225/">Privacy Act system of records notice, USPS 820.225 Federal Ballot Mail</a> · July 17, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the federal Privacy Act system for names, addresses, states, and barcodes, five-year retention, and the stated law-enforcement purpose</span></li><li id="src-31" value="31"><a href="/records/usps-sorn-response-2026-09-01/">Privacy Act response to comments on USPS 820.225</a> · September 1, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the September 1 notice that the system is effective with that publication</span></li><li id="src-32" value="32"><a href="/records/blumenthal-whistleblower-2026-09-01/">Blumenthal release on a USPS whistleblower disclosure about the ballot-mail portal</a> · September 1, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; whistleblower allegations about the portal, labeled as allegations, and the September 8 document request</span></li><li id="src-33" value="33"><a href="/records/usps-portal-statement-2026-09-01/">USPS statement on development of the U.S. Federal Ballot Mail Portal</a> · September 1, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the Postal Service response: months of development, consistent with court orders, finalizing for voluntary familiarization, no pre-acceptance verification</span></li></ol><h3 class="src-group">Official data</h3><ol class="src-list" start="13"><li id="src-13" value="13"><a href="/records/usc-2-7/">2 U.S.C. 7, election day</a> · saved September 8, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; federal election day</span></li><li id="src-14" value="14"><a href="/records/uocava-52usc20302/">52 U.S.C. 20302, overseas and military ballots</a> · saved September 8, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the 45-day overseas and military ballot deadline</span></li></ol><h3 class="src-group">Coverage checked</h3><ol class="src-list" start="15"><li id="src-15" value="15"><a href="/records/ap-2026-09-06/">Trump administration appeals mail voting decision to Supreme Court</a> · published September 6, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; Sunday AP dispatch checked against the filings</span></li><li id="src-16" value="16"><a href="/records/pbs-2026-09-06/">Trump administration makes another appeal to Supreme Court to allow mail ballot restrictions</a> · published September 6, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; PBS page of the Sunday AP dispatch</span></li><li id="src-17" value="17"><a href="/records/cnbc-2026-09-06/">Trump admin appeals again to Supreme Court to restrict mail-in ballots</a> · published September 6, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; CNBC page of the Sunday AP dispatch</span></li><li id="src-18" value="18"><a href="/records/cbs-2026-09-06/">Trump administration makes another Supreme Court appeal on mail ballot fight</a> · published September 6, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; CBS Sunday story</span></li><li id="src-19" value="19"><a href="/records/nbc-2026-09-06/">Trump administration asks Supreme Court to allow mail ballot restrictions to move forward for a third time</a> · published September 6, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; NBC Sunday write, including the federal-database line</span></li><li id="src-20" value="20"><a href="/records/hill-2026-09-06/">DOJ makes new appeal for Supreme Court to allow mail voting changes</a> · published September 6, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; Hill Sunday write, including citizenship-data and indefinitely halted</span></li><li id="src-21" value="21"><a href="/records/hill-2026-09-03/">Postal Service asks Supreme Court to allow Trump&#39;s mail voting changes</a> · published September 3, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; Hill Thursday write of 26A297</span></li><li id="src-22" value="22"><a href="/records/cbs-2026-09-03/">DOJ asks Supreme Court to allow Postal Service rules on mail ballots</a> · published September 3, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; CBS Thursday coverage</span></li><li id="src-23" value="23"><a href="/records/npr-2026-09-03/">What to know about mail-in voting and Trump&#39;s order</a> · published September 3, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; NPR explainer the day of the Thursday application</span></li><li id="src-24" value="24"><a href="/records/pbs-pi-2026-09-04/">Federal judge extends block on Trump order seeking to limit mail voting in the midterms</a> · published September 4, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; PBS on the September 4 injunction</span></li><li id="src-25" value="25"><a href="/records/abc-pi-2026-09-04/">Federal judge extends block on new USPS mail ballot rules</a> · published September 4, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; ABC on the September 4 injunction</span></li><li id="src-26" value="26"><a href="/records/ap-pi-2026-09-04/">Judge extends block on Trump order aiming to limit mail voting</a> · published September 4, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; AP on the September 4 injunction</span></li><li id="src-27" value="27"><a href="/records/nbc-pi-2026-09-04/">Judge again blocks Trump&#39;s effort to restrict mail voting</a> · published September 4, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; NBC on the September 4 injunction</span></li><li id="src-28" value="28"><a href="/records/brown-2026-09-04/">Washington AG Nick Brown release on the preliminary injunction</a> · published September 4, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the 25-states count, checked against the Sunday caption</span></li></ol><p class="sources-line"><a href="/methodology">How we check</a> <span data-layer="proof"> &middot; where this page says <em>computed</em>, the arithmetic was done from the saved data series; where it says <em>record</em> or <em>checked</em>, the words trace to a saved document</span>.</p></section><details class="record-ledger" data-record-ledger data-layer="fact" data-state-derived><summary>Figures and source passages <span class="series-count">39</span></summary><article class="ledger-row" data-state-slot="slot-91a18d3c-8ebf-4855-88ac-3d3ad1eed10c"><h3>Days filing to election</h3><strong class="ledger-value" data-state-figure="story-figure:70f80f4314b57dfec72e721222f825b01fa888d76df0f5bc4989dc2ce0769fb3" data-state-figure-role="current" data-figure-value="58" data-figure-unit="days">58 days</strong><div class="ledger-calculation" data-computed><p>Computed: November 3, 2026 minus September 6, 2026 = 58 days.</p><details class="ledger-source-passage"><summary>November 3, 2026 · U.S. District Court for the District of Massachusetts</summary><blockquote data-source-copy>elections occurring before or on November 3, 2026</blockquote><a href="/records/story-pin:bd273781aa767102e7a2f0e5fb14554c3064511f7ca07b240db9ec36f71c9384/">Read the source</a></details><details class="ledger-source-passage"><summary>September 6, 2026 · Supreme Court of the United States</summary><blockquote data-source-copy>September 6, 2026</blockquote><a href="/records/story-pin:0c14d0c996eac9302f2fa1f790b57aa88cd75551ec56eb2e7e7d09eb4070e8ab/">Read the source</a></details></div></article><article class="ledger-row" data-state-slot="slot-a1d3f90f-da28-451b-8668-c75c2de2cb2e"><h3>Days pi to election</h3><strong class="ledger-value" data-state-figure="story-figure:4c0ef2849b132eb85a118388e6b6f3392e2ec46a2ad292432a043bac52b8d351" data-state-figure-role="current" data-figure-value="60" data-figure-unit="days">60 days</strong><div class="ledger-calculation" data-computed><p>Computed: November 3, 2026 minus September 4, 2026 = 60 days.</p><details class="ledger-source-passage"><summary>November 3, 2026 · U.S. District Court for the District of Massachusetts</summary><blockquote data-source-copy>elections occurring before or on November 3, 2026</blockquote><a href="/records/story-pin:bd273781aa767102e7a2f0e5fb14554c3064511f7ca07b240db9ec36f71c9384/">Read the source</a></details><details class="ledger-source-passage"><summary>September 4, 2026 · U.S. District Court for the District of Massachusetts</summary><blockquote data-source-copy>September 4, 2026</blockquote><a href="/records/story-pin:bd273781aa767102e7a2f0e5fb14554c3064511f7ca07b240db9ec36f71c9384/">Read the source</a></details></div></article><article class="ledger-row" data-state-slot="slot-b0fe1eb9-6801-4c62-b3e3-213aa1bcad1a"><h3>Days 297 to election</h3><strong class="ledger-value" data-state-figure="story-figure:a67afc54c3208095d82adde2d828f569a387d90416ba8e152b50e466d4814870" data-state-figure-role="current" data-figure-value="61" data-figure-unit="days">61 days</strong><div class="ledger-calculation" data-computed><p>Computed: November 3, 2026 minus September 3, 2026 = 61 days.</p><details class="ledger-source-passage"><summary>November 3, 2026 · U.S. District Court for the District of Massachusetts</summary><blockquote data-source-copy>elections occurring before or on November 3, 2026</blockquote><a href="/records/story-pin:bd273781aa767102e7a2f0e5fb14554c3064511f7ca07b240db9ec36f71c9384/">Read the source</a></details><details class="ledger-source-passage"><summary>September 3, 2026 · Supreme Court of the United States</summary><blockquote data-source-copy>September 3, 2026</blockquote><a href="/records/story-pin:8c3f5e01d125c64f39b1f5d0079e6ef06a179807858d468665e7cc3a52b478bc/">Read the source</a></details></div></article><article class="ledger-row" data-state-slot="slot-d9f07be5-0e5d-4b6b-bcb1-7d578b45fecb"><h3>Days rule to election</h3><strong class="ledger-value" data-state-figure="story-figure:4e2bd5bbedd25511fd9b4af472730a91b9c6a054597871e2a561a0f63e12c7b2" data-state-figure-role="current" data-figure-value="69" data-figure-unit="days">69 days</strong><div class="ledger-calculation" data-computed><p>Computed: November 3, 2026 minus August 26, 2026 = 69 days.</p><details class="ledger-source-passage"><summary>November 3, 2026 · U.S. District Court for the District of Massachusetts</summary><blockquote data-source-copy>elections occurring before or on November 3, 2026</blockquote><a href="/records/story-pin:bd273781aa767102e7a2f0e5fb14554c3064511f7ca07b240db9ec36f71c9384/">Read the source</a></details><details class="ledger-source-passage"><summary>August 26, 2026 · United States Postal Service</summary><blockquote data-source-copy>Wednesday, August 26, 2026</blockquote><a href="/records/story-pin:78a6944d26561bddfd979798bf3ddd27b2330594118c9fdeea4142f5e6c10fbe/">Read the source</a></details></div></article><article class="ledger-row" data-state-slot="slot-b5010ada-f88b-4e99-a1fc-2204be781525"><h3>Days eo to election</h3><strong class="ledger-value" data-state-figure="story-figure:e0bf33e1dee21af59f323364b9587ccdfe824c18217f25ee46eb873da5a6b538" data-state-figure-role="current" data-figure-value="217" data-figure-unit="days">217 days</strong><div class="ledger-calculation" data-computed><p>Computed: November 3, 2026 minus March 31, 2026 = 217 days.</p><details class="ledger-source-passage"><summary>November 3, 2026 · U.S. District Court for the District of Massachusetts</summary><blockquote data-source-copy>elections occurring before or on November 3, 2026</blockquote><a href="/records/story-pin:bd273781aa767102e7a2f0e5fb14554c3064511f7ca07b240db9ec36f71c9384/">Read the source</a></details><details class="ledger-source-passage"><summary>March 31, 2026 · Executive Office of the President</summary><blockquote data-source-copy>Executive Order 14399 of March 31, 2026</blockquote><a href="/records/story-pin:a1f98307bd569918599841ca56b3be2a577b5e91b6ec073031dd906a0b907596/">Read the source</a></details></div></article><article class="ledger-row" data-state-slot="slot-1552b12d-63d5-4aac-bf13-72c1037df625"><h3>Days filing to uocava</h3><strong class="ledger-value" data-state-figure="story-figure:4da8089a711b23121db668b6b88ba8a8ac0813dbd158cbc552b400b1513ef5d7" data-state-figure-role="current" data-figure-value="13" data-figure-unit="days">13 days</strong><div class="ledger-calculation" data-computed><p>Computed: September 19, 2026 minus September 6, 2026 = 13 days.</p><details class="ledger-source-passage"><summary>September 19, 2026 · U.S. District Court for the District of Massachusetts</summary><blockquote data-source-copy>September 19, 2026 (45 days before the election)</blockquote><a href="/records/story-pin:bd273781aa767102e7a2f0e5fb14554c3064511f7ca07b240db9ec36f71c9384/">Read the source</a></details><details class="ledger-source-passage"><summary>September 6, 2026 · Supreme Court of the United States</summary><blockquote data-source-copy>September 6, 2026</blockquote><a href="/records/story-pin:0c14d0c996eac9302f2fa1f790b57aa88cd75551ec56eb2e7e7d09eb4070e8ab/">Read the source