src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:63:          <li>The same Sunday Sauer withdrew application 26A297, filed Thursday against that temporary order. The word "another" in the coverage is this third trip to the Court. Application 26A124, filed July 27, was granted August 24.<Cite s="scotus-26a297-withdrawal" passage="the United States withdraws its September 3 stay application" /><Cite s="scotus-26a124-docket" passage="Application (26A124) granted by the Court." /></li>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:86:        <p data-layer="narrative">A group of states had already sued over the executive order. On August 24 the Supreme Court granted application 26A124, staying the district court's injunction of that order. A stay is an order that pauses another court's order. The unsigned opinion said the states' challenge was not ripe because the Court did not yet know what a final rule would say. It added that the disposition "does not mean that any measure taken by the Government to implement the Order will necessarily be lawful."<Cite s="scotus-26a124-opinion" passage="any measure taken by the Government to implement" /></p>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:90:        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 4 Talwani issued a preliminary injunction. A preliminary injunction is a court order that forbids someone from doing something while a lawsuit continues. This one names specific Domestic Mail Manual sections and applies to elections on or before November 3. She dissolved the August 27 temporary order the same day. These holdings are preliminary findings for that order, not a final judgment on the merits.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="The Temporary Restraining Order" /></p>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:94:        <p data-layer="narrative">Talwani wrote under "Likelihood of Success on the Merits" that the plaintiffs had established a substantial likelihood of success on their Elections Clause claim. She found the rule is an election rule that regulates the time, place, and manner of elections, and that it is unconstitutional where Congress has not authorized such a rule under the Elections Clause.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="Likelihood of Success on the Merits" /></p>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:107:          <p class="chart-source">Sunday's application is the third stay request in this fight.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket" passage="No. 26A305" /></p>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:135:          <p>It also lets another shoe drop in the Kafkaesque nightmare that our precedents have been steadily creating for certain plaintiffs who seek to bring election-related challenges.<Cite s="scotus-26a124-opinion" passage="Kafkaesque" /></p>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:168:              Coverage of Sunday's filing called it "another" appeal. The docket for 26A297, the Thursday filing, shows it withdrawn the same day 26A305 was docketed. Jackson's Sunday order is a response deadline of 4 p.m. on September 9. Why it matters: Sunday's application is 26A305; the Thursday application is not pending.<Cite s="scotus-26a297-docket" passage="Application (26A297) withdrawn." /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket" passage="4 p.m. (EDT) on September 9, 2026." />
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:196:          <p class="chart-source">26A139, a companion application by Alabama and other states in the first round, was denied as moot when 26A124 was granted.<Cite s="scotus-26a124-docket" passage="Application (26A124) granted by the Court." /></p>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:240:        <p data-layer="narrative">Sunday's story ran on the Associated Press wire and on PBS and CNBC in that form, on CBS with a shorter "President Trump" line, and in separate writes at NBC and The Hill. Thursday's application had its own Hill, CBS, and NPR stories. The errors below are the ones the filings contradict.<Cite s="ap-2026-09-06" passage="The appeal marks the third time" /><Cite s="cbs-2026-09-06" passage="enforcing President Trump's executive order" /></p>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:248:          <OutletCheck outlet="NPR" claim={`wrote on September 3 that key parts of the USPS plan were blocked by a temporary restraining order, that voting by mail was still available to eligible voters, and that the August ruling was on a procedural question.`} verdict="Checks out against the August 27 order then in force, 26A124's ripeness holding, and the September 3 docket." chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="npr-2026-09-03" passage="currently blocked by a court order" /></OutletCheck>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:263:        <p data-layer="narrative">The claim readers brought is the one in the candidate headlines: a new block, and another appeal. Both are true as dates. The new block is the September 4 injunction. The earlier appeal that "another" points to is 26A297 on Thursday, and behind that 26A124 in July.<Cite s="scotus-26a297-docket" passage="Application (26A297) withdrawn." /></p>
