   327	
   328	replica explosives. Indeed, the Postal Service has also used that authority to adopt
   329	
   330	policies that specifically govern election-related mail, such as supporting the use of
   331	
   332	the Election Mail logo and prioritizing ballot mail for processing and delivery.
   333	
   334	      The Rule’s regulation of the U.S. Mail—not federal elections—is plainly con-
   335	
   336	stitutional. While the States have primary authority to regulate the manner of elec-
   337	                                           4
   338	
   339	
   340	tions, they cannot choose to use the federal mails to carry out their elections but then
   341	
   342	insist that their election-related mail is somehow exempt from the Postal Service’s
   343	
   344	rulemaking authority, conferred by Congress, to regulate envelope design and ad-
   345	
   346	dressee information. Neither the statutory nor constitutional provisions contain or
   347	
   348	compel any such carveout from this type of core regulation of use of the federal mails.
   349	
   350	      The district court’s preliminary-injunction opinion fails to seriously address,
   351	
   352	much less refute, any of these points. Instead, while devoting 18 pages to background,
   353	
   354	App., infra, 2a-19a, and another 15 pages to the equities, id. at 32a-46a, the court’s
   355	
   356	analysis of the merits is a scant seven pages, the substance of which can truly be
   357	
   358	reduced to three sentences: The Rule regulates elections rather than the mail be-
   359	
   360	cause it is election-specific; no statute explicitly authorizes USPS to adopt mandatory
   361	
   362	rules for the preparation of election mail; and thus the Rule usurps the authority of
   363	
   364	States and Congress to regulate federal elections. See id. at 25a-31a. The court pro-
   365	
   366	vided no response whatsoever to the fatal flaws in that simplistic rationale: (1) the
   367	
   368	plain text of the Postal Service’s rulemaking powers unambiguously authorizes the
   369	
   370	imposition of mandatory envelope-design and addressee-information requirements
   371	
   372	for specific categories of sensitive mail; (2) USPS has previously adopted these types
   373	
   374	of policies in the context of cremated remains and replica explosives; (3) there is no
   375	
   376	statutory or constitutional basis to distinguish the Postal Service’s existing election-
   377	
   378	specific policies merely because they are permissive rather than mandatory; and (4)
   379	
   380	there is no viable constitutional objection to the Rule if it falls within the statutory
   381	
   382	authority that Congress delegated to the Postal Service, because Congress unques-
   383	
   384	tionably has the power to regulate both the manner of federal elections and the use
   385	
   386	of the federal mails.
   387	                                           5
   388	
   389	
   390	      The district court’s baseless order also imposes serious irreparable harm on
   391	
   392	the federal government, the States themselves, and the voting public. Indeed, this
   393	
   394	Court has already recognized earlier in this case that “[t]he Government is likely to
   395	
   396	suffer irreparable harm without a stay” “[b]ecause reversal of the District Court’s
   397	
   398	judgment” against implementation of the final Rule “would come too late for the 2026
   399	
   400	midterms.” California, 2026 WL, 2473573, at *4. In particular, the injunction bars
   401	
   402	the Postal Service from verifying that mailed envelopes comply with the Rule’s re-
   403	
   404	quirements for the upcoming elections, see App., infra, 47a-48a, nullifying the Postal
   405	
   406	Service’s efforts to address the risk that the federal mails will be used to perpetrate
   407	
   408	voter fraud, 91 Fed. Reg. at 54,969—a particularly pernicious species of fraud that
   409	
   410	dilutes the votes of lawful voters, prevents election results from reflecting the will of
   411	
   412	the American people, and undermines “public confidence in the integrity of the elec-
   413	
   414	toral process,” Crawford v. Marion Cnty. Election Bd., 553 U.S. 181, 197 (2008).
   415	
   416	      Ballots have already begun to be mailed in North Carolina, and more States
   417	
   418	will begin the mailing process as the injunction remains in place—including Alabama
   419	
   420	on September 9 and at least five States the week of September 13. Once those ballot
   421	
   422	envelopes enter the mailstream, there is no retrieving them. And while the injunction
   423	
   424	allows the Postal Service and the States to continue taking steps to ensure that their
   425	
   426	envelopes satisfy the Rule’s requirements, see App., infra, 47a-48a, each day the in-
   427	
   428	junction is in place risks sowing confusion and chaos because the injunction makes
   429	
   430	those preparatory steps voluntary, rather than mandatory. Thus, if the injunction is
   431	
   432	eventually stayed and the rule takes effect for this year’s elections—as should occur—
   433	
   434	there is a grave risk that States may not have taken the necessary steps to comply,
   435	
   436	due to the false sense of latitude created by the court’s improper injunction, poten-
   437	                                            6
   438	
   439	
   440	tially depriving those States’ citizens of the ability to vote by mail. In addition, the
   441	
   442	injunction even bars the Postal Service from conducting the training necessary for
   443	
   444	the enjoined verification process for the upcoming elections, see ibid., which makes
   445	
   446	an immediate stay all the more essential.
   447	
   448	      Respondents cannot defeat a stay merely by asserting that they will be harmed
   449	
   450	by implementation of the Rule. Consistent with the principle that “the most critical”
   451	
   452	stay factors are whether the applicant has shown a likelihood of success on the merits
   453	
   454	and irreparable injury, Nken v. Holder, 556 U.S. 418, 434 (2009), this Court reiterated
   455	
   456	this past Friday that “balanc[ing] the equities” matters only “[i]n close cases,” Na-
   457	
   458	tional Republican Congressional Comm. v. Brown, No. 26A274, 2026 WL 2618462, at
   459	
   460	*1 (Sept. 4, 2026) (per curiam) (citing Hollingsworth v. Perry, 558 U.S. 183, 190 (2010)
   461	
   462	(per curiam)). This is not remotely a close case: As discussed above, the Court has
   463	
   464	already found that the government is suffering serious irreparable harm warranting
   465	
   466	a stay, and the Rule’s plain lawfulness is underscored by the district court’s inability
   467	
   468	to provide much more than ipse dixit to the contrary. In these circumstances, the
   469	
   470	valid Rule should not be enjoined for even a single day more based just on respond-
   471	
   472	ents’ complaints that compliance will harm them. That is especially so because re-
   473	
   474	spondents’ claims of harm are overblown. As for the suggestion that USPS is not
   475	
   476	prepared to perform its functions under the Rule, the district court relied on specula-
   477	
   478	tive fears, mischaracterizations of the record, and delays occasioned by its own im-
   479	
   480	proper orders. And as for the suggestion that the States are not prepared to satisfy
   481	
   482	their obligations under the Rule, the district court relied on ordinary compliance
   483	
   484	costs, self-serving assertions, and self-inflicted impediments.
   485	                                           7
   486	
   487	
   488	      More fundamentally, though, there is no “reverse Purcell” principle under
   489	
   490	which district courts are free to issue improper injunctions close to an election just
   491	
   492	because parties purportedly cannot comply with a lawful regulation in time for the
   493	
   494	election. That is especially so here, where there is no claim that the purported ina-
   495	
   496	bility to comply in time for the election itself renders the Rule unlawful. The district
   497	
   498	court made no such holding, and it could not have done so. Congress expressly ex-
   499	
   500	empted the Postal Service from arbitrary-and-capricious review under the Adminis-
   501	
   502	trative Procedure Act (APA), see 39 U.S.C. 410(a), which is the only conceivable basis
   503	
   504	for such a claim if the Rule is otherwise lawful for future elections where there is
   505	
   506	more time to comply.
   507	
   508	      Simply put, the best way to address respondents’ fears about inability to com-
   509	
   510	ply with the Rule is to grant an immediate administrative stay of the order, followed
   511	
   512	by a full stay pending further review. That will remove the improper cloud of uncer-
   513	
   514	tainty that the district court has cast over the Rule, making clear to all involved that
   515	
   516	compliance with the Rule is not optional and must start without further delay.
