    99	                   INTRODUCTION AND SUMMARY OF ARGUMENT
   100	
   101	             The United States Postal Service (USPS) promulgated a Final Rule imposing
   102	
   103	      modest envelope-design and data-reporting standards for federal ballot mail for States
   104	
   105	      that wish to transmit ballots via U.S. mail. See Ballot Mail for Federal Elections, 91 Fed.
   106	
   107	      Reg. 54,966 (Aug. 26, 2026). The Rule creates standardized design and review
   108	
   109	      requirements for ballot envelopes that build on practices long recommended by
   110	
   111	      USPS, some of which are already in use in many States (including many of the
   112	
   113	      plaintiffs here). In particular, the Rule requires that envelopes include the official
   114	
   115	      Election Mail logo, be automation compatible—so that they can be processed by
   116	
   117	      USPS’s high-speed mail processing equipment—and use unique barcodes. Id. at
   118	
   119	      54,990-91. And it requires States to upload the name-and-address information of
   120	
   121	      intended recipients of ballot mail—information that all election officials sending
   122	
   123	      ballot mail necessarily already possess—as well as the unique barcode number, into an
   124	
   125	      online portal. Id.
   126	
   127	             A group of States (California v. USPS, No. 26-2301) and organizations (League of
   128	
   129	      Women Voters (LWVM) v. Trump, No. 26-2029) challenged the Rule premised largely
   130	
   131	      on the theory—seemingly formed months before the Rule was issued—that USPS
   132	
   133	      intends to seize control of the administration of federal elections. It does not. The
   134	
   135	      Rule allows States—not USPS—to identify which voters should receive mail ballots.
   136	
   137	      USPS will not scrutinize those decisions, determine voter eligibility, ballot content,
   138	
   139	      mailing or receipt deadlines, or ballot-counting procedures. Compliance with the
   140	Case: 26-2029   Document: 00118503581         Page: 5     Date Filed: 09/05/2026     Entry ID: 6840966
   141	
   142	
   143	
   144	
   145	      Rule’s modest envelope design and informational requirements need not and should
   146	
   147	      not prevent a single voter from voting by mail.
   148	
   149	            Nevertheless, last week, the district court granted plaintiffs’ motion for a
   150	
   151	      temporary restraining order (TRO), which defendants sought to stay. The district
   152	
   153	      court has now issued a preliminary injunction premised on essentially the same
   154	
   155	      reasoning. California Dkt. 156; League of Women Voters Dkt. 285 (hereinafter “Op.”).
   156	
   157	            The court’s holding is gravely mistaken. The Rule’s improvements and
   158	
   159	      modernizations of USPS’s handling of this particularly sensitive category of mail are
   160	
   161	      fully consistent with USPS’s statutory and constitutional authority. USPS has
   162	
   163	      longstanding authority to “adopt . . . such rules and regulations . . . as may be
   164	
   165	      necessary in the execution of its functions,” 39 U.S.C. § 401(2), which include “the
   166	
   167	      collection, handling, transportation, delivery, forwarding, returning, and holding of
   168	
   169	      mail.” Id. § 404(a)(1). USPS has previously wielded that authority to issue design and
   170	
   171	      mail-preparation requirements for other types of mail that are sensitive yet non-
   172	
   173	      dangerous, such as cremated remains or replica explosives. And USPS has also
   174	
   175	      previously taken election mail-specific actions surrounding envelope design and
   176	
   177	      processing, such as recommending the use of the Election Mail logo and prioritizing
   178	
   179	      ballot mail for processing and delivery. There is no material distinction between those
   180	
   181	      prior actions and this one with respect to USPS’s legal authority.
   182	
   183	            A stay pending appeal is warranted. Under the district court’s order, while the
   184	
   185	      government may continue to review envelope design and facilitate use of the online
   186	                                                  2
   187	Case: 26-2029   Document: 00118503581           Page: 6   Date Filed: 09/05/2026     Entry ID: 6840966
   188	
   189	
   190	
   191	
   192	      portal by States that wish to use those processes, it may not implement the Rule’s
   193	
   194	      verification process even for States that would voluntarily participate. The
   195	
   196	      government suffers irreparable harm; the injunction threatens USPS’s ability to
   197	
   198	      implement the Rule’s beneficial practices for upcoming ballot mail, even if it is later
   199	
   200	      stayed or vacated. These harms accrue with each passing day and cannot be remedied
   201	
   202	      by appellate review in the ordinary course, which would “come far too late.”
   203	
   204	      California, 2026 WL 2473573, at *4. Because the “most critical” stay factors favor the
   205	
   206	      defendants, Nken v. Holder, 556 U.S. 418, 434 (2009), plaintiffs’ asserted harms from
   207	
   208	      the Rule (even if credited) cannot be sufficient to defeat a stay of an improper
   209	
   210	      injunction of a lawful rule. This Court should grant an immediate administrative stay
   211	
   212	      and a full stay pending further review.
