   214	                                          STATEMENT
   215	
   216	      I.    Regulatory Background
   217	
   218	            Congress has empowered USPS to “adopt . . . such rules and regulations . . . as
   219	
   220	      may be necessary in the execution of its functions under this title and such other
   221	
   222	      functions as may be assigned to [USPS] under any provisions of law outside of this
   223	
   224	      title.” 39 U.S.C. § 401(2). Congress also provided USPS “all other powers incidental,
   225	
   226	      necessary, or appropriate to the carrying on of its functions or the exercise of its
   227	
   228	      specific powers.” Id. § 401(10). One of those specific powers is “to provide for the
   229	
   230	      collection, handling, transportation, delivery, forwarding, returning, and holding of
   231	
   232	      mail, and for the disposition of undeliverable mail.” Id. § 404(a)(1).
   233	                                                   3
   234	Case: 26-2029    Document: 00118503581        Page: 7      Date Filed: 09/05/2026     Entry ID: 6840966
   235	
   236	
   237	
   238	
   239	             Exercising this delegated authority, the Final Rule amends USPS’s Domestic
   240	
   241	      Mail Manual to impose modest reporting and design requirements on federal ballot
   242	
   243	      mail envelopes. The Rule requires federal ballots transmitted through the U.S. mail to
   244	
   245	      be mailed in envelopes that include the official Election Mail logo, are automation
   246	
   247	      compatible, and use unique barcodes. See 91 Fed. Reg. at 54,990-91. When outbound
   248	
   249	      federal ballot mail is presented for mailing, USPS will match the sampled barcode data
   250	
   251	      to the portal data, to ensure compliance with the Rule. Id. at 54,990-91. All mailings
   252	
   253	      that comply with these requirements will be accepted; those that do not will be
   254	
   255	      “returned to the authorized ballot mailer” who may then “address the error(s)” and
   256	
   257	      “resubmit[] the mailing.” Id. at 54,991.
   258	
   259	             USPS will not perform any verification of voter eligibility; its role is limited to
   260	
   261	      ensuring that envelopes comply with the Rule’s design requirements. As the Rule
   262	
   263	      emphasizes, “[e]ligible voters will therefore be listed [in the Portal] by and at the
   264	
   265	      discretion of election officials, and USPS will not second-guess the states’
   266	
   267	      submissions. State election officials will thus retain full control over who is permitted
   268	
   269	      to vote in federal elections in their state by U.S. mail.” 91 Fed. Reg. at 54,969. States
   270	
   271	      likewise need not worry about the accuracy of lists maintained within USPS—all lists
   272	
   273	      will consist solely of information provided by the States alone. On or near the date of
   274	
   275	      the election, USPS will also provide each State that uses the U.S. mail to transport
   276	
   277	      ballots with a single list containing the State’s submissions of the ballots it intends to
   278	
   279	      send. Id. at 54,982.
   280	                                                   4
   281	Case: 26-2029    Document: 00118503581         Page: 8     Date Filed: 09/05/2026       Entry ID: 6840966
   282	
   283	
   284	
   285	
   286	             Several features of this Rule are not new. For years, USPS has recommended
   287	
   288	      that ballot mail envelopes use unique barcodes, be automation compatible, include the
   289	
   290	      official Election Mail logo, and undergo mailpiece design review in advance of
   291	
   292	      mailing. See USPS, Kit 600: Official Election Mail Guide (Kit 600),
   293	
   294	      https://about.usps.com/kits/kit600.pdf; Domestic Mail Manual 703.8.0,
   295	
   296	      https://pe.usps.com/text/dmm300/703.htm#ep1174014. This Rule simply turns
   297	
   298	      “these longstanding recommendations”—which some of the State plaintiffs have
   299	
   300	      already adopted, at least in part—into requirements. 91 Fed. Reg. at 54,986; see id. at
   301	
   302	      54,978. These requirements are only a baseline; “nothing in the [R]ule prevents or
   303	
   304	      discourages election officials from including envelope design elements required by
   305	
   306	      state law,” nor will the Rule “create a single, uniform ballot envelope design across all
   307	
   308	      states[.]” Id. at 54,978. And most importantly, the Rule “leav[es] the administration
   309	
   310	      of elections firmly in the hands of the [S]tates.” Id. at 54,970. USPS “will not play
   311	
   312	      any role in determining voter eligibility, maintaining voter rolls, or counting ballots.”
   313	
   314	      Id. at 54,978.
   315	
   316	             The Rule explains that these requirements “will afford significantly enhanced
   317	
   318	      visibility into the sending of Federal Ballot Mail, and this will in turn facilitate law
   319	
   320	      enforcement efforts by enabling law enforcement to better detect potential issues
   321	
   322	      meriting further investigation.” Id. at 54,969. Ultimately, “[w]hether or not voter
   323	
   324	      fraud is common or uncommon,” the Rule is intended “to facilitate enforcement of
   325	
   326	      federal law, reduce the risk of fraud, and help protect the integrity of federal
   327	                                                    5
   328	Case: 26-2029   Document: 00118503581        Page: 9        Date Filed: 09/05/2026   Entry ID: 6840966
   329	
   330	
   331	
   332	
   333	      elections,” id.—at least with respect to the narrow but important role played by USPS
   334	
   335	      in States that choose to send ballots by U.S. mail.
   336	
   337	      II.   Factual and Procedural Background
   338	
   339	            These suits began as challenges to Executive Order 14,399, 91 Fed. Reg. 17,125
   340	
   341	      (Mar. 31, 2026), which, among other things, directed USPS to “‘initiate a proposed
   342	
   343	      rulemaking’” related to election mail, Trump v. California, No. 26A124, 2026 WL
   344	
   345	      2473573, at *1 (U.S. Aug. 24, 2026). The district court previously enjoined
   346	
   347	      implementation of that aspect of the Executive Order with respect to the upcoming
   348	
   349	      November 2026 election. On August 24, 2026, the Supreme Court stayed that
   350	
   351	      injunction. Id.
   352	
   353	            On August 26, 2026, USPS published the Final Rule in the Federal Register.
   354	
   355	      That same day, both the LWVM plaintiffs and the California plaintiffs sought to
   356	
   357	      challenge the Rule. On August 27, the district court granted plaintiffs’ request for a
   358	
   359	      TRO, and the federal defendants appealed and sought a stay. While those stay
   360	
   361	      motions remained pending, on September 4, 2026, the district court converted the
   362	
   363	      TRO into a preliminary injunction, relying on substantially the same reasoning.
   364	
   365	            Like the TRO, the preliminary injunction permits States’ voluntary participation
   366	
   367	      in the Rule’s envelope design review process and use of USPS’s online portal but
   368	
   369	      prohibits the government from verifying that ballot envelopes comply with the Rule’s
   370	
   371	      requirements before accepting them for mailing. Under the order, “[f]or elections
   372	
   373	      occurring before or on November 3, 2026,” the defendants “may not implement in
   374	                                                  6
   375	Case: 26-2029      Document: 00118503581      Page: 10      Date Filed: 09/05/2026     Entry ID: 6840966
   376	
   377	
   378	
   379	
   380	       any way and may not take any further preparatory steps to implement” the enjoined
   381	
   382	       provisions. Op. 48. The district court also denied the federal defendants’ request for
   383	
   384	       a stay pending appeal. Op. 46-47.
   385	
   386	                                            ARGUMENT
   387	
   388	              A stay pending appeal is warranted because the government is likely to succeed
   389	
   390	       on the merits, will face irreparable injury, and the equities support a stay. See Nken v.
   391	
   392	       Holder, 556 U.S. 418, 426 (2009).
   393	
   394	       I.     The Government Is Likely to Succeed on the Merits Because the Final
   395	              Rule Is Lawful
   396	              The Final Rule is a lawful exercise of USPS’s authority to regulate the mail. It
   397	
   398	       imposes only modest requirements on the use of the mail and does not regulate state
   399	
   400	       administration of elections. That reality wholly undermines the district court’s order,
   401	
   402	       which found summarily that USPS lacked statutory authority to issue the Rule and
   403	
   404	       that the Rule is unconstitutional.
   405	
   406	              A.     The Final Rule is authorized by statute.
   407	
   408	              1. Plaintiffs’ claims that the Rule is contrary to statute are subject to narrowly
   409	
   410	       circumscribed judicial review. Congress has generally exempted USPS from
   411	
   412	       Administrative Procedure Act (APA) review. See 39 U.S.C. § 410(a). In light of this
   413	
   414	       preclusion of review, plaintiffs must satisfy the ultra-vires review standards under
   415	
   416	       Leedom v. Kyne, 358 U.S. 184, 188 (1958), to prevail on their statutory claims. Mittleman
   417	
   418	       v. Postal Regul. Comm’n, 757 F.3d 300, 305, 307 (D.C. Cir. 2014). To succeed on a
   419	
   420	
   421	                                                    7
   422	Case: 26-2029    Document: 00118503581          Page: 11     Date Filed: 09/05/2026       Entry ID: 6840966
   423	
   424	
   425	
   426	
   427	       Leedom ultra vires claim—essentially a “Hail Mary pass”—a plaintiff must demonstrate
   428	
   429	       that “an agency has taken action entirely in excess of its delegated powers and
   430	
   431	       contrary to a specific prohibition in a statute.” NRC v. Texas, 605 US. 665, 681 (2025)
   432	
   433	       (emphasis and quotation marks omitted). Finding that “an agency has arguably
   434	
   435	       reached a conclusion which does not comport with the law” is not sufficient. Id.
   436	
   437	       (quotation marks omitted).
   438	
   439	              The district court held that the Leedom standard is inapplicable unless Congress
   440	
   441	       “has provided a reticulated judicial review scheme.” Op. 28 n.32 (quoting Board of
   442	
   443	       Governors of the Fed. Rsrv. Sys. v. MCorp Fin., Inc., 502 U.S. 32, 43 (1991)). That is
   444	
