    43	ORDER OF COURT
    44	Entered: July 25, 2026
    45	Under the Constitution, state and local officials are responsible for administering federal
    46	elections. In the spring of 2026, President Trump issued an executive order with nationwide effects
    47	on how state and local officials can administer federal elections, including the upcoming primary
    48	and general elections in September and November. In particular, the executive order directed
    49	substantial involvement by the United States Postal Service in deciding which ballots sent to and
    50	from voters would be delivered. The plaintiffs in this case -- 23 states and the District of Columbia
    51	(the "Plaintiff States") -- filed this lawsuit against the President, federal agencies, and agency heads,
    52	challenging the executive order as ultra vires and unconstitutional. The district court entered final
    53	judgment in favor of the Plaintiff States as to some of their claims and enjoined most of the
    54	defendants (but not the President) from implementing certain provisions of the executive order,
    55	but only as to federal elections on or before November 3, 2026, and only in the Plaintiff States.
    56	The federal defendants and 12 states that intervened in the case in support of the executive order
    57	have now filed separate motions seeking a stay of the district court's injunction pending appeal,
    58	arguing only that the Plaintiff States lack standing to bring their claims and that those claims are
    59	not ripe. We deny their motions.
    60	I. BACKGROUND
    61	On March 31, 2026, President Trump issued Executive Order 14399: "Ensuring
    62	Citizenship Verification and Integrity in Federal Elections" (the "EO"). 91 Fed. Reg. 17125
    63	(Mar. 31, 2026). The EO targets voting by mail. In Section 1, titled "Purpose and Policy," the EO
    64	declares that "[t]o enhance election integrity via the United States Mail, additional measures are
    65	necessary." Id. at 17125. It then directs federal agencies and officials to take certain actions at
    66	specific times in the months leading up to "regularly scheduled Federal election[s]," including the
    67	November 3, 2026 election. Id.
    68	This lawsuit focuses on Sections 2, 3, and 5 of the EO. Section 2 is titled "Establishment
    69	and Transmission of State Citizenship Lists and Prioritization of Investigations and Prosecutions
    70	
    71	-2-
    72	
    73	Related to Election Fraud." Id. Section 2(a) directs the Department of Homeland Security (DHS),
    74	along with other agencies, to compile lists of "individuals confirmed to be United States citizens
    75	who will be above the age of 18 at the time of an upcoming Federal election and who maintain a
    76	residence in the subject State" ("DHS Citizenship Lists"). Id. Under Section 2(a), federal officials
    77	"shall . . . transmit" the DHS Citizenship Lists to state election officials "no fewer than 60 days
    78	before each regularly scheduled Federal election," meaning by September 4, 2026, for the
    79	November 3, 2026 general election. Id. Also, DHS "shall establish procedures" to permit
    80	individuals to "access" and "update" their records and states "to routinely supplement and provide
    81	suggested modifications or amendments" to these lists. Id. Section 2(b) orders the Attorney
    82	General to "prioritize the investigation and . . . prosecution" of two groups of individuals and
    83	entities: (1) "State and local officials or any others involved in" federal election administration
    84	who issue ballots to individuals not eligible to vote in federal elections and (2) "individuals and
    85	public or private entities engaged in . . . the printing, production, shipment, or distribution of
    86	ballots to individuals" not eligible to vote in federal elections. Id. at 17126.
    87	Section 3 of the EO is titled "United States Postal Service Rulemaking on Mail-In and
    88	Absentee Ballots," and focuses on the United States Postal Service (USPS). Section 3(b) directs
    89	the Postmaster General to "initiate a proposed rulemaking" within 60 days of the EO's issuance to
    90	"protect the integrity of the mail" in federal elections. Id. The notice of proposed rulemaking
    91	"shall include, at minimum," various requirements for the design of envelopes used by state and
    92	local officials to enable voting by mail, including specific markings and unique barcodes for all
    93	such envelopes.1 Id. Section 3(b) then requires that the proposed rule provide that "all outbound
    94	ballot mail" sent by states and localities to voters "must be mailed" in such envelopes and that
    95	USPS "shall not transmit mail-in or absentee ballots from any individual" voter unless the
    96	individual's name appears on a state-specific list. Id. Finally, Section 3(d) directs that "[a]ny final
    97	rule" adopted pursuant to Section 3 "shall be issued no later than 120 days from the date of" the
    98	EO, meaning by July 29, 2026. Id. at 17127.
    99	Section 5, titled "Enforcement," orders the Attorney General and agency heads to "take all
   100	lawful steps to deter and address noncompliance with Federal law" by the two groups targeted by
   101	the order: "State or local election officials" and "public or private entities engaged in" ballot
   102	printing or distribution. Id. For example, it directs federal officials to "withhold[] Federal funds
   103	from noncompliant States and localities where such withholding is authorized by law." Id. It
   104	invites referral to the Department of Justice of "[e]vidence of violations" by these two groups for
   105	"consideration of investigation or charges." Id. Section 5 also requires states and localities to
   106	"preserve, for a 5-year period, all records and materials" related to voter participation in federal
   107	elections. Id.
   108	In early April, the Plaintiff States brought this action against the President and the various
   109	agencies and agency heads identified in the EO. In their amended complaint, they challenged
   110	Sections 2, 3, and 5 of the EO as ultra vires and unconstitutional, in violation of the separation of
