   127	Plaintiff States' claims as to the November 2026 elections were ripe for review because the EO
   128	created a "direct and immediate dilemma" for the Plaintiff States. (Quoting Stern v. U.S. Dist. Ct.
   129	for the Dist. of Mass., 214 F.3d 4, 10 (1st Cir. 2000) (citation modified).)
   130	One week later, the district court granted the Plaintiff States' motion for summary judgment.
   131	It concluded, given the undisputed facts in the summary judgment record, that the Plaintiff States
   132	had standing to sue based on both current and certainly impending injuries. Among these injuries,
   133	the court explained, were planning and compliance costs and the threat of criminal prosecution.
   134	To start, the district court determined that the Plaintiff States are "experiencing injury now"
   135	because the EO disrupted their ongoing efforts to administer multiple elections in the coming
   136	months. As the court pointed out, it was uncontroverted that, at the time they filed their lawsuit,
   137	the Plaintiff States were "actively administering or preparing to administer federal
   138	primaries" -- some of which are scheduled for as early as September 2026 -- and "preparing to
   139	administer the November 2026 general election." And, by the time they filed their summary
   140	judgment motion, certain Plaintiff States "already had to pull elections staff from critical projects
   141	required by statute" to "develop a compliance plan for the EO," given that the EO directs multiple
   142	changes to the voting-by-mail process. The court also determined that, both for legal and practical
   143	reasons, the Plaintiff States could not delay these planning and compliance efforts. For example,
   144	in several of the Plaintiff States, statutes require officials to provide mail-in ballots to individuals
   145	eligible to vote by mail weeks in advance, and thus they "must plan to comply with the EO's
   146	directives on mail ballots" now. These state statutory obligations were "undisputed" by the
   147	Government. And as a practical matter, the court relied on record evidence that delays in planning
   148	would increase the "financial strain" on state election offices and threaten disenfranchisement of
   149	some of the Plaintiff States' citizens.
   150	2
   151	
   152	Because the federal defendants and the intervenor states raise largely overlapping
   153	arguments in their stay motions, we discuss those arguments together. The intervenor states do
   154	press a separate argument as well, which we identify and analyze below.
   155	3
   156	
   157	In addition to the lawsuit by the Plaintiff States giving rise to this appeal, eight nonprofit
   158	organizations filed a separate lawsuit also challenging the EO. The district court consolidated the
   159	two cases pursuant to Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 42(a) but only for limited purposes. Its
   160	initial ruling on prudential ripeness applied to both cases. Its subsequent summary judgment ruling
   161	now on appeal to us, however, applied only to the Plaintiff States' claims.
   162	-4-
   163	
   164	Relatedly, the district court determined that the Plaintiff States faced certainly impending
   165	compliance costs from the EO's directives. For example, it noted that "[n]early half of [the]
   166	Plaintiff States have already purchased mail ballot envelopes for the 2026 federal election cycle
   167	that will not be in compliance with the requirements" listed in the EO, assuring that they will incur
   168	monetary costs from any change required by USPS. And the states would have no choice but to
   169	purchase new envelopes: The EO directs USPS to propose a rule that it will not "transmit"
   170	non-compliant mail-ballot envelopes. See 91 Fed. Reg. at 17125-26. Thus, it was "undisputed"
   171	by the Government that, absent an injunction, the Plaintiff States "will incur compliance costs" in
   172	the coming weeks or months.
   173	Finally, the district court concluded that the Plaintiff States faced a credible threat of
   174	criminal prosecution under the EO. Reading the EO as a whole, the court determined that multiple
   175	sections of the EO "implicitly threaten[ed] enforcement" of federal criminal statutes against state
   176	and local officials who, for example, furnished ballots to voters who are not included on the DHS
   177	Citizenship Lists. And, the court pointed out, the Government conceded that any list it compiled
   178	under Section 2(a) would "necessarily be incomplete," based on the limitations of its own records,
   179	meaning that some citizens who were eligible to vote in a particular state nevertheless would be
   180	missing from the list. Reading the EO based on those undisputed facts, the court reasoned that the
   181	threat of criminal enforcement was "sufficiently imminent" as to the 2026 elections to create
   182	Article III injury. Thus, the court determined that the Plaintiff States had standing to challenge the
   183	EO.
   184	The district court then turned to the merits. It ultimately concluded that the President
   185	lacked constitutional authority to order changes to how states and localities administer federal
   186	elections. The federal defendants and the intervenor states then filed separate notices of appeal
   187	and motions to stay in the district court. The court denied the stay motions.
   188	On July 7, 2026, the district court entered its final judgment.4 It declared that Sections 2
   189	and 3 are "ultra vires and violate the separation of powers." Then, it enjoined the federal
   190	defendants, not including the President, from "implementing, giving effect to, or enforcing"
   191	Sections 2 and 3 of the EO "as to [the] Plaintiff States with respect to the November 3, 2026 or
   192	any earlier federal election." Notably, the court did not enjoin the federal defendants from taking
   193	any actions under Sections 2 and 3 in states other than the Plaintiff States for federal elections on
   194	or before November 3, 2026. Nor did it enjoin the federal defendants from taking such actions in
   195	any state for federal elections after November 3, 2026. Finally, the court dismissed with prejudice
   196	the Plaintiff States' challenge to the recordkeeping portion of Section 5 based on its ruling that this
   197	portion of the EO was merely "precatory."
   198	
   199	4
   200	
   201	Although the district court entered final judgment after the notices of appeal were filed,
   202	we have appellate jurisdiction under Federal Rule of Appellate Procedure 4(a)(2) because the
   203	district court's order "announce[d] a decision purporting to dispose of all of [the Plaintiff States']
   204	claims." FirsTier Mortg. Co. v. Invs. Mortg. Ins., 498 U.S. 269, 277 (1991); see Fed. R. App. P.
   205	4(a)(2) ("A notice of appeal filed after the court announces a decision or order -- but before the
   206	entry of the judgment or order -- is treated as filed on the date of and after the entry.").
   207	-5-
   208	
   209	That same day, the federal defendants filed this emergency motion to stay the district
   210	court's injunction pending appeal. The separate stay motion of the intervenor states followed.
   211	II. LEGAL STANDARD
   212	"'A stay is an intrusion into the ordinary processes of administration and judicial review'
   213	and is not granted as 'a matter of right.'" Washington v. HUD, 171 F.4th 473, 488 (1st Cir. 2026)
   214	(quoting Nken v. Holder, 556 U.S. 418, 427 (2009)). In seeking a stay, the Government bears the
   215	burden of showing that it is "entitled to this 'extraordinary remedy' under Nken's familiar
   216	four-factor test." Id. (quoting Nken, 556 U.S. at 437 (Kennedy, J., concurring)).
   217	Under the Nken test, the Government must (1) make "a strong showing" that it is likely to
   218	succeed on the merits of its appeal and also show that (2) it "will be irreparably injured absent a
   219	stay"; (3) the issuance of the stay will not "substantially injure the other parties interested in the
   220	proceeding"; and (4) the public interest weighs in favor of a stay. Id. (quoting Nken, 556 U.S. at
   221	434). The first two factors are the "most critical." Id. (quoting Nken, 556 U.S. at 434). When
   222	assessing the fourth factor, "we consider the interests of the public in general," including American
   223	voters, "not just the interests of the parties." Id.
   224	Because "addressing interlocutory motions on a tight timeline is not always optimal for
   225	orderly judicial decisionmaking," in this posture we are "especially . . . rel[iant] on the parties to
   226	frame the issues for decision." New York v. Trump, 133 F.4th 51, 66 (1st Cir. 2025) (citation
   227	modified).
   228	III. DISCUSSION
   229	Before analyzing the Nken factors, we set out the scope of the issues presented to us in the
   230	stay motions. Although the district court held that the EO is unconstitutional, that question is not
   231	presently before us because the Government does not defend the legality of the EO in requesting
   232	a stay. Instead, it argues only that the Plaintiff States do not have standing to challenge the EO
   233	because their claims are premature. And its standing and ripeness arguments focus on a single
   234	claim: that the EO was not injuring the Plaintiff States at the time they filed their complaint, and
   235	that it would not injure them soon, because the federal defendants were still implementing it.
   236	The Government also does not contest the district court's assessment of the summary
   237	judgment record that informed its final ruling.5 Before the district court, the Government did not
   238	dispute the declarations and other factual submissions by the Plaintiff States. And in its request
   239	for a stay pending appeal, it contests only the legal implications of those undisputed facts.
   240	Finally, the Government has conceded several points in the district court and in its motion
   241	to our court. For example, it concedes that the federal defendants have been moving ahead to
   242	implement the directives in the EO, including by "[e]stablishing infrastructure to" "compile,
   243	maintain, and transmit [the DHS] Citizenship Lists" and "taking steps to create a [USPS] portal"
   244	for collecting information about individuals who are eligible to vote by mail. The Government
   245	5
   246	
   247	The intervenor states largely ignore the summary judgment record and cite instead to
   248	allegations in the Plaintiff States' complaint in their stay motion.
   249	-6-
   250	
   251	also has conceded that it seeks to enforce the EO nationwide for the November 2026 election and
   252	would do so but for the district court's injunction. And the Government has indicated that it may
   253	use the DHS Citizenship Lists to conduct investigations and potential criminal prosecutions of
   254	state and local officials. As we will explain, these concessions undermine its arguments that the
   255	Plaintiff States lack standing to challenge the EO because their claims are premature.
