    94	likely to suffer irreparable harm without a stay. See Trump
    95	v. Cook, 609 U. S. ___, ___ (2026) (slip op., at 8). It must
    96	also demonstrate that the balance of equities does not coun-
    97	sel against relief. See ibid. Of these factors, the likelihood
    98	of success on the merits and the likelihood of irreparable
    99	harm “are the most critical.” Nken v. Holder, 556 U. S. 418,
   100	434 (2009).1
   101	                              II
   102	  As to the first factor: The Government is likely to prevail
   103	on the merits of its argument that the District Court lacked
   104	jurisdiction to enter the judgment.
   105	  Two related doctrines of justiciability block the States’
   106	suit. One is standing, which requires “an injury that is con-
   107	crete, particularized, and imminent rather than ‘conjec-
   108	tural or hypothetical.’ ” Carney v. Adams, 592 U. S. 53, 60
   109	(2020). The other is ripeness, which forbids federal courts
   110	to adjudicate disputes that are “dependent on ‘contingent
   111	future events that may not occur as anticipated, or indeed
   112	may not occur at all.’ ” Trump v. New York, 592 U. S. 125,
   113	131 (2020) (per curiam). Because “standing is not dis-
   114	pensed in gross,” we address each challenged provision of
   115	the Order separately. TransUnion LLC v. Ramirez, 594
   116	U. S. 413, 431 (2021).
   117	
   118	
   119	——————
   120	  1 The States do not dispute that this application presents a significant
   121	
   122	legal question. Trump v. Cook, 609 U. S., at ___ (slip op., at 8).
   123	4                   TRUMP v. CALIFORNIA
   124	
   125	                          Per Curiam
   245	ballot to a 17-year-old who is eligible to vote in a primary
   246	under state law. Not so. Section 7(b) of the Order requires
   247	§2(b) to “be implemented consistent with applicable law.”
   248	Id., at 17127. And on top of that, §2(b) limits itself to only
   249	“appropriate” prosecutions. Id., at 17126. Given these pro-
   250	visions, the Government has unsurprisingly disavowed any
   251	intention to prosecute state officials for lawfully providing
   252	ballots to 17-year-olds.
   253	                                 C
   254	   Last is the States’ challenge to §3, which directs the
   255	Postal Service to issue a notice of proposed rulemaking. Ac-
   256	cording to the States, this internal directive demands that
   257	they overhaul their existing election procedures. See Oppo-
   258	sition to Applications 24–26; see also post, at 1, 3, 7 (opinion
   259	of JACKSON, J.). It does not. Section 3 orders the Postal
   260	Service to initiate the process of proposing a rule that might
   261	bind the States in the future. It is not a proposed rule, let
   262	alone a final rule. And it imposes no legal requirements on
   263	the States (or, for that matter, on anyone outside the Exec-
   264	utive Branch).
   265	   The District Court’s conclusion that this internal di-
   266	rective inflicts an Article III injury on the States necessarily
   267	rests on an “attenuated chain of inferences.” Clapper, 568
   268	U. S., at 414–415, n. 5. At the time this suit was filed—the
   269	time when standing is measured—the District Court had to
   270	engage in a string of speculations to find this suit justicia-
   271	ble. It first had to speculate about whether the Postal Ser-
   272	vice would propose a rule. Then it had to speculate about
   273	how the agency would weigh the comments it received. Cf.
   274	Perez v. Mortgage Bankers Assn., 575 U. S. 92, 96 (2015)
   275	(emphasizing that agencies typically “must consider and re-
   276	spond to significant comments”). Then it had to speculate
   277	about whether the agency would issue a final rule. See
   278	Long Island Care at Home, Ltd. v. Coke, 551 U. S. 158, 175
   279	(2007) (explaining that a proposed rule is “simply a
   280	8                   TRUMP v. CALIFORNIA
   281	
   282	                          Per Curiam
   283	
   284	proposal,” and an agency “might choose to adopt the pro-
   285	posal or to withdraw it”). Then it had to speculate about
   286	what the final rule would say. Cf. Encino Motorcars, LLC
   287	v. Navarro, 579 U. S. 211, 218 (2016) (noting that the final
   288	rule “took the opposite position from the proposed rule”).
   289	Finally, after all that, the District Court still had to specu-
   290	late about whether the text of a hypothetical final rule
   291	would harm the States. It should go without saying that
   292	the District Court made a decision “dependent on ‘contin-
   293	gent future events that may not occur as anticipated, or in-
   294	deed may not occur at all.’ ” Trump v. New York, 592 U. S.,
   295	at 131.
   296	   The States cannot preemptively concretize their injury by
   297	expending funds before the Postal Service finalizes a rule.
   298	Spending money to hedge against a “speculative threat”
   299	does not constitute an Article III injury. Clapper, 568 U. S.,
   300	at 416. Though it may sometimes be “a reasonable reac-
   301	tion” to “incu[r] certain costs” in advance of a final rule,
   302	those costs cannot establish standing when the final rule
   303	remains a “hypothetical future harm that is not certainly
   304	impending.” Ibid. Federal courts review final rules, not
   305	proposed rules—and certainly not antecedent internal di-
   306	rectives to propose a rule. If the Postal Service’s final rule
   307	harms the States, they may challenge that rule.
   308	                             III
   309	  The remaining stay factors also favor relief. The Govern-
   310	ment is likely to suffer irreparable harm without a stay.
   311	The District Court’s breach of Article III interferes with the
   312	internal operations of the Executive Branch. See INS v.
   313	Legalization Assistance Project of Los Angeles County Fed-
   314	eration of Labor, 510 U. S. 1301, 1306 (1993) (O’Connor, J.,
   315	in chambers). The injunction bars Homeland Security from

