    89	(‘‘Executive Order’’ or ‘‘Order’’). The
    90	Executive Order stresses the federal
    91	government’s ‘‘unavoidable duty under
    92	Article II of the Constitution of the
    93	United States to enforce [f]ederal law,
    94	which includes preventing violations of
    95	[f]ederal criminal law and maintaining
    96	public confidence in election
    97	outcomes.’’ Id. To these ends, the Order
    98	points out the need for additional
    99	measures concerning the use of the mail
   100	in order to facilitate enforcement of
   101	federal law, reduce the risk of fraud, and
   102	help protect the integrity of federal
   103	elections. Id. Following the issuance of
   104	the Order, the Postal Service engaged in
   105	an interagency review process through
   106	the Office of Management and Budget
   107	(‘‘OMB’’), and subsequently issued a
   108	proposed rule regarding the preparation
   109	of ballot mail for federal elections to
   110	facilitate the enforcement of federal law
   111	and to implement best practices for
   112	Postal Service operations regarding
   113	ballot mail. Ballot Mail for Federal
   114	Elections, 91 FR 32915 (June 2, 2026).
   115	Although exempt by 39 U.S.C. 410(a)
   116	from the notice and comment
   117	requirements of the Administrative
   118	Procedure Act (‘‘APA’’) (5 U.S.C. 553)
   119	regarding proposed rulemaking, the
