src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-59-        <p class="tt-label">Three things to know</p>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-60-        <ol>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:61:          <li>On Sunday, September 6, Solicitor General D. John Sauer filed application 26A305. An application is a request to a justice to freeze a lower-court order while an appeal continues. This one asks the Supreme Court to stay, meaning pause, a September 4 injunction from U.S. District Judge Indira Talwani in Boston.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket" passage="No. 26A305" /></li>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-62-          <li>Talwani's injunction forbids the Postal Service from carrying out named mandatory sections of its Ballot Mail for Federal Elections rule, issued and effective August 21 and published August 26, for elections on or before November 3. It dissolves her August 27 temporary restraining order, a 14-day pause that the new order replaces.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="elections occurring before or on November 3" /><Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="Effective August 21, 2026" /></li>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:63:          <li>The same Sunday Sauer withdrew application 26A297, filed Thursday against that temporary order. The word "another" in the coverage is this third trip to the Court. Application 26A124, filed July 27, was granted August 24.<Cite s="scotus-26a297-withdrawal" passage="the United States withdraws its September 3 stay application" /><Cite s="scotus-26a124-docket" passage="Application (26A124) granted by the Court." /></li>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-64-        </ol>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-65-      </aside>
--
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-67-
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-68-      <div data-layer="proof" class="event-record-context">
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:69:        <p class="sources-line">This story rests on Executive Order 14399 as published in the Federal Register, the Postal Service's final rule (issued and effective August 21, published August 26), the Privacy Act notices for system USPS 820.225, Judge Talwani's September 4 injunction, the three Supreme Court applications and their dockets, Sauer's withdrawal letter, 2 U.S.C. 7, 52 U.S.C. 20302, Senator Blumenthal's September 1 whistleblower release, the Postal Service's September 1 portal statement, and coverage checked against those records. The days on this page are counted from those dates. The figures come from the official Federal Register PDFs.</p>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-70-      </div>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-71-
--
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-88-        <p data-layer="narrative">On August 27 Talwani entered a temporary restraining order against mandatory parts of the new rule. On September 3 Sauer asked the Court, in application 26A297, to stay that order. The docket shows the application was submitted to Justice Jackson. She set a response for September 8. The emergency docket is the Court's fast track for those requests, usually without a full argument.<Cite s="scotus-26a297-docket" passage="submitted to Justice Jackson" /></p>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-89-
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:90:        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 4 Talwani issued a preliminary injunction. A preliminary injunction is a court order that forbids someone from doing something while a lawsuit continues. This one names specific Domestic Mail Manual sections and applies to elections on or before November 3. She dissolved the August 27 temporary order the same day. These holdings are preliminary findings for that order, not a final judgment on the merits.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="The Temporary Restraining Order" /></p>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-91-
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-92-        <h3 id="why-blocked" data-layer="fact">Why the judge blocked it</h3>
--
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-117-          <figcaption><strong>The enjoined Domestic Mail Manual sections</strong><span>What each named section of the August 26 rule requires, in the rule's own terms.</span></figcaption>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-118-          <DataTable columns={["Section", "What it requires"]} rows={dmmRows} highlightColumn={0} />
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:119:          <p class="chart-source">The September 4 injunction names these sections and the first two sentences of 705.24.5.2. It does not enjoin 705.24.5.3(d). Separate from the injunction, section 705.24.5 "does not apply to Return Federal Ballot Mail."<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="DMM Section 705.24.5.3(a), (b), (c)" /><Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="does not apply to Return Federal Ballot Mail" /></p>
