src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-154-              <span class="catch-tag">Told versus record</span>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-155-              <span class="catch-lead">The block is of the rule, not the order.</span>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:156:              The Associated Press, PBS NewsHour, and CNBC wrote that Talwani extended an order against the Postal Service "enforcing President Donald Trump's executive order." CBS wrote "enforcing President Trump's executive order." The September 4 injunction names sections of the Postal Service rule issued August 21 and published August 26. The Court's August 24 stay had already paused her earlier injunction of the executive order itself. Why it matters: a reader of those dispatches can think the justices are being asked to revive the March order, when the fight before the Court is the Postal Service's published rule.<Cite s="ap-2026-09-06" passage="enforcing President Donald Trump" /><Cite s="cbs-2026-09-06" passage="enforcing President Trump's executive order" /><Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="Ballot Mail for Federal Elections" />
