src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-160-              <span class="catch-tag">Told versus record</span>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-161-              <span class="catch-lead">Citizenship lists are a different paper.</span>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:162:              The Hill wrote that Sauer asked the justices to "permit the administration to bar the U.S. Postal Service from sending mail-in ballots to residents of states that have not submitted a list of eligible voters based on citizenship data." The Sunday application asks the Court to stay the September 4 injunction of the Postal Service rule. The rule's portal fields are name, address, two barcodes, and originating state. The citizenship list sits in section 2 of the executive order, a Homeland Security task, not in the Postal Service rule's enrollment form. Why it matters: the injunction the Court is being asked to lift reaches envelope standards, portal registration and enrollment, outbound verification, and the nonacceptance clauses, not a citizenship roll, and it is a Postal Service rule, not an order that the administration bar the Postal Service.<Cite s="hill-2026-09-06" passage="permit the administration to bar the U.S. Postal Service" /><Cite s="eo-14399" passage="State Citizenship List" />
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-163-              <a class="catch-jump" href="#claim-check">Claim check</a>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-164-            </li>
--
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-172-              <span class="catch-tag">Who pays</span>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-173-              <span class="catch-lead">Mail voters in the plaintiff states.</span>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:174:              The September 4 order binds the Postal Service defendants for elections on or before November 3. It does not limit that command to the plaintiff states. Sauer told the Court North Carolina had already begun mailing, Alabama would on September 9, and at least five states the week of September 13. If the stay is granted, election offices sending non-UOCAVA outbound ballots would face the portal and envelope rules while some of those mailings are already moving. Why it matters: the next order lands on those offices and the voters waiting on outbound ballots, not only on voters in the states that sued, and not on UOCAVA ballots the rule excludes.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="Ballots have already begun to be mailed in North Carolina" /><Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="elections occurring before or on November 3" />
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-175-              <a class="catch-jump" href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-176-            </li>
