src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-197-        </figure>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-198-
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:199:        <p data-layer="narrative">The Sunday application's caption lists {computed.plaintiffStates} states, the District of Columbia, and Josh Shapiro, Governor of Pennsylvania, as plaintiffs. {computed.intervenorStates} states intervened on the government's side. Washington Attorney General Nick Brown's September 4 release said "25 states have secured a" preliminary injunction. That is the same coalition: Brown names 23 states, including Washington, plus the District of Columbia and Pennsylvania's governor. "25 states" is a loose label because the District of Columbia is not a state.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="Josh Shapiro, Governor of Pennsylvania" /><Cite s="brown-2026-09-04" passage="25 states have secured a" /></p>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-200-
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-201-        <p data-layer="narrative">August 24 was a win on procedure, not on the rule. The Court stayed an injunction of the executive order because, it said, the states had to speculate about a rule that had not issued. The Postal Service had already issued that rule and made it effective on August 21, {computed.daysEffectiveToStay} days before the opinion. The rule reached the Federal Register {computed.daysStayToRule} days after the opinion, on August 26. Talwani then enjoined named sections of that rule. Sunday's application argues that the new injunction is "materially identical" to the August 27 temporary order and that the Postal Service is regulating mail, not elections.<Cite s="scotus-26a124-opinion" passage="string of speculations" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="materially identical" /></p>
--
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-220-
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-221-        <ol class="dated-list" data-layer="fact">
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:222:          <li><strong>September 4:</strong> North Carolina, Sauer told the Court, had already begun mailing ballots. NPR, writing the day before, said the state had to start mailing absentee ballots on Friday.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="Ballots have already begun to be mailed in North Carolina" /><Cite s="npr-2026-09-03" passage="North Carolina has to start mailing out its absentee ballots on Friday" /></li>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-223-          <li><strong>September 9, 4 p.m. Eastern:</strong> Jackson's deadline for a response to 26A305. The same calendar day is the Alabama mailing date Sauer named.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket" passage="4 p.m. (EDT) on September 9, 2026." /></li>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-224-          <li><strong>Week of September 13:</strong> "at least five States," Sauer wrote, begin mailing.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="at least five States the week of September 13" /></li>
--
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-242-
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-243-        <div data-layer="fact">
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:244:          <OutletCheck outlet="The Hill" claim={`wrote on Sunday that Sauer asked the justices to &ldquo;permit the administration to bar the U.S. Postal Service from sending mail-in ballots to residents of states that have not submitted a list of eligible voters based on citizenship data.&rdquo;`} verdict="The Sunday application asks the Court to stay the September 4 injunction of the Postal Service rule. The portal fields in the rule are name, address, barcodes, and originating state. The citizenship list is section 2 of the executive order. The 40-page count and the withdrawal of Thursday's application check out. Talwani's order covers elections on or before November 3; The Hill also wrote that she halted the plan 'while lower courts decided on the policy,' which is how long the injunction lasts during the case, not a claim about later elections." chip="mislabeled" status="warn"><Cite s="hill-2026-09-06" passage="permit the administration to bar the U.S. Postal Service" /></OutletCheck>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:245:          <OutletCheck outlet="Associated Press" claim={`wrote that Talwani extended an order against the Postal Service &ldquo;enforcing President Donald Trump's executive order.&rdquo; PBS NewsHour and CNBC carried the same dispatch.`} verdict="The Sunday filing asks a stay of the September 4 injunction of the Postal Service rule. The August 24 Supreme Court stay had already paused the injunction of the executive order. The third-time count, the Sauer quotes, and the North Carolina mailing line check out against 26A305." chip="mislabeled" status="warn"><Cite s="ap-2026-09-06" passage="enforcing President Donald Trump" /><Cite s="pbs-2026-09-06" passage="enforcing President Donald Trump" /></OutletCheck>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:246:          <OutletCheck outlet="CBS News" claim={`wrote that Talwani extended an order against the Postal Service &ldquo;enforcing President Trump's executive order.&rdquo;`} verdict="Same mismatch as the Associated Press dispatch: the September 4 injunction names sections of the Postal Service rule, not the March executive order. CBS did not write 'Donald' in that line." chip="mislabeled" status="warn"><Cite s="cbs-2026-09-06" passage="enforcing President Trump's executive order" /></OutletCheck>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-247-          <OutletCheck outlet="NBC News" claim={`wrote that the rule would let the Postal Service &ldquo;potentially reject ballots that are sent to and from voters not included in a federal database.&rdquo;`} verdict="That wording checks out. USPS 820.225 is a federal Privacy Act system at Postal Service headquarters and a supplier cloud environment. It records names, addresses, issuing states, and barcodes, keeps them for five years, and states a law-enforcement purpose. State and local officials load the rows. It is not a citizenship file." chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="nbc-2026-09-06" passage="not included in a federal database" /><Cite s="usps-sorn-820-225" passage="Records are retained for 5 years." /></OutletCheck>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-248-          <OutletCheck outlet="NPR" claim={`wrote on September 3 that key parts of the USPS plan were blocked by a temporary restraining order, that voting by mail was still available to eligible voters, and that the August ruling was on a procedural question.`} verdict="Checks out against the August 27 order then in force, 26A124's ripeness holding, and the September 3 docket." chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="npr-2026-09-03" passage="currently blocked by a court order" /></OutletCheck>
--
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-257-        <div data-layer="fact">
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-258-          <CheckedBlock label="The coverage's claim: the administration is asking the Court to let it enforce the March executive order for the midterms" variant="contested">
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:259:            <p>Too broad. Application 26A305 asks a stay of the September 4 injunction of named sections of the Postal Service rule issued August 21 and published August 26. The March order's own injunction was stayed on August 24, and that opinion said the stay did not decide whether later implementing measures would be lawful. The injunction the Court is being asked to lift reaches mandatory outbound envelope standards and review; mandatory return-envelope standards and review; mandatory portal registration; mandatory voter enrollment; outbound verification; the first two sentences of the presentation rules; the nonacceptance, further-review, and no-responsibility clauses; and further preparatory steps to implement those sections.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="APPLICATION FOR A STAY OF THE INJUNCTION" /><Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="may not take any further preparatory steps to implement" /></p>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-260-          </CheckedBlock>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-261-        </div>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-262-
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:263:        <p data-layer="narrative">The claim readers brought is the one in the candidate headlines: a new block, and another appeal. Both are true as dates. The new block is the September 4 injunction. The earlier appeal that "another" points to is 26A297 on Thursday, and behind that 26A124 in July.<Cite s="scotus-26a297-docket" passage="Application (26A297) withdrawn." /></p>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-264-
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-265-        <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>how the day counts were made</summary>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro:266:          <p>November 3, 2026, minus September 6 is {computed.daysFilingToElection} days. Minus September 4 is {computed.daysPiToElection}. Minus September 3 is {computed.days297ToElection}. Minus August 26 (publication) is {computed.daysRuleToElection}. Minus August 21 (effective date, rule line 73) is {computed.daysEffectiveToElection}. Minus March 31 is {computed.daysEoToElection}. September 19 minus September 6 is {computed.daysFilingToUocava}. November 3 minus September 19 is {computed.uocavaDaysBeforeElection}, the statutory 45-day overseas and military ballot date when a valid request arrived in time; the rule excludes UOCAVA ballots. August 26 minus August 24 is {computed.daysStayToRule}. August 24 minus August 21 is {computed.daysEffectiveToStay}. Plaintiff states were counted from the parties list in 26A305: {computed.plaintiffStates} named states, plus the District of Columbia and the Pennsylvania governor, and {computed.intervenorStates} named intervenors.</p>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-267-        </details>
src/pages/events/mail-voting/september-2026.astro-268-      </section>

