   190	and about the chaotic design process that almost guarantees malfunctioning.
   191	On August 30, 2026, on the Whistleblower’s behalf, amicus transmitted a
   192	lawful Disclosure describing those systems to Senator Richard Blumenthal, the
   193	Ranking Member of the Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations. Senator
   194	Blumenthal forwarded the Disclosure to the Postmaster General the next day. The
   195	Disclosure reveals new facts, but it does not stand alone. Instead, it is amply
   196	corroborated by the public record, including recent reporting on the systems’ design
   197	and build process. 5
   198	Three relevant themes emerge from the Disclosure. First, even if the new
   199	systems work exactly as designed, they will deny ballots to eligible voters. The
   200	systems were designed with a pair of chokepoints: one that can block ballots from
   201	even being brought to USPS facilities, and another that can reject an entire mailing—
   202	with tens of thousands of ballots or even more—when a barcode on a single sampled
   203	envelope fails to properly scan. Second, the systems will almost certainly fail, because
   204	they are the product of a rushed, secretive, and chaotic development process. The
   205	systems are largely untested and, as of the hearing below, still inoperable. Third, the
   206	systems were built in defiance of court orders, and the Postal Service appears to have
   207	misled the courts and Congress about its compliance with those orders.
   208	I. Overview of the systems designed to implement the Order and the Rule.
