   218	with the Postal Service; and the Postal Service would “not transmit the ballot of any
   219	voter” not so enrolled. App. 2a–3a.
   220	The Rule purports to carry out that directive. Under the Rule, every federalelection ballot sent by mail must be enclosed in an envelope bearing a unique
   221	Intelligent Mail barcode (“IMb”) and the official Election Mail logo; state election
   222	officials must submit their envelope designs to the Postal Service for pre-approval;
   223	election officials must upload the name, address, and barcode of every voter receiving
   224	a mail ballot to a new Postal Service database, the Federal Ballot Mail Portal; and
   225	the Postal Service must verify, before accepting any outbound ballot mailing, that the
   226	barcodes on the envelopes match the data in the Portal. 91 Fed. Reg. 54990–91; App.
   227	7a–8a. If the barcodes on a mailing do not match, the Postal Service will not mail it.
   228	91 Fed. Reg. 54991; App. 8a.
   229	To implement that last requirement, the Postal Service undertook to roll out
   230	an entirely new set of information technology systems—the kind of massive project
   231	that ordinarily takes nine months to a year, or even longer, to design, build, test, and
   232	bring online. Disclosure ¶ 14. The Postal Service needed to build the Portal; it needed
   233	to establish connections between the Portal and existing bulk-mail systems, so that
   234	the manifest a local election official uploads for each batch of ballots can be verified
   235	against the Portal’s data before the official brings the batch to a post office; and it
   236	
